ace National Scenic Trail is five sections of hiking trail running roughly parallel to the 444-mile long Natchez Trace Parkway scenic motor road. The foot trails total more than 60 miles and offer opportunities to explore wetlands, swamps, hardwood forest, and the history of the area.",fullDescription:"Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is an official National Park Service site. The Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is five sections of hiking trail running roughly parallel to the 444-mile long Natchez Trace Parkway scenic motor road. The foot trails total more than 60 miles and offer opportunities to explore wetlands, swamps, hardwood forest, and the history of the area. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking the Blackland Prairie Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-6 Hours",description:"The Blackland Prairie section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is a popular 6 mile hiking trail in and around Tupelo, Mississippi."},{name:"Hiking the Yockanookany Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-15 Hours",description:"North of Jackson, Mississippi the Yockanookany section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is the longest of the five developed trail sections at 26 miles. Eight miles run alongside the Ross R. Barnett Reservoir, "},{name:"Hiking the Highland Rim Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-10 Hours",description:"The Highland Rim section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is a popular twenty mile hiking and equestrian corridor south of Nashville Tennessee along the Natchez Trace Parkway from Milepost 427.4 to 407.9."}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Natchez Trace Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(662) 680-4027"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is open every day during daylight hours. Severe weather resulting in washouts and downed trees can cause temporary trail closures.",address:"Alabama, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Natchez%20Trace%20National%20Scenic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/natt/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"trail-of-tears-national-historic-trail",name:"Trail Of Tears National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E670B-1DD8-B71B-0B25C5CD76BF4A04.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E6D6E-1DD8-B71B-0BF2FB679C481F1A.jpg"],shortDescription:"Remember and commemorate the survival of the Cherokee people, forcefully removed from their homelands in Georgia, Alabama, and Tennessee to live in Indian Territory, now Oklahoma. They traveled by foot, horse, wagon, or steamboat in 1838-1839.",fullDescription:"Trail Of Tears National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. Remember and commemorate the survival of the Cherokee people, forcefully removed from their homelands in Georgia, Alabama, and Tennessee to live in Indian Territory, now Oklahoma. They traveled by foot, horse, wagon, or steamboat in 1838-1839. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the Trail of Tears",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The trail commemorates the forced removal of Cherokee from their homelands; the paths that 17 Cherokee detachments followed westward; and the revival of the Cherokee Nation. We encourage you to retrace the trail on foot—"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Trail Of Tears Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Trail Of Tears National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alabama, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Trail%20Of%20Tears%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/trte/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["R