anceFees:"Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Denali%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/dena/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"gates-of-the-arctic-national-park",name:"Gates of the Arctic National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A8B6B-1DD8-B71B-0B8B89FE0C6B6F4F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A89F4-1DD8-B71B-0B52204A2EBF61A4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A8FC3-1DD8-B71B-0BB9EF068FF8EFF7.jpg"],shortDescription:"One of the last truly wild places on earth, with no roads or trails, lying entirely north of the Arctic Circle.",fullDescription:"Gates of the Arctic National Park and Preserve is a vast wilderness in northern Alaska, with no roads, trails, or visitor facilities. It preserves the wild and untouched beauty of the Brooks Range, offering unmatched opportunities for solitude, wilderness trekking, and viewing caribou migrations under the midnight sun.",thingsToDo:["Backpacking","River Rafting","Wildlife Viewing","Dog Sledding","Aurora Viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Arrigetch Peaks Route",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"Variable",description:"A demanding route over rugged, trail-less terrain to the spectacular granite spires of the Arrigetch Peaks."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Coldfoot Visitor Center",info:"Interagency visitor center providing arctic travel permits and safety briefings.",phone:"(907) 459-3730",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM Daily (Summer)"},rangerPrograms:["Summer wilderness orientation talks","Arctic environment ranger-led discussions"],campingInfo:"Wilderness camping only. No designated campgrounds. Backcountry registration is highly recommended.",wildlife:["Caribou","Grizzly Bears","Wolves","Dall Sheep"],bestViewpoints:["Arrigetch Peaks","Alatna River Valley"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Gates%20of%20the%20Arctic%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gaar/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"July to August",accessibilityInfo:"Extremely limited accessibility; this is a rugged wilderness area without developed facilities.",travelTips:["You must carry a bear-resistant food canister (BRFC).","Always file a backcountry trip plan before setting out."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"glacier-bay-national-park",name:"Glacier Bay National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C790BBF-1DD8-B71B-0B0AE92D0B9C24EB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C790BBF-1DD8-B71B-0B0AE92D0B9C24EB.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C790D9C-1DD8-B71B-0B4327321DED12AE.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79135D-1DD8-B71B-0BDF788A6D50B7A9.jpg"],shortDescription:"A land of dynamic glaciers, soaring temperate rainforest, wild coastlines, and deep sheltered fjords.",fullDescription:"Glacier Bay National Park and Preserve is a massive highlight of Alaska's Inside Passage. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site featuring deep-blue glaciers spilling into the ocean, towering snow-capped mountains, and abundant marine wildlife including humpback whales, harbor seals, and sea otters.",thingsToDo:["Boat Cruises","Sea Kayaking","Whale Watching","Hiking","Glacier Viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Forest Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 mile",description:"Scenic walk through a dense, mossy temperate rainforest near Bartlett Cove."},{name:"Bartlett River Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"Trail through lush forest to a tidal river estuary where wildlife is frequently spotted."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Glacier Bay Visitor Center",info:"Located on the second floor of the Glacier Bay Lodge, featuring exhibits, maps, and ranger-led programs.",phone:"(907) 697-2230",hours:"10:00 AM - 5:00 PM Daily"},rangerPrograms:["Daily interpretive programs on cruise ships","Evening lodge campfire talks"],campingInfo:"Bartlett Cove Campground is a free walk-in campground. Backcountry camping is popular.",wildlife:["Humpback Whales","Sea Otters","Harbor Seals","Bald Eagles","Brown Bears"],bestViewpoints:["Margerie Glacier","Bartlett Cove"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Glacier%20Bay%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/glba/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"June to August",accessibilityInfo:"Glacier Bay Lodge, several trails around Bartlett Cove, and standard tour boats are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Booking boat tours in advance is highly recommended.","Dress in warm layers, even during mid-summer."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"katmai-national-park",name:"Katmai National Park and Preserve",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9622f16328fcd7e1.webp",photoGallery:["https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9622f16328fcd7e1.webp","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A32DB-1DD8-B71B-0B37F66E9D0832AF.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A3473-1DD8-B71B-0B65158B5807CE0D.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A35BF-1DD8-B71B-0BB1644AA6CF4C8B.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A3776-1DD8-B71B-0BB94C2392CD5B01.jpg"],shortDescription:"Famous for brown bears fishing at Brooks Falls and the Valley of Ten Thousand Smokes.",fullDescription:"Katmai National Park and Preserve is located near King Salmon on the Alaska Peninsula. It is world-renowned for its dense population of Alaskan brown bears, which gather in large numbers at Brooks Falls to feed on migrating sockeye salmon. It also protects the dramatic volcanic landscape left by the 1912 eruption of Novarupta, creating the surreal Valley of Ten Thousand Smokes.",thingsToDo:["Bear Viewing","Sport Fishing","Volcanic Sightseeing","Hiking","Backcountry Trekking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Brooks Falls Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"Leading through boreal forest to the famous bear viewing platforms at Brooks Falls."},{name:"Valley of Ten Thousand Smokes Overlook",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Dramatic view of the volcanic ash-filled valley from the end of the Valley Road."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Brooks Camp Visitor Center",info:"Offers mandatory bear safety briefings, maps, and information on bear viewing platforms.",phone:"907-246-3305",hours:"8:00 AM - 5:00 PM Daily (Summer)"},rangerPrograms:["Mandatory bear safety orientation","Daily hikes to Brooks Falls with a ranger"],campingInfo:"Brooks Camp Campground is highly popular and requires early reservations on Recreation.gov.",wildlife:["Alaskan Brown Bears","Bald Eagles","Rainbow Trout","Moose","Wolves"],bestViewpoints:["Brooks Falls Platform","Dumpling Mountain Summit"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"near King Salmon, Alaska Peninsula, AK",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Katmai%20National%20Park%20and%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/katm/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"July (bear peak) and September",accessibilityInfo:"Brooks Camp is somewhat accessible; the boardwalks leading to Brooks Falls have wheelchair ramps, but trails are gravel/dirt.",travelTips:["Book your flights to King Salmon and floatplane to Brooks Camp months in advance.","Strict food storage regulations apply everywhere in the park."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"kenai-fjords-national-park",name:"Kenai Fjords National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6F4663BE-B09C-01AA-1ABBB8850868C2D0.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6F2AB13B-A9A4-ABE2-E74F8E73914A44B4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7135CE6E-FEBA-740C-B794409CA37C3741.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/707B3E46-E078-CB22-F6E71AECC2292518.jpg"],shortDescription:"Where mountains, ice, and ocean meet, featuring the massive Harding Icefield and breathtaking marine fjords.",fullDescription:"Kenai Fjords National Park, located near the town of Seward on the Kenai Peninsula, protects the spectacular Harding Icefield, from which numerous glaciers flow down into ocean fjords. The park offers incredible opportunities to witness calving glaciers, hike on glacier trails, and spot rich marine wildlife like puffins, sea lions, and killer whales.",thingsToDo:["Glacier Hiking","Boat Cruises","Sea Kayaking","Icefield Trekking","Wildlife Cruises"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Exit Glacier Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 mile",description:"Well-maintained paved and gravel trail leading near the active edge of Exit Glacier."},{name:"Harding Icefield Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"8.2 miles",description:"A challenging climb alongside Exit Glacier to breathtaking views looking out over the 700-square-mile icefield."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Kenai Fjords National Park Visitor Center",info:"Located in Seward Harbor, providing boat tour schedules, exhibits, and films.",phone:"(907) 318-2040",hours:"9:00 AM - 6:00 PM Daily (Summer)"},rangerPrograms:["Exit Glacier guided walks","Junior Ranger activity stations"],campingInfo:"Exit Glacier Campground is a 12-site walk-in campground (first-come, first-served).",wildlife:["Humpback Whales","Killer Whales (Orcas)","Steller Sea Lions","Puffins","Sea Otters","Mountain Goats"],bestViewpoints:["Exit Glacier","Harding Icefield Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kenai%20Fjords%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kefj/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"June to August",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center in Seward and the lower overlook trail at Exit Glacier are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["A boat tour is the only way to see the deep marine fjords and calving glaciers.","Prepare for rapid weather changes and rain on the trails."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"kobuk-valley-national-park",name:"Kobuk Valley National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A1214-1DD8-B71B-0B00D823BD9BF4CF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9B41FCED-9287-749E-01E2C1A2DFE31A1A.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A134A-1DD8-B71B-0B3C2789713B6113.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9B57B97E-9471-A430-A0233761F8133CE6.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9B843EAB-BE9D-AF8A-7D23CF259CD9FCB9.jpg"],shortDescription:"Features the massive, active Great Kobuk Sand Dunes rising in the middle of the arctic forest.",fullDescription:"Kobuk Valley National Park is a remote arctic wonderland in northwestern Alaska. It is famous for the Great Kobuk Sand Dunes—massive, active sand fields that rise unexpectedly out of the surrounding boreal forest. The park is also a critical crossing point for the ancient Western Arctic caribou herd, which migrates across the Kobuk River twice a year.",thingsToDo:["Sand Dune Hiking","Boating","Wildlife Spotting","Backcountry Trekking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Great Kobuk Dunes Trek",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"Variable",description:"Trail-less exploration across the towering active dunes rising out of the boreal woods."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Northwest Arctic Heritage Center",info:"Located in Kotzebue, offering interactive exhibits on the natural history and Inupiat culture of the arctic.",phone:"(907) 318-2230",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM Monday-Friday"},rangerPrograms:["Cultural heritage talks","Arctic ecosystem educational movies"],campingInfo:"Wilderness camping only. No established facilities.",wildlife:["Caribou","Grizzly Bears","Wolves","Moose","Sheefish"],bestViewpoints:["Great Kobuk Sand Dunes","Kobuk River Bluffs"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kobuk%20Valley%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kova/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"July to August",accessibilityInfo:"Extremely rugged and remote; there are no paved trails or facilities within the park limits.",travelTips:["Arrange air taxi transportation from Kotzebue or Bettles in advance.","Prepare for heavy mosquito swarms in early summer."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"lake-clark-national-park",name:"Lake Clark National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A9F9E-1DD8-B71B-0B6CEC8EF3F377DA.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AA462-1DD8-B71B-0BB13D97381C04B0.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AA140-1DD8-B71B-0BE3ECC0A8EE0DE5.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A9D02-1DD8-B71B-0B154E93FCC5D7F9.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C521C4DB-C6CB-EB6B-242225CB76BAB42E.jpg"],shortDescription:"A stunning, diverse wilderness with turquoise lakes, active volcanoes, and brown bear viewing.",fullDescription:"Lake Clark National Park and Preserve protects a pristine ecosystem where three mountain ranges meet. The park features the striking turquoise waters of Lake Clark, active volcanoes like Mount Redoubt, and dense salmon-filled rivers that draw coastal brown bears. Accessible only by plane, it is an unspoiled haven for adventure seekers.",thingsToDo:["Bear Viewing","Kayaking","Fishing","Hiking","Volcano Sightseeing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Tanalian Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"Leads through scenic birch forests to a magnificent roaring waterfall and volcanic canyon."},{name:"Kontrashibuna Lake Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.5 miles",description:"Trail continuing beyond the falls to a pristine alpine lake framed by steep mountain cliffs."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Port Alsworth Visitor Center",info:"Main park office in Port Alsworth, offering maps, safety information, and junior ranger booklets.",phone:"(907) 644-3626",hours:"8:00 AM - 5:00 PM Daily (Summer)"},rangerPrograms:["Bear safety briefings","Guided hikes around Port Alsworth"],campingInfo:"Backcountry camping is widely allowed. Silver Salmon Creek has private wilderness lodges.",wildlife:["Grizzly Bears","Moose","Sockeye Salmon","Dall Sheep","Bald Eagles"],bestViewpoints:["Lake Clark Shoreline","Tanalian Mountain Summit"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake%20Clark%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lacl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"June to September",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and a few nearby cabins are accessible, but the backcountry lacks any improvements.",travelTips:["Charter flights from Anchorage are the standard way to enter the park.","Carry bear spray and know how to use it safely."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"wrangell-st-elias-national-park",name:"Wrangell–St. Elias National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AAD63-1DD8-B71B-0BCE10FFD741A687.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AB124-1DD8-B71B-0B224A3B96A7304E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AB2FD-1DD8-B71B-0BC380F8D220B0BA.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AB5F0-1DD8-B71B-0B0A0B409DEAB3ED.jpg"],shortDescription:"The largest national park in the United States, an expansive landscape of massive glaciers, active volcanoes, and historic copper mines.",fullDescription:"Wrangell-St. Elias National Park and Preserve is the largest national park in the United States, covering over 13 million acres—the size of six Yellowstones combined. It features nine of the 16 highest peaks in the US, active volcanoes, massive tide-water glaciers, and the historic copper mining town of Kennecott.",thingsToDo:["Glacier Trekking","Historic Mine Tours","River Rafting","Scenic Flights","Backcountry Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Root Glacier Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"Trail starting from historic Kennecott, leading directly onto the surface of Root Glacier."},{name:"Erie Mine Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"8.0 miles",description:"Historic route tracking alongside the glacier, leading to a copper mine bunkhouse structure perched on a cliff."}],scenicDrives:["McCarthy Road (unpaved gravel, slow speed)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wrangell-St. Elias National Park Visitor Center",info:"Located in Copper Center, featuring an exhibits hall, theater, and bookstore.",phone:"907-822-5234"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Kennecott Mill tours","Guided walk on Root Glacier"],campingInfo:"Kendesnii Campground is a free first-come, first-served campground. Backcountry camping is popular.",wildlife:["Dall Sheep","Mountain Goats","Caribou","Grizzly Bears","Moose"],bestViewpoints:["Kennecott Mill Town","Root Glacier Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park and public lands are always open but visitor facilities have limited seasons: Open in Summer. Call ahead for hours: Copper Center (907) 822-7250, Slana (907) 822-7401, Chitina (907) 823-7250 and Kennecott (907) 205-7106.",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wrangell-St.%20Elias%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/wrst/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"June to mid-September",accessibilityInfo:"The main visitor center in Copper Center and the Kennecott Visitor Center are accessible.",travelTips:["Car rental companies often restrict driving on McCarthy Road due to gravel surface; check policy.","Bring warm clothing and traction devices for glacier walks."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"alagnak-wild-river",name:"Alagnak Wild River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E8391-1DD8-B71B-0BB3E18B7F96110A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E8391-1DD8-B71B-0BB3E18B7F96110A.jpg"],shortDescription:"Renowned for its world-class salmon fishing, the Alagnak River offers exceptional wilderness whitewater rafting and remote wildlife viewing in southwestern Alaska.",fullDescription:"The Alagnak Wild River flows from pristine lakes in Katmai National Park and Preserve into the Bristol Bay watershed. It is celebrated globally for its abundant populations of all five species of Pacific salmon, attracting sport fishers, rafters, and wildlife. This free-flowing river corridor provides critical habitats for brown bears, bald eagles, and moose, offering visitors a true Alaskan wilderness experience along its spectacular scenic waters.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Alagnak Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Alagnak Wild River, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 246-3305"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Alagnak%20Wild%20River",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/alag/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"alaska-public-lands",name:"Alaska Public Lands",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/40B47ACB-B7D6-AF9A-12AC3E00CB1263C4.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Your primary source for planning outdoor recreation across Alaska's public lands, offering trip guidance, exhibits, and educational resources.",fullDescription:"The Alaska Public Lands Information Centers provide a single point of contact for visitors seeking to explore the vast national parks, forests, and wildlife refuges across Alaska. With physical centers in Anchorage, Fairbanks, Ketchikan, and Tok, these facilities offer interactive exhibits, wildlife displays, personalized trip planning, and educational ranger programs. They are operated cooperatively by multiple state and federal land management agencies to help travelers navigate the wilderness safely and responsibly.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Alaska Public Lands Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Alaska Public Lands, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 644-3661"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open Daily from Sunrise to Sunset",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Alaska%20Public%20Lands",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/anch/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"aleutian-islands-world-war-ii-national-historic-area",name:"Aleutian Islands World War II National Historic Area",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7BC7CF-1DD8-B71B-0B73637962E06C74.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorates the forgotten military struggle and the forced evacuation of the Aleut people during the World War II Aleutian Campaign.",fullDescription:"The Aleutian Islands World War II National Historic Area, located on Amaknak Island, preserves the history of the military base at Fort Schwatka and the impact of the war on the region. This historic site honors the soldiers who served in the harsh subarctic climate and documents the tragic internment of the native Unangax (Aleut) people, who were forcibly evacuated to camps in southeastern Alaska during the conflict. Interactive exhibits at the museum bring this often-overlooked chapter of American history to life.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Aleutian Islands World War II Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Aleutian Islands World War II National Historic Area, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 581-9944"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Daily 8:00AM - 5:00PM (per nps.gov)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Aleutian%20Islands%20World%20War%20II%20National%20Historic%20Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/aleu/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"aniakchak-national-monument-preserve",name:"Aniakchak National Monument & Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A78AD-1DD8-B71B-0BF20886BAA0C0DA.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A7D1E-1DD8-B71B-0B03A4FBF4D86D31.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A7E85-1DD8-B71B-0BEDEFFC89815B42.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A7FDE-1DD8-B71B-0B357DE404A64A20.jpg"],shortDescription:"Centering on a massive, six-mile-wide volcanic caldera, Aniakchak is one of the most remote and wildest units in the national park system.",fullDescription:"Aniakchak National Monument and Preserve encompasses a vibrant volcanic landscape dominated by a spectacular 3,500-foot-deep caldera created during a massive eruption thousands of years ago. Located on the wild Alaska Peninsula, the park is famous for its extreme weather, rugged terrain, and the free-flowing Aniakchak River, which cuts through the caldera wall. Because of its remote setting and challenging weather, it receives very few visitors, offering an incredibly rugged and untouched wilderness experience for expert backpackers and rafters.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Aniakchak Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Aniakchak National Monument & Preserve, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 246-3305"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Aniakchak%20National%20Monument%20%26%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/ania/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"bering-land-bridge-national-preserve",name:"Bering Land Bridge National Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A859F-1DD8-B71B-0B24F438FBCB2BE9.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A8443-1DD8-B71B-0BDA7966D18282FC.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4E189372-A511-5F4D-17A56D6B9D2BD7E6.jpg"],shortDescription:"A remnant of the ancient land bridge that once connected Asia and North America, preserving prehistoric archaeology and volcanic hot springs.",fullDescription:"Bering Land Bridge National Preserve sits on the Seward Peninsula, protecting a vital portion of the historic land bridge that facilitated the migration of humans, plants, and animals between continents during the last Ice Age. Today, this vast tundra landscape features incredible geological formations like the Serpentine Hot Springs, massive volcanic maars, and a rich diversity of wildlife, including muskoxen, reindeer, and migratory birds. It also preserves ancient archaeological sites and continues to support the traditional subsistence lifestyles of local Inupiat communities.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bering Land Bridge Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Bering Land Bridge National Preserve, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 759-8020"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bering%20Land%20Bridge%20National%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/bela/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Preserve"},{id:"cape-krusenstern-national-monument",name:"Cape Krusenstern National Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/778e7bc7cfe7f5ce.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Protects a sequence of 114 coastal beach ridges that document 5,000 years of human adaptation and Arctic history.",fullDescription:"Cape Krusenstern National Monument is a vast, roadless Arctic coastal wilderness featuring a succession of beach ridges deposited by ocean currents over millennia. These ridges contain priceless archaeological records detailing thousands of years of Eskimo habitation, marine mammal hunting, and cultural evolution. Visitors who brave this remote landscape will find dynamic lagoon systems, wild coastal tundra, and a rich array of wildlife, including caribou, grizzly bears, and migrating waterfowl, alongside ongoing subsistence activities of the local Inupiat.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cape Krusenstern Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Cape Krusenstern National Monument, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 442-3890"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cape%20Krusenstern%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cakr/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"i-upiat-heritage-center",name:"Iñupiat Heritage Center",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8130DF-1DD8-B71B-0B99B1F06EB07E76.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in Utqiaġvik, this center honors the rich culture, history, and ancient whaling traditions of the Inupiat people of the Arctic.",fullDescription:"The Iñupiat Heritage Center in Utqiaġvik (formerly Barrow), Alaska, promotes and preserves the unique history, language, and culture of the Inupiat. It tells the fascinating story of traditional bowhead whaling, an essential practice that has sustained Arctic communities for thousands of years. The center features exhibit galleries, a library, and a large workshop space where local craftspeople and carvers create traditional tools, boats, and works of art, providing visitors a firsthand look at native Arctic traditions.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Iñupiat Heritage Center Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Iñupiat Heritage Center, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 434-0805"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The physical site is not managed by the NPS. Please call 907 852-0422 for hours of operation.",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=I%C3%B1upiat%20Heritage%20Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/inup/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"klondike-gold-rush-national-historical-park",name:"Klondike Gold Rush National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7BBBA0-1DD8-B71B-0BBE49A649E59052.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7BBBA0-1DD8-B71B-0BBE49A649E59052.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic trails, buildings, and stories of the famous 1897-1898 Klondike Gold Rush in the frontier town of Skagway.",fullDescription:"Klondike Gold Rush National Historical Park commemorates the thousands of hopeful stampeders who surged north to the Yukon gold fields in search of fortune. Based in Skagway, Alaska, the park preserves beautifully restored historic buildings from the boomtown era, the rugged Chilkoot Trail once climbed by gold seekers, and the scenic White Pass. Visitors can explore interactive exhibits, take ranger-led walking tours, and experience the raw excitement and extreme hardships of the turn-of-the-century gold rush.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Klondike Gold Rush Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Klondike Gold Rush National Historical Park, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 983-9200"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Klondike Gold Rush National Historical Park is open year-round, though access to historic buildings and most visitor services are provided only in summer months, May through late September. From October through late April, visitor services ",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Klondike%20Gold%20Rush%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/klgo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"noatak-national-preserve",name:"Noatak National Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C616CB02-D421-3562-CEDF608D9E401930.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A19A9-1DD8-B71B-0BEF579CA52B5653.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A1B02-1DD8-B71B-0B9517B26267D5D2.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C53C2650-AA06-F1FF-BAD7F1163BE9D1C3.jpeg"],shortDescription:"Encompasses the largest undisturbed mountain-ringed river basin in the United States, offering pristine wilderness and wild scenic river waters.",fullDescription:"Noatak National Preserve protects the extensive watershed of the Noatak River, which flows through a majestic arctic valley bordered by the Brooks Range. This vast wilderness remains completely untouched by modern development, serving as a vital scientific baseline for studying subarctic and arctic ecosystems. The preserve is a prime destination for remote river floating, fishing, and backpacking, where visitors can experience absolute solitude and spot caribou herds, grizzly bears, wolves, and a wide variety of birds migrating through the Arctic.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Noatak Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Noatak National Preserve, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 318-2230"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Noatak%20National%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/noat/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Preserve"},{id:"sitka-national-historical-park",name:"Sitka National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A8147-1DD8-B71B-0B9D9FA5E52A677B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A873D-1DD8-B71B-0B8AD5C703A13270.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A8147-1DD8-B71B-0B9D9FA5E52A677B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the 1804 battle between the Tlingit people and Russian traders, showcasing towering totem poles in a coastal rain forest.",fullDescription:"Sitka National Historical Park, Alaska's oldest federally designated park, preserves the battleground site where native Kiks.ádi Tlingit forces clashed with Russian traders and Aleut allies in 1804. Located in a lush temperate rainforest of spruce and hemlock, the park features a spectacular collection of historic totem poles lining scenic coastal walking trails. The visitor center includes cultural exhibits, a museum, and active carving studios where visitors can interact with Native Alaskan artists preserving ancient woodworking and weaving techniques.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Sitka Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Sitka National Historical Park, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 318-2170"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Park grounds are open for day-use year-round. Pedestrian through-traffic is allowed between Sawmill Creek Road and the visitor center parking lot (over the Indian River footbridge) at any time provided pedestrians do not loiter.",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sitka%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sitk/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"yukon-charley-rivers-national-preserve",name:"Yukon - Charley Rivers National Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5441BF50-05AD-F33E-6A0339476B5B18EF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/56A9969F-9BE7-F7A4-2DF42106328FEFBE.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/576A1897-A20F-B183-CE6A8AB15207E519.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/586B0BCE-D61C-2105-5C518EB2E5A138C0.jpg"],shortDescription:"Protects a massive segment of the mighty Yukon River and the entire basin of the pristine, wild Charley River in eastern Alaska.",fullDescription:"Yukon-Charley Rivers National Preserve offers a rugged frontier landscape encompassing 115 miles of the historic Yukon River and the entire, untouched watershed of the Charley River. This preserve is famous for its rich gold rush history, active paleontological sites, and incredible opportunities for river running and wilderness exploration. Visitors can see historic cabins from the trapping and prospecting eras, observe nesting peregrine falcons along towering cliffs, and experience a wild, roadless landscape where the spirit of the old Alaskan frontier lives on.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Yukon - Charley Rivers Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Yukon - Charley Rivers National Preserve, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(907) 459-3730"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alaska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Yukon%20-%20Charley%20Rivers%20National%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/yuch/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"}],"american samoa":[{id:"national-park-of-american-samoa",name:"National Park of American Samoa",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84F643-1DD8-B71B-0BC6F3EA2E1F58AB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84F643-1DD8-B71B-0BC6F3EA2E1F58AB.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84F785-1DD8-B71B-0B93723B92528909.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84F8DB-1DD8-B71B-0BD434E17818331B.jpg"],shortDescription:"The only American National Park south of the equator, protecting rugged volcanic peaks, lush tropical rainforests, and vibrant coral reefs. It offers a unique cultural experience in a remote South Pacific archipelago.",fullDescription:`Established in 1988, the National Park of American Samoa spans three islands—Tutuila, Ofu, and Ta‘ū—showcasing a distinct intersection of Polynesian culture and volcanic geological history. The park serves as a sanctuary for rare flora and fauna, including the endangered Samoan flying fox, within an ecosystem that has remained relatively undisturbed for millennia. Geologically, the islands are the tops of shield volcanoes that emerged from the Pacific floor, resulting in dramatic cliffs, jagged shorelines, and steep ridges blanketed in dense rainforest that transitions to complex coral reefs below the waterline.

The park is unique because it is managed through a long-term lease agreement with local village councils, honoring the fa'asamoa (the Samoan way of life). Visitors here are not just experiencing nature, but are guests in ancestral lands where land and sea are deeply intertwined with local tradition. The isolation of the archipelago has led to high levels of endemism, making it a critical research site for biologists studying island biogeography and tropical reef ecology in a protected marine environment.`,thingsToDo:["Snorkel the pristine coral reefs at Ofu Beach","Hike the ridge trails for panoramic views of the South Pacific","Learn about traditional Samoan culture through interaction with local village communities","Birdwatch for native species like the Fruit Dove and the Samoan Flying Fox","Explore the WWII history sites found along the Tutuila coastal areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mount Alava Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7 miles roundtrip",description:"A challenging hike that ascends through tropical rainforest to the summit of Mount Alava, providing unrivaled views of Pago Pago Harbor and the surrounding volcanic ridges."},{name:"Tuafanua Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles roundtrip",description:"This trail descends through lush jungle to a dramatic cliffside coastline where visitors can witness the raw power of the Pacific Ocean crashing against the volcanic rocks."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"National Park Visitor Center",info:"Located in the Pago Pago village, the visitor center serves as the main hub for park information, safety briefings, and cultural educational materials.",phone:"(684) 633-7085",hours:"Monday - Friday, 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Guided Rainforest Hikes","Cultural History Presentations","Junior Ranger Program"],campingInfo:"Camping is not permitted within the National Park of American Samoa. Visitors typically arrange homestays in local villages or stay in accommodations within the Pago Pago area.