"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"North Rim Drive, South Rim Drive, 9 overlooks open daily all year round. The Welcome Center is open daily from 8AM to 5PM and closed for Thanksgiving, Christmas, and New Years Day.",address:"Arizona, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Canyon%20de%20Chelly%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cach",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"casa-grande-ruins-national-monument",name:"Casa Grande Ruins National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D9776-1DD8-B71B-0B4D6AE7F16F082E.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D9904-1DD8-B71B-0B3C8ECB25C6B23E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D9C42-1DD8-B71B-0B8517554C3471EE.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D9E06-1DD8-B71B-0BB8059CC583426B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Explore the 'Great House,' a massive, four-story adobe structure built by the ancient Sonoran Desert people over 700 years ago.",fullDescription:"Casa Grande Ruins National Monument protects a remarkable collection of structures built by the Hohokam people (now known as the Ancient Sonoran Desert People) between 1200 and 1450 CE. The centerpiece is the 'Casa Grande' (Great House), a multi-story, earthen structure whose purpose remains debated but likely served as an astronomical observatory or a communal gathering space. This site also showcases an extensive network of irrigation canals, highlighting the advanced agricultural practices of this ancient desert civilization.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Casa Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(520) 723-3172"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The monument is open 7 days a week, with the exception of being closed Juneteenth, Independence Day, Thanksgiving, and Christmas. The gate closes 15 minutes prior to closing, with access for exiting the park still available on the outgoing ",address:"Arizona, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Casa%20Grande%20Ruins%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cagr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"chiricahua-national-monument",name:"Chiricahua National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/391E0C41-F660-DF55-E2CD9A1ACFBC63C2.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3B1F1A1C-99B2-E34B-7A367B1E6813F216.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3B451434-9A88-EB48-5CE248F3253C564F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3AA42DAB-A4B2-BF29-C512A967630CAFDD.jpg"],shortDescription:"Hike through a 'Wonderland of Rocks,' a unique landscape of balanced rocks and towering pinnacles formed by volcanic activity.",fullDescription:"Chiricahua National Monument preserves a majestic collection of rhyolite rock formations, shaped over millions of years by volcanic eruptions, erosion, and weathering. This 'sky island' ecosystem supports diverse plant and animal life, creating a biological crossroads where species from four distinct biogeographic regions converge. The monument also holds deep historical significance as a stronghold and spiritual home for the Chiricahua Apache, including leaders like Cochise and Geronimo, playing a pivotal role in the Apache Wars.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail to Natural Bridge",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"3-4 Hours",description:"This trail is a great opportunity to avoid the crowds in March and to see wildlife. It crosses through a stunning variety of ecosystems before ending at a bridge overlook. The small water-carved bridge is located across "},{name:"Hike the Trails at Chiricahua",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-9 Hours",description:"17 miles of trails will take you into a wilderness of rocks. Chiricahua offers a range of day hiking options from easy to strenuous."},{name:"Echo Canyon to Visitor Center",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"This moderate, 4.2 mile/6.8 kilometer trail begins at Echo Canyon trailhead and consists of Echo Canyon, Upper Rhyolite Canyon, and Lower Rhyolite Canyon trails ending at the Visitor Center. It goes downhill through spec"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Chiricahua Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(520) 824-3560"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees