restaurants, lodging, the Yosemite Museum and the Ansel Adams Gallery, with prints of the photographer’s renowned black-and-white landscapes of the area.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Rock Climbing","Scenic Driving","Photography","Star Gazing","Ranger Programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mist Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A steep trail passing Vernal Fall and Nevada Fall with spectacular mist spray."},{name:"Yosemite Falls Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.2 miles",description:"One of Yosemite's oldest trails leading to the top of North America's tallest waterfall."},{name:"Mariposa Grove of Giant Sequoias",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A gentle trail leading through hundreds of giant, ancient sequoia trees."}],scenicDrives:["Tioga Road","Glacier Point Road","Valley Loop Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Yosemite Valley Visitor Center",info:"Provides books, maps, orientation films, and expert ranger recommendations for exploring Yosemite Valley.",phone:"209-372-0200"},rangerPrograms:["Yosemite Valley Ranger Walks","Evening Campfire Talks","Junior Ranger Programs"],campingInfo:"13 campgrounds are available, with several requiring advanced reservations via Recreation.gov.",wildlife:["Black Bears","Mule Deer","Coyotes","Peregrine Falcons","Sierra Nevada Bighorn Sheep"],bestViewpoints:["Tunnel View","Glacier Point","Olmsted Point","El Capitan Meadow"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Yosemite Valley, CA 95389",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Yosemite%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/yose",bestTimeToVisit:"May to September (Spring/Summer for waterfalls)",accessibilityInfo:"Yosemite Valley Visitor Center, Valley Shuttle, and major viewpoints like Tunnel View and Glacier Point are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Reservations may be required to enter the park during peak seasons/weekends.","Store all food and scented items in bear-proof lockers.","Arrive early to secure parking in Yosemite Valley."],nearbyAttractions:["Mariposa Grove","Tuolumne Meadows","Hetch Hetchy Reservoir"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"sequoia-national-park",name:"Sequoia National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A301B-1DD8-B71B-0B8311F9B0AC4F69.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A2835-1DD8-B71B-0B468E834049FBE4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A301B-1DD8-B71B-0B8311F9B0AC4F69.jpg"],shortDescription:"Home to giant sequoia trees, including the General Sherman Tree, the largest tree on Earth by volume.",fullDescription:"Sequoia National Park is adjacent to Kings Canyon National Park in California's southern Sierra Nevada mountains. It's known for its giant sequoia trees, including the General Sherman Tree, the largest living tree on Earth. The park also features the underground Crystal Cave, Moro Rock, and Mount Whitney, the tallest peak in the contiguous United States.",thingsToDo:["Walking among giant sequoias","Hiking Moro Rock","Scenic Driving","Camping","Cave Tours"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Congress Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A paved loop trail winding through the heart of the giant sequoia forest near General Sherman."},{name:"Moro Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.6 miles",description:"A steep staircase hike with over 350 steps carved into stone, offering panoramic Sierra Nevada views."},{name:"Lakes Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"12.0 miles",description:"A challenging wilderness trail climbing to spectacular glacial lakes."}],scenicDrives:["Generals Highway","Kings Canyon Scenic Byway"],visitorCenter:{name:"Foothills Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor center on Generals Highway providing permits, maps, exhibits, and local weather updates.",phone:"559-565-3341",hours:"9:00 AM - 4:30 PM Daily"},rangerPrograms:["Giant Forest Walks","Campfire Programs","Astrophysics & Star Gazing Talks"],campingInfo:"Campgrounds like Lodgepole and Dorst Creek offer rustic sites. Reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Black Bears","Mule Deer","Mountain Lions","Golden Eagles"],bestViewpoints:["Moro Rock","Beetle Rock","Tunnel Log","Halstead Meadow"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"47050 Generals Hwy, Three Rivers, CA 93271",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sequoia%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/seki",bestTimeToVisit:"June to September (warm weather, fully open roads)",accessibilityInfo:"The Giant Forest Museum and several trails, including the Big Trees Trail and the General Sherman Tree trail, are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Generals Highway has sharp turns and length restrictions for large vehicles.","Always carry tire chains in the winter.","Cell service is very limited inside the forest."],nearbyAttractions:["Kings Canyon National Park","Giant Forest Museum","Hume Lake"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"kings-canyon-national-park",name:"Kings Canyon National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A301B-1DD8-B71B-0B8311F9B0AC4F69.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A2835-1DD8-B71B-0B468E834049FBE4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A301B-1DD8-B71B-0B8311F9B0AC4F69.jpg"],shortDescription:"Home to some of the world's largest giant sequoias and the deepest canyon in North America, this park features dramatic glaciated valleys and towering Sierra peaks.",fullDescription:"Kings Canyon National Park protects a dramatic landscape of massive sequoia groves, including the General Grant Tree, known as the Nation's Christmas Tree. The park features the deep, glacier-carved Kings Canyon, flanked by towering granite cliffs and the roaring Kings River. Backcountry trails lead to the high-elevation wilderness of the High Sierra, making it a premier haven for backpackers and climbers.",thingsToDo:["Scenic Driving","Hiking","Backpacking","Wildlife Watching","Visiting General Grant Tree"],hikingTrails:[{name:"General Grant Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A gentle, paved loop highlighting the historic General Grant tree and the Fallen Monarch."},{name:"Zumwalt Meadow Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic boardwalk trail offering views of granite walls, lush meadow, and the Kings River."},{name:"Mist Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"8.0 miles",description:"A popular trail following the Kings River up to one of the park's largest waterfalls."}],scenicDrives:["Kings Canyon Scenic Byway"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kings Canyon Visitor Center (Grant Grove)",info:"Offers interactive exhibits on sequoias, wilderness trip planning, books, and topographic maps.",phone:"(559) 565-3341"},rangerPrograms:["Grant Grove Historic Walk","Wilderness Orientation Talks"],campingInfo:"Several campgrounds in Grant Grove and Cedar Grove offer a total of over 300 campsites.",wildlife:["Black Bears","Mule Deer","Bighorn Sheep","Yellow-bellied Marmots"],bestViewpoints:["Panoramic Point","Junction View","Roaring River Falls"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Grant Grove Village, CA 93633",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kings%20Canyon%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/seki",bestTimeToVisit:"Late Spring to Early Autumn",accessibilityInfo:"The General Grant Loop is paved and wheelchair accessible. Accessible restrooms are available at the visitor center.",travelTips:["The scenic byway into the canyon is closed during the winter season.","Cedar Grove is highly remote; fill up on gas before heading down."],nearbyAttractions:["Sequoia National Park","Boyden Cavern","Hume Lake"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"joshua-tree-national-park",name:"Joshua Tree National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85EF02-1DD8-B71B-0B467F2B5F7483AC.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85EF02-1DD8-B71B-0B467F2B5F7483AC.jpg"],shortDescription:"Where the high Mojave Desert meets the low Colorado Desert, this park is defined by its iconic, twisty Joshua trees and massive, surreal geologic rock formations.",fullDescription:"Joshua Tree National Park preserves a unique desert ecosystem shaped by strong winds, sudden torrents of rain, and extreme temperatures. Climbers from around the world flock to its famous monzogranite boulder piles, while hikers explore palm oases hidden within rugged canyons. The park's exceptionally dark night skies offer spectacular stargazing, making it a prime destination for astronomy enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Stargazing","Rock Climbing","Hiking","Photography","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hidden Valley Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 miles",description:"A scenic loop trail enclosed by towering boulder walls, perfect for families."},{name:"Ryan Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A steep climb to the summit of Ryan Mountain offering panoramic desert views."},{name:"Barker Dam Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.1 miles",description:"A historic loop trail leading to a small reservoir built by early cattlemen."}],scenicDrives:["Park Boulevard Scenic Drive","Geology Tour Road"],visitorCenter:{name:"Joshua Tree Visitor Center",info:"Located in the town of Joshua Tree, providing maps, bookstore items, park passes, and local advice.",hours:"8:00 AM - 5:00 PM Daily",phone:"(760) 367-5500"},rangerPrograms:["Desert Ecology Walks","Night Sky Programs","Keys Ranch Guided Tours"],campingInfo:"9 campgrounds exist in the park, with some accepting reservations and others being first-come, first-served.",wildlife:["Coyotes","Roadrunners","Desert Bighorn Sheep","Desert Tortoises","Gambel's Quail"],bestViewpoints:["Keys View","Cholla Cactus Garden","Skull Rock","Arch Rock"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"74485 National Park Drive, Twentynine Palms, CA 92277",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Joshua%20Tree%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/jotr",bestTimeToVisit:"October to May (pleasant spring/autumn temperatures)",accessibilityInfo:"Keys View overlook is fully wheelchair accessible. Accessible restrooms and exhibit areas are available at all visitor centers.",travelTips:["There is no cell reception or drinking water inside the park. Bring at least one gallon of water per person per day.","Beware of sudden desert temperature drops after sunset.","Keep distance from desert wildlife."],nearbyAttractions:["Pioneertown","Salton Sea","Palm Springs"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"lassen-volcanic-national-park",name:"Lassen Volcanic National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C873811-1DD8-B71B-0B9C62ED8E12E7B5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8739BB-1DD8-B71B-0B8336A925B1A6C7.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C873E3A-1DD8-B71B-0BBD47F2E441D2F7.jpg"],shortDescription:"This active volcanic landscape features steaming sulfur vents, boiling mud pots, and all four types of volcanoes found in the world.",fullDescription:"Lassen Volcanic National Park is dominated by Lassen Peak, one of the largest plug dome volcanoes on Earth, which last erupted violently in 1915. Visitors can explore hydrothermal areas like Bumpass Hell, where boiling springs and mud pots hiss and bubble. The park also showcases pristine mountain lakes, fields of cinder cones, and dense evergreen forests that recover dynamically from volcanic activity.",thingsToDo:["Exploring Geothermal Areas","Hiking Lassen Peak","Stargazing","Kayaking","Scenic Road Trips"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bumpass Hell Trail",difficulty:"Easy-Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A boardwalk trail through the park's largest active hydrothermal area with boiling springs and steam vents."},{name:"Lassen Peak Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"5.0 miles",description:"A steep, challenging climb to the summit of Lassen Peak at 10,457 feet."},{name:"Manzanita Lake Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A flat, relaxing loop trail offering excellent photography reflections of Lassen Peak."}],scenicDrives:["Lassen Volcanic National Scenic Byway"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kohm Yah-mah-nee Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor center at the southwest entrance, featuring an educational film, exhibits, and dining.",phone:"(530) 595-6100"},rangerPrograms:["Geology Talks at Bumpass Hell","Astronomy Night Programs"],campingInfo:"7 campgrounds are available, including Manzanita Lake Campground which offers cabins and tent sites.",wildlife:["Black Bears","Mule Deer","Steller's Jays","Chipmunks","Mountain Chickadees"],bestViewpoints:["Lassen Peak Overlook","Devastated Area","Bumpass Hell Overlook"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Private Vehicle $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"38050 Highway 36 East, Mineral, CA 96063",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lassen%20Volcanic%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lavo",bestTimeToVisit:"July to September (snow fully melted)",accessibilityInfo:"Kohm Yah-mah-nee Visitor Center and the Manzanita Lake Museum are wheelchair accessible. Accessible boardwalks are built at Bumpass Hell.",travelTips:["Lassen Park Highway gets heavily snowed in and is usually closed to vehicles from November to late June.","Do not step off designated trails and boardwalks in hydrothermal areas."],nearbyAttractions:["Subway Cave Lava Tube","Burney Falls","Lake Almanor"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"pinnacles-national-park",name:"Pinnacles National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9CCA8ED3-ABB9-CB69-EF4B32F2ADACEC47.jpeg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9CCA8ED3-ABB9-CB69-EF4B32F2ADACEC47.jpeg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/38194093-C21D-59D4-B979ECD053B96101.jpg"],shortDescription:"Formed by ancient volcanic eruptions, this park features massive monoliths, talus caves, and serves as a critical release site for the endangered California condor.",fullDescription:"Pinnacles National Park protects a striking chaparral landscape defined by towering volcanic rock spires, popular among rock climbers. Hikers can journey through dark talus caves formed by boulders wedged into narrow canyons, emerging to oak woodlands and grassy slopes. As a key recovery zone, the park offers visitors a rare opportunity to spot the majestic, soaring California condor.",thingsToDo:["Condor Watching","Cave Exploration","Rock Climbing","Hiking","Wildflower Viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bear Gulch Cave Trail",difficulty:"Easy-Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"Leads through a dramatic talus cave (requires flashlights) up to the reservoir."},{name:"High Peaks Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"5.3 miles",description:"A steep climb into the heart of the volcanic rock spires with handrails carved into sheer cliffs."},{name:"Condor Gulch Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail providing excellent vantage points for condor watching."}],scenicDrives:["East Entrance Scenic Road"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pinnacles Visitor Center (East Side)",info:"Offers books, exhibits, ranger advice, camping check-in, and condor tracking updates.",hours:"9:00 AM - 4:00 PM Daily",phone:"(831) 389-4486"},rangerPrograms:["Condor Recovery Talks","Talus Cave Guided Hikes","Evening Campfire Programs"],campingInfo:"Pinnacles Campground (East Side) offers campsites and a swimming pool. No vehicle access between east and west sides.",wildlife:["California Condors","Turkey Vultures","Coyote","Bobcats","Townsend's Big-eared Bats"],bestViewpoints:["High Peaks","Condor Gulch Overlook","Pinnacles Reservoir"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"5000 Highway 146, Paicines, CA 95043",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pinnacles%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/pinn",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring (for wildflowers and green hills) or Autumn",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center is accessible. Accessible trails include the Bench Trail (0.5 miles) on the East Side.",travelTips:["The park has two separate entrances (East and West) that do not connect by road inside the park.","Carry flashlights for talus cave trails. Caves may close due to bat nesting."],nearbyAttractions:["Monterey Bay","Salinas Valley Wine Country","San Juan Bautista Mission"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"redwood-national-and-state-parks",name:"Redwood National and State Parks",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/CD69DD56-E050-4F4E-DDF622317D38250E.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to the tallest trees on Earth, this park complex protects nearly half of the world's remaining old-growth coast redwood canopy.",fullDescription:"Managed cooperatively by the NPS and California State Parks, Redwood National and State Parks preserve magnificent forests of coast redwoods, some of which tower over 350 feet and live for up to 2,000 years. The parks also encompass dramatic Pacific coastlines, wild rivers, and oak woodlands. Visitors can hike through cathedral-like groves, spot Roosevelt elk in lush prairies, or explore tide pools along the rugged beaches.",thingsToDo:["Walking among giant redwoods","Scenic coastal drives","Elk watching","Beach exploration","Tidepooling"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Tall Trees Grove Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"A steep descent into a remote redwood grove along Redwood Creek (permit required)."},{name:"Lady Bird Johnson Grove Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle, level walk through a scenic upland grove of old-growth redwoods."},{name:"Fern Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 miles",description:"A stunning walk through a narrow canyon with walls draped entirely in lush ferns, featured in Jurassic Park."}],scenicDrives:["Newton B. Drury Scenic Parkway","Howland Hill Road","Coastal Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kuchel Visitor Center",info:"The largest visitor center in the park, situated directly on the beach, offering exhibits, films, and books.",phone:"(707) 464-6101"},rangerPrograms:["Fern Canyon Walks","Campfire Programs at Elk Prairie","Tidepool Explorations"],campingInfo:"Four developed campgrounds are situated in the state park sections. Reservations are required.",wildlife:["Roosevelt Elk","Black Bears","Gray Whales (coastal views)","Bald Eagles","Steller's Jays"],bestViewpoints:["Redwood Creek Overlook","High Bluff Overlook","Klamath River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"1111 Second Street, Crescent City, CA 95531",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Redwood%20National%20and%20State%20Parks",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/redw",bestTimeToVisit:"May to September (driest and mildest months)",accessibilityInfo:"Kuchel Visitor Center, Crescent Beach Overlook, and several flat trails like Lady Bird Johnson Grove have wheelchair accessible paths.",travelTips:["Howland Hill Road is a dirt road but highly scenic; RVs are not recommended.","Wear sturdy waterproof shoes for Fern Canyon.","Advance permits are required for Tall Trees Grove and Fern Canyon in summer."],nearbyAttractions:["Trees of Mystery","Trinidad State Beach","Crescent City Lighthouse"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"channel-islands-national-park",name:"Channel Islands National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8DE774D1-CB22-37F2-D826E3F0A73D303A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8E0CA65D-0CF8-87C6-DE4B4AA7AAC02DEA.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8DF30150-D91A-D761-4A5064BA77154E2C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8E1AE30B-DBDB-C193-C1023F2C7591ABA0.jpg"],shortDescription:"Encompassing five remarkable islands off the Southern California coast, this park isolates unique endemic species, earning it the title the Galapagos of North America.",fullDescription:"Channel Islands National Park protects a rich marine and terrestrial ecosystem that has evolved in isolation for thousands of years, giving rise to unique species like the island scrub-jay and island fox. The surrounding waters are rich with kelp forests, sea lions, and migrating whales, making it a world-class destination for sea kayaking, snorkeling, and diving. Visitors can access the rugged, undeveloped islands via boat or plane to experience pristine coastal wilderness.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Channel Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(805) 658-5730"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"California, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Channel%20Islands%20National%20Park",offici