857, businessman and transportation entrepreneur John Butterfield was awarded a contract to establish an overland mail route between the eastern United States and growing populations in the Far West. What became known as the Butterfield Overland Trail made an arcing sweep across the southern rim of the country. Stagecoaches left twice a week carrying passengers, freight, and mail. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Butterfield Overland Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"California, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Butterfield%20Overland%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/buov/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"cabrillo-national-monument",name:"Cabrillo National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83C0FF-1DD8-B71B-0B85BBD2186E9F44.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83C0FF-1DD8-B71B-0B85BBD2186E9F44.jpg"],shortDescription:"In 1542, Juan Rodriguez Cabrillo climbed out of his boat and onto shore, becoming the first European to set foot on what is now the West Coast of the United States. Cabrillo National Monument not only tells the story of this 16th-century exploration but also houses a wealth of cultural and natural resources. Join us and embark on your own Voyage of Exploration.",fullDescription:"Cabrillo National Monument is an official National Park Service site. In 1542, Juan Rodriguez Cabrillo climbed out of his boat and onto shore, becoming the first European to set foot on what is now the West Coast of the United States. Cabrillo National Monument not only tells the story of this 16th-century exploration but also houses a wealth of cultural and natural resources. Join us and embark on your own Voyage of Exploration. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Oceanside Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"45-60 Minutes",description:"The Oceanside Trail significantly increases recreational opportunities by providing an entirely new trail that increases the park’s trail network by over 20% while also offering a car-free mode of access to the tidepool "},{name:"Bayside Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Learn about what you can see and do while hiking the Bayside Trail at Cabrillo National Monument."},{name:"Coastal Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Learn about hiking the Coastal Trail at Cabrillo National Monument!"}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],v