e Plains High School he attended, and the historic train depot that served as his 1976 presidential campaign headquarters. The site beautifully illustrates the journey of a peanut farmer from rural Georgia who rose to the nation's highest office, reflecting his character, values, and dedication to public service. It provides a unique lens into 20th-century American history through the lens of one of its most distinguished figures.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Jimmy Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(229) 824-4104"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Plains Depot was chosen for President Carter's 1976 Presidential Campaign Headquarters because it was vacant and it had a public restroom. Today the building is a museum for Jimmy Carter's campaign for Georgia State Senate, Governor of Geor",address:"Georgia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Jimmy%20Carter%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/jica/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"martin-luther-king-jr-national-historical-park",name:"Martin Luther King Jr. National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/martin-luther-king-jr-national-historical-park-georgia.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in Atlanta, this park preserves the birthplace, church, and final resting place of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., offering a profound journey through the life and legacy of the Civil Rights Movement's most iconic leader. It is a powerful testament to the struggle for equality and justice.",fullDescription:"The park includes Dr. King's birth home on Auburn Avenue, Ebenezer Baptist Church where he and his father served as pastors, and The King Center, where he and Coretta Scott King are interred. Visitors can tour these hallowed sites to gain a deeper understanding of the early influences on King's life, his transformative work as a civil rights activist, and the enduring impact of his nonviolent philosophy on America and the world. It serves as an essential destination for learning about the Civil Rights Movement and its ongoing relevance.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Martin Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(404) 331-5190"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:`Access to buildings at the Martin Luther King, Jr. National Historical Park is as follows:

