June 1864 as Major General William T. Sherman's Union army advanced on Atlanta. Despite Union attempts to break Confederate lines, the battle resulted in heavy casualties and demonstrated the formidable defensive tactics employed. Visitors can hike miles of trails that trace the lines of battle, explore well-preserved trenches and cannon emplacements, and enjoy interpretive programs that detail the strategic importance and human cost of this pivotal campaign. The park offers both historical immersion and scenic natural beauty.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Kennesaw Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kennesaw Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(770) 427-4686"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $5; Per Person $5 (per nps.gov)",hours:`Kennesaw Mountain NBP is open from 6:30 AM - 8:30 PM during Daylight Saving Time (March - November) and 6:30 AM - 6:30 PM Standard Time (December - February) 

