FF66F6F15B6EEB9.jpg"],shortDescription:"This urban oasis preserves a 48-mile stretch of the Chattahoochee River and its surrounding lands, offering Atlanta residents and visitors diverse recreational opportunities like fishing, paddling, and hiking. It provides crucial green space and water resources.",fullDescription:"The Chattahoochee River National Recreation Area consists of 15 land units stretching from north of Atlanta down to Lake Lanier, providing a vital natural escape within a bustling metropolitan area. The river itself supports a thriving trout fishery and is popular for kayaking, canoeing, and tubing, offering a serene contrast to urban life. Miles of hiking trails wind through forests and along the riverbanks, providing opportunities for wildlife viewing and peaceful contemplation. This park serves as a critical resource for outdoor recreation, environmental education, and protecting the ecological health of the Chattahoochee River corridor.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Exploring Gold Branch",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1-6 Hours",description:"Escape the crowds with a short drive to Gold Branch in east Cobb County. Hike down to Bull Sluice Lake to wet a line or have a picnic in the woods. If you wish, take a nice long hike to really get away from the crowds."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Chattahoochee Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(770) 399-8070"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $5; Per Person $5 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The park lands and waters are open for visitation from 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset seven days a week. The Island Ford Visitor Center located in historic Hewlett Lodge is open from 9 am to 5 pm seven days a week exce",address:"Georgia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chattahoochee%20River%20National%20Recreation%20Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/chat/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"cumberland-island-national-seashore",name:"Cumberland Island National Seashore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C803FAD-1DD8-B71B-0BF8968CDECF2468.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C804721-1DD8-B71B-0B674DD331D1FC2B.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C805318-1DD8-B71B-0BEE73A8A7441F08.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C805E82-1DD8-B71B-0B894349D0BBFF6B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Cumberland Island National Seashore is Georgia's largest and southernmost barrier island, offering pristine undeveloped beaches, wild horses, historic ruins, and lush maritime forests, accessible only by ferry. It's a wilderness retreat of exceptional natural beauty.",fullDescription:"This remarkable island is a testament to natural preservation, featuring 18 miles of untouched shoreline, ancient live oaks draped in Spanish moss, and a population of feral horses that roam freely. Visitors can explore the Dungeness Ruins, the remnants of a grand Carnegie estate, or discover the historic Plum Orchard mansion. With limited development and visitor access via ferry, Cumberland Island offers a rare opportunity for solitude, camping, hiking, and wildlife observation in a dynamic coastal ecosystem, truly showcasing the raw beauty of Georgia's barrier islands.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Southend Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"3-5 Hours",description:"Take a 4.5 miles hike through the forest, over the marsh and along the beach. Take some time to explore the Dungeness historic district, wildlife and history is everywhere! This Is a great 3-4 hour day hike for first tim"},{name:"Hike to Little Greyfield",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"Take a secluded hike through the maritime forest and along the beach. This hike will take you through saw palmetto, wax myrtle and under the canopy of sprawling live oak trees. The trail will intersect with the Little Gr"},{name:"Hike to Sea Camp Beach",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"20-30 Minutes",description:"With 17 miles of undeveloped beach, it is easy to find your own personal space on Cumberland’s beach. The wide-open space of the beach provides a striking contrast to the dense, tangled maritime forest. When you step out"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cumberland Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(912) 882-4336"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The park is open 24 hours a day, all year. The island is only accessible by boat.",address:"Georgia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cumberland%20Island%20National%20Seashore",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cuis/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",ac