reek Battlefield preserves the site of the Battle of Kettle Creek, fought on February 14, 1779, in Wilkes County, Georgia. This crucial clash was one of the most important Revolutionary War battles fought in the southern theater, resulting in a surprising and decisive victory for the patriot forces over a larger loyalist militia. The victory disrupted British plans to recruit loyalists, boosted patriot morale in the South, and prevented the British from establishing firm control over the Georgia backcountry. Today, visitors can walk the trails, view monuments, and explore the historic hillsides.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Kettle Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(706) 678-5553"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Georgia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kettle%20Creek%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/keco/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"trail-of-tears-national-historic-trail",name:"Trail Of Tears National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E670B-1DD8-B71B-0B25C5CD76BF4A04.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E6D6E-1DD8-B71B-0BF2FB679C481F1A.jpg"],shortDescription:"Remember and commemorate the survival of the Cherokee people, forcefully removed from their homelands in Georgia, Alabama, and Tennessee to live in Indian Territory, now Oklahoma. They traveled by foot, horse, wagon, or steamboat in 1838-1839.",fullDescription:"Trail Of Tears National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. Remember and commemorate the survival of the Cherokee people, forcefully removed from their homelands in Georgia, Alabama, and Tennessee to live in Indian Territory, now Oklahoma. They traveled by foot, horse, wagon, or steamboat in 1838-1839. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the Trail of Tears",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The trail commemorates the forced removal of Cherokee from their homelands; the paths that 17 Cherokee detachments followed westward; and the revival of the Cherokee Nation. We encourage you to retrace the trail on foot—"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Trail Of Tears Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Trail Of Tears National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Georgia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Trail%20Of%20Tears%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/trte/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],guam:[{id:"war-in-the-pacific-national-historical-park",name:"War in the Pacific National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84E1DA-1DD8-B71B-0B51C510B3B8336F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84E1DA-1DD8-B71B-0B51C510B3B8336F.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorating the bravery and sacrifices of those who fought and suffered during the Pacific Theater of World War II on Guam. This park preserves the diverse landscapes of beaches, cliffs, and ridges that served as a backdrop for the 1944 liberation.",fullDescription:`War in the Pacific National Historical Park preserves the significant sites associated with the 1944 Battle for the liberation of Guam. The park encompasses seven distinct sites scattered across the island, ranging from the landing beaches at Asan and Agat to the rugged inland ridges and caves where intense combat occurred. Visitors can explore relics of war, including pillboxes, gun emplacements, and trenches, set against the backdrop of Guam's stunning tropical flora and coral reefs. The park serves as a solemn memorial to the experiences of the Chamorro people during the occupation and the American forces who liberated the island.

Geologically, the park highlights Guam's unique limestone plateaus and volcanic ridges which proved pivotal to military strategies during the invasion. The marine components of the park protect vital reef ecosystems, offering a poignant contrast between the destruction of war and the resilience of the natural environment. Interpretation throughout the park bridges the history of the occupation with the natural biodiversity of the Western Pacific, providing a multidimensional experience for history enthusiasts, hikers, and marine life observers alike.`,thingsToDo:["Snorkel the pristine waters of the Agat Unit to observe marine life and underwater remnants of the conflict","Hike the Asan Ridge trail to witness commanding panoramic views of the Asan Beach landing site","Attend a ranger-led interpretive talk at the T. Stell Newman Visitor Center to learn about the Chamorro experience","Explore the historical gun emplacements and coastal defense batteries at Piti Guns","Picnic at Asan Beach while reflecting on the historic 1944 landing operations"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Asan Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A rugged trail that climbs up to the ridgeline, offering spectacular views of the Asan Beach landing zones and the surrounding tropical forest."},{name:"Piti Guns Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, well-marked path that leads visitors through the jungle to see three large coastal defense guns placed by Japanese forces."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Marine Corps Drive (Route 1) Coastal Tour",length:"15 miles",description:"A driving route that connects the various park units along the western coastline, providing views of the Philippine Sea and access to multiple historical markers."}],visitorCenter:{name:"T. Stell Newman Visitor Center",info:"Located just outside the main gate of Naval Base Guam, this facility offers interactive exhibits, a bookstore, and orientation films.",phone:"(671) 333-4055",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM – 4:30 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Junior Ranger Program","Battlefield History Guided Walking Tours"],campingInfo:"Camping is not permitted within the boundaries of the War in the Pacific National Historical Park.",wildlife:["Green Sea Turtle","Mariana Fruit Bat","Micronesian Kingfisher","Blacktip Reef Shark"],bestViewpoints:["Asan Ridge Lookout","Ga'an Point"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"1356 Route 1, Sumay, Guam 96915",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=T.+Stell+Newman+Visitor+Center+Guam",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/wapa/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"The dry season from January to May offers the most reliable weather for hiking and snorkeling.",accessibilityInfo:"The T. Stell Newman Visitor Center is fully ADA accessible. Portions of the coastal parks feature accessible walkways, though rugged inland trails remain natural and steep.",travelTips:["Stay hydrated as the humidity and tropical sun can be intense even on cloudy days","Bring reef-safe sunscreen if you plan to snorkel in the park's coastal waters","Respect all historical artifacts; do not remove, touch, or climb on WWII relics"],nearbyAttractions:["Fish Eye Marine Park","Fort Santa Cruz","Governor Joseph Flores Memorial Park"],designation:"National Historical Park"}]},r={hawaii:[{id:"haleakal-national-park",name:"Haleakalā National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3D05E583-1DD8-B71B-0BBFF82F7F78AF6A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3D771172-1DD8-B71B-0B981DA08B1FE163.jpg"],shortDescription:"Haleakalā National Park preserves the dormant Haleakalā volcano, offering breathtaking sunrise views above the clouds and unique high-altitude ecosystems.",fullDescription:"Dominating the eastern side of Maui, Haleakalā National Park encompasses the summit and crater of the world's largest dormant volcano. Visitors can witness spectacular sunrises or sunsets from 10,023 feet, explore the volcanic landscapes of the crater floor, and discover rare endemic species like the nēnē (Hawaiian goose) and the ʻāhinahina (Haleakalā silversword). The park also protects the lush Kipahulu Valley, accessible via the scenic Road to Hāna, showcasing pristine rainforest and dramatic waterfalls.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Moderate Hike into Haleakalā's Crater",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Enter the park's Wilderness on the Keoneheʻeheʻe (Sliding Sands) Trail and take a moderate out-and-back hike partway down the windswept cinder slope. This is among the most popular hikes in the park thanks to its unbeata"},{name:"Strenuous Hike Across the Crater Floor",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"7-9 Hours",description:'Experienced hikers can "thru" hike the crater from Keoneheʻeheʻe (Sliding Sands) Trailhead, across the crater floor, and out to Halemauʻu Trailhead. This hike is extremely strenuous and should only be undertaken by indiv'},{name:"Short Hike to Leleiwi Overlook",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Take a short .3 mile (.5 km) roundtrip walk out to Leleiwi Overlook. Pass native plants on your way to a spectacular view of the crater and the Koʻolau Gap."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Haleakalā Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 572-4400"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Haleakal%C4%81%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hale",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"hawaii-volcanoes-national-park",name:"Hawaii Volcanoes National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C4E8415A-08E5-5976-833F494FFCA3FFE6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C5D3655B-BF42-2E22-8068376035581BB2.jpg"],shortDescription:"Hawaii Volcanoes National Park is home to two of the world's most active volcanoes, Kīlauea and Mauna Loa, constantly shaping the landscape with lava flows.",fullDescription:"This dynamic park on Hawaiʻi Island showcases the raw power of creation and destruction, providing unparalleled opportunities to witness ongoing volcanic processes. Visitors can explore vast lava fields, steam vents, and historic craters, often experiencing the glow of molten lava from Kīlauea. The park also protects diverse ecosystems, from lush rainforests to stark volcanic deserts, alongside sacred cultural sites that tell the story of the Hawaiian people's deep connection to their land and the goddess Pele.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Haʻakulamanu via Sulphur Banks Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-45 Minutes",description:"National park encompassing two active volcanoes, Kīlauea and Mauna Loa, with dramatic volcanic landscapes, rare flora and fauna, and cultural and educational programs."},{name:"Kūpinaʻi Pali (Waldron Ledge)",difficulty:"Easy",length:"45-60 Minutes",description:"Walk along an old road damaged by an earthquake in 1983 and get panoramic views of Kaluapele"},{name:"Devastation Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Stroll on a paved path through a recovering landscape that was buried by falling cinder from the 1959 Kīlauea Iki eruption."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Hawaii Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 985-6011"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hawaii%20Volcanoes%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/havo",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"kalaupapa-national-historical-park",name:"Kalaupapa National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79B6CD-1DD8-B71B-0BB2B4265DD8C023.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79B7CD-1DD8-B71B-0B8DB166CD47FFDB.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79B8BD-1DD8-B71B-0B3642258A7FDC63.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79BAC7-1DD8-B71B-0B078B6EFFA7389B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Kalaupapa National Historical Park preserves the remote Molokaʻi peninsula, a place of isolation and resilience for those afflicted with Hansen's disease.",fullDescription:"Nestled beneath towering sea cliffs, Kalaupapa offers a profound glimpse into a unique chapter of Hawaiian and world history. From 1866 to 1969, thousands of people diagnosed with Hansen's disease (leprosy) were forcibly isolated here. The park honors their strength and spirit, alongside the enduring legacy of caregivers like Saint Damien and Saint Marianne Cope. Its stunning natural beauty, including the world's tallest sea cliffs, serves as a poignant backdrop to stories of compassion, struggle, and eventual healing within this incredibly isolated community.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Kalaupapa Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 567-6802"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kalaupapa%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kala",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"kaloko-honok-hau-national-historical-park",name:"Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6BC27DEB-B36A-8C1A-B085AE8A9DD275D7.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6BC27DEB-B36A-8C1A-B085AE8A9DD275D7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Kaloko-Honokōhau National Historical Park protects ancient Hawaiian settlements, including elaborate fishponds, petroglyphs, and coastal living sites.",fullDescription:"This coastal park on the Kona coast of Hawaiʻi Island offers a tangible connection to traditional Hawaiian life. It preserves the remnants of an ancient Hawaiian settlement, including well-preserved fishponds (loko iʻa), ancient house sites, sacred temples (heiau), and captivating petroglyph fields. Visitors can walk the historic coastal trails, observe native wildlife, and learn about the sustainable practices and intricate social structures that allowed early Hawaiians to thrive in this beautiful yet challenging environment. The park embodies a living cultural landscape, where the past informs the present.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Take a Lava Hike!",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Many trails in the park were created in ancient times and others are from the historical era. These were trails for pack animals. The main park trail is a loop that circles the park and is 4.5 miles long or 7.2 kilometer"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Kaloko-Honokōhau Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 329-6881"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kaloko-Honok%C5%8Dhau%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kaho",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"pu-uhonua-o-h-naunau-national-historical-park",name:"Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6CE82489-D587-C5DF-A30BD917F1D7270B.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park is an ancient Hawaiian place of refuge, where those who broke kapu (sacred laws) could seek sanctuary.",fullDescription:"This sacred site on the Kona coast of Hawaiʻi Island served as a critical haven in ancient times, providing absolution for kapu breakers and protection for defeated warriors. The park features meticulously restored temples (heiau), royal grounds, and a massive L-shaped wall dating back to the late 1500s, delineating the sacred ground. Visitors can walk among ancient structures, observe traditional Hawaiian craftsmanship, and immerse themselves in the deep spiritual and cultural significance of this remarkable 'place of refuge' that preserved peace and order in ancient Hawaiian society.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the 1871 Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"The rugged lava shoreline of the South Kona coast beckons you to explore the backcountry trails of the park. Have you ever imagined visiting a historic abandoned Hawaiian village? Come and experience this special and uni"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Puʻuhonua Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 328-2326"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Sunset times vary throughout the year. For exact closing times, please see our Facebook page or call the visitor center.",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pu%CA%BBuhonua%20o%20H%C5%8Dnaunau%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/puho",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"honouliuli-national-historic-site",name:"Honouliuli National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-honouliuli-national-historic-site-hawaii-nps.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Honouliuli National Historic Site marks the location of the largest and longest-operating World War II internment camp in Hawaiʻi.",fullDescription:"This solemn site on Oʻahu commemorates the experiences of those incarcerated during World War II, including Japanese Americans and foreign nationals of Japanese, German, and Italian descent, as well as prisoners of war. Honouliuli played a critical role in the wartime narrative, serving as a significant site of civilian internment and POW detention from 1943 until 1946. While remnants of the camp are still being preserved, the site serves as a powerful reminder of the fragility of civil liberties during times of national crisis and promotes reflection on justice and resilience.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Honouliuli Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 422-3300"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Honouliuli National Monument is currently closed to the public.",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Honouliuli%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hono",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"pearl-harbor-national-memorial",name:"Pearl Harbor National Memorial",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/h-pearl-harbor-uss-bowfin.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Pearl Harbor National Memorial commemorates the December 7, 1941 attack, honoring those who perished and marking the United States' entry into World War II.",fullDescription:"Located on Oʻahu, this memorial serves as a poignant reminder of the devastating surprise attack that thrust the United States into World War II. The centerpiece is the USS Arizona Memorial, built over the sunken hull of the battleship where 1,177 crewmen lost their lives. Visitors can also explore exhibits detailing the attack, its aftermath, and the broader context of the war in the Pacific. Pearl Harbor is a sacred site of reflection, remembrance, and education, ensuring that the sacrifices made on that fateful day are never forgotten.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Pearl Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 422-3399"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pearl%20Harbor%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/perl",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Memorial"},{id:"ala-kahakai-national-historic-trail",name:"Ala Kahakai National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/12DBDA50-CD81-A60F-A535BCB55C07A5F9.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6A4CC75F-C4A7-736C-7464E6E7C177E8BE.jpg"],shortDescription:"Ala Kahakai National Historic Trail is a 175-mile network of ancient Hawaiian foot trails, connecting sacred sites, settlements, and resources along the coast of Hawaiʻi Island.",fullDescription:"This remarkable trail system winds its way along the coast of Hawaiʻi Island, offering a pathway through history and culture. Originally used by ancient Hawaiians for travel, trade, and cultural practices, the trail links numerous ahupuaʻa (land divisions), ancient Hawaiian habitation sites, fishponds, and heiau. While not a contiguous recreational trail, it protects significant cultural landscapes and offers opportunities to experience the island's natural beauty and rich heritage. The Ala Kahakai is a living testament to the enduring connection between the Hawaiian people and their ancestral lands and seas.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Take a Lava Hike!",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Many trails in the park were created in ancient times and others are from the historical era. These were trails for pack animals. The main park trail is a loop that circles the park and is 4.5 miles long or 7.2 kilometer"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ala Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(808) 217-0307"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Hawaii, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ala%20Kahakai%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/alka",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],idaho:[{id:"yellowstone-national-park",name:"Yellowstone National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/28B56C01-ABED-ED7B-FDC8E6D3FC843D09.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/28B56C01-ABED-ED7B-FDC8E6D3FC843D09.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/29EB105C-BCE3-4859-9B83F22E1091AA2F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D5920-1DD8-B71B-0B83F012ED802CEA.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2A83F1F6-BA74-D152-1F43D8C6910C3897.jpg"],shortDescription:"Yellowstone National Park is located primarily in Wyoming. Please refer to Wyoming for full information and details.",fullDescription:"This entry represents the portion of Yellowstone National Park located in this state. The park is famously known and managed as a single unit. For complete itineraries, visitor centers, hiking trails, camping info, and travel guides, please select Wyoming from the browse states list.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Yellowstone River Picnic Area Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-3 Hours",description:"Explore local highlights."},{name:"Yellowstone Lake Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"Hike to a high mountain meadow for a commanding view of the West Thumb of Yellowstone Lake and the distant Absaroka Mountains."},{name:"West Thumb Geyser Basin Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Stroll through a geyser basin of colorful hot springs and dormant lakeshore geysers situated on the scenic shores of Yellowstone Lake."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Yellowstone National Park Visitor Information",info:"Please select Wyoming for visitor center information and contact details.",hours:"Varies",phone:"Located in Wyoming. Please check the official NPS page or search under Wyoming for details."},rangerPrograms:[],campingInfo:"Refer to main state profile.",wildlife:[],bestViewpoints:[],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Idaho and Montana and Wyoming, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Yellowstone%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/yell",bestTimeToVisit:"Year-round",accessibilityInfo:"Varies.",travelTips:["Select Wyoming to view comprehensive itineraries and details."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"craters-of-the-moon-national-monument-and-preserve",name:"Craters of the Moon National Monument and Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B12C4D19-0A4E-52EC-8CE6D0D975978393.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B24EFB0A-ED35-B380-5D70548FBC2F5801.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B27903D0-EBEF-E6D7-5BBF613D6FB9B11C.jpg"],shortDescription:"Craters of the Moon features an otherworldly volcanic landscape with vast lava fields, cinder cones, and lava tubes that mimic the surface of the moon. This stark, dramatic terrain offers a unique glimpse into the Earth's fiery geological past.",fullDescription:"This vast ocean of lava flows and sagebrush desert, located in central Idaho, was formed by a series of volcanic eruptions over the past 15,000 years, creating one of the best-preserved flood basalt areas in the continental United States. Visitors can explore a diverse array of volcanic features, including spatter cones, craters, and extensive lava tube caves formed by flowing magma. The monument also protects a unique ecosystem where hardy plants and animals have adapted to thrive in extreme conditions. Designated for its exceptional geological value, Craters of the Moon provides a profound educational experience on volcanic processes and ecological resilience.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks","Hiking","Scenic Drives","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike Devils Orchard",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-45 Minutes",description:"Enjoy an easy, 0.5 mile walk through an open limber pine woodland."},{name:"Visit the Spatter Cones",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"10-15 Minutes",description:"Take a short walk to view some of the park's mini-volcanoes up close and personal."},{name:"Hike the North Crater Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"90-120 Minutes",description:"This strenuous 3.5-mile trail drops into the mouth of North Crater."}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Craters Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(208) 527-1300"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes","Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Idaho, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Craters%20of%20the%20Moon%20National%20Monument%20and%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/crmo",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash.","Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"hagerman-fossil-beds-national-monument",name:"Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3DE8A223-C218-D1DA-CB8CF6266B45585F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3D8D0614-0D98-C696-1C209934805FE0A4.jpg"],shortDescription:"Hagerman Fossil Beds protects the world's richest known fossil deposits from the late Pliocene epoch, including an extraordinary collection of Hagerman Horse fossils, the earliest known ancestor of modern horses. This site offers a window into North America's ancient grasslands.",fullDescription:"Situated along the Snake River in south-central Idaho, Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument preserves a remarkable record of prehistoric life from approximately 3.5 million years ago. The monument's main treasure is the fossil bed of over 200 individual Hagerman Horses, *Equus simplicidens*, along with more than 140 other species of Pliocene plants and animals. These fossils provide invaluable insights into the evolution of North American mammals and the ancient paleoenvironment of the western United States. While the primary fossil beds are not directly accessible to visitors, a visitor center and scenic overlooks provide interpretive exhibits and breathtaking views of the fossil-rich bluffs.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Emigrant Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Emigrant Trail roughly parallels visible remnants of the Oregon Trail, capturing some of the experience of mid-1800s emigrants moving West."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Hagerman Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(208) 933-4105"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The road leading to the Monument is a public road and is open 24 hours a day. Access to the Monument Overlooks closes at dusk.",address:"Idaho, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hagerman%20Fossil%20Beds%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hafo",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"nez-perce-national-historical-park",name:"Nez Perce National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/31a3c6eb36257487.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87AFBC-1DD8-B71B-0BE33C27113E993C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87B657-1DD8-B71B-0B0970B12C65A5F7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Nez Perce National Historical Park connects 38 geographically dispersed sites across four states, preserving the history, culture, and stories of the Nimiipuu (Nez Perce) people and their interactions with diverse cultures. This unique park honors a resilient nation.",fullDescription:"This expansive national historical park tells the story of the Nimiipuu (Nez Perce) people, whose ancestral lands spanned parts of Idaho, Oregon, Washington, and Montana. The park encompasses significant cultural, historical, and sacred sites, detailing their traditional way of life, their profound connection to the land, and their complex interactions with explorers, missionaries, and settlers. It commemorates pivotal moments such as the 1877 Flight of the Nez Perce and their enduring sovereignty. Through its many sites, the park promotes understanding of Nez Perce culture, environmental stewardship, and the ongoing legacy of their struggle for justice and self-determination.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Nez Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(208) 843-7009"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Summer visitor center operating hours are 8:30 am - 4:00 pm daily. In the winter, the visitor center is open 5-days a week from Tuesday - Saturday from 8:30 am - 4:00 pm.",address:"Idaho and Montana and Oregon and Washington, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Nez%20Perce%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/nepe",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"minidoka-national-historic-site",name:"Minidoka National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C874621-1DD8-B71B-0B29C1E739E9977F.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Minidoka National Historic Site commemorates the experience of over 13,000 Japanese Americans forcibly incarcerated here during World War II, a somber reminder of a profound civil liberties violation. The site preserves their stories and the physical remnants of their temporary community.",fullDescription:"During World War II, the U.S. government incarcerated over 120,000 Japanese Americans without due process, based solely on their ancestry. Minidoka was one of ten such War Relocation Authority camps, where Japanese American citizens and resident aliens from Alaska, Washington, and Oregon were held captive from 1942 to 1945. The site now preserves foundational elements of the camp, including the entry gate, remnants of barracks, and the historic landscape. Through exhibits and interpretation, Minidoka National Historic Site ensures that the lessons of this challenging period are remembered and understood, serving as a powerful testament to civil rights and the enduring fight against prejudice.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Minidoka Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(208) 825-4169"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Historic Site is open daily during daylight hours year-round for self-guided tours. Visitor center hours and ranger program offerings vary seasonally.",address:"Idaho, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Minidoka%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/miin",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"city-of-rocks-national-reserve",name:"City of Rocks National Reserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/54D6C83C-0760-48AD-4EB8DE2C2C329980.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/54D6C83C-0760-48AD-4EB8DE2C2C329980.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A31DB8E0-1DD8-B71B-0B550930E432F744.jpg"],shortDescription:"City of Rocks National Reserve features a spectacular landscape of towering granite spires and monoliths, a world-renowned rock-climbing destination and a historic landmark on the California Trail. This geological wonder offers both extreme recreation and deep historical resonance.",fullDescription:"Nestled in the remote corner of southern Idaho, City of Rocks is famous for its massive, granite-sculpted rock formations, some rising hundreds of feet into the air. These unique geologic features have attracted emigrants on the California Trail, who left behind axle grease graffiti, and continue to draw world-class rock climbers today. The reserve protects a unique high-desert ecosystem rich in biodiversity, including pinyon pine and juniper forests. Co-managed by the National Park Service and the Idaho Department of Parks and Recreation, it provides a fascinating blend of natural beauty, challenging outdoor activities, and a tangible link to America's westward expansion, making it a truly distinctive protected area.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Geological Interpretive Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"City of Rocks is well known for its geologic wonders. The Geological Interpretive Trail will guide you through many types of unique rock features and formations and explain the forces that created them. There are 12 inte"},{name:"Hike Creekside Towers Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Creekside Towers is an out-and-back 1.2-mile trail. Nearby trails (such as Stairways or South Fork Circle Creek) could make for a longer, more adventurous loop. The gentle grade of 122 feet relative elevation is suitable"},{name:"Hike the City of Rocks Loop",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"4-6 Hours",description:"This remote loop hike is a ranger favorite. The combination of pine trees, rock formations, and epic views offer a surprise at every turn of the trail."}],scenicDrives:["City Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"City Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(208) 824-5901"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Idaho, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=City%20of%20Rocks%20National%20Reserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/ciro",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Preserve"},{id:"california-national-historic-trail",name:"California National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7987-1DD8-B71B-0B3C3123ED8F686B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7CDA-1DD8-B71B-0B999C4AC7A9D37C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7E5D-1DD8-B71B-0B0A1BE6987E18AF.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7FCF-1DD8-B71B-0BA502EF5988BC47.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/56ACD550-C0CE-423B-9448A9D0ABCDD9E7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. The California National Historic Trail is over 5,000 miles long and covers portions of 10 states. Step into history along more than 1,000 miles of ruts and traces from travelers and their overland wagons.",fullDescription:"California National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. The California National Historic Trail is over 5,000 miles long and covers portions of 10 states. Step into history along more than 1,000 miles of ruts and traces from travelers and their overland wagons. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the California Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. There are a variety of hikin"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"California Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for California National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Idaho, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=California%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cali/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"ice-age-floods-national-geologic-trail",name:"Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A003C5CE-E0EB-2AC6-FC8EDC134B1DFD19.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/0E7BEDF2-952B-D9E0-038D808912A45A65.jpeg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/D4C33AFB-B84F-E87C-E19705F8380417D1.jpeg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/0BDAB952-1DD8-B71B-0B509D5898489BD4.jpg"],shortDescription:"At the end of the last Ice Age, 18,000 to 15,000 years ago, an ice dam in northern Idaho created Glacial Lake Missoula stretching 3,000 square miles around Missoula, Montana. The dam burst and released flood waters across Washington, down the Columbia River into Oregon before reaching the Pacific Ocean. The Ice Age Floods forever changed the lives and landscape of the Pacific Northwest.",fullDescription:"Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail is an official National Park Service site. At the end of the last Ice Age, 18,000 to 15,000 years ago, an ice dam in northern Idaho created Glacial Lake Missoula stretching 3,000 square miles around Missoula, Montana. The dam burst and released flood waters across Washington, down the Columbia River into Oregon before reaching the Pacific Ocean. The Ice Age Floods forever changed the lives and landscape of the Pacific Northwest. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ice Age Floods Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(509) 237-9722"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Idaho, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ice%20Age%20Floods%20National%20Geologic%20Tra