rous plants, thriving in habitats from oak savannas to black oak forests. Visitors can explore miles of sandy beaches, hike through diverse habitats, and birdwatch, discovering why this park is considered one of the most biodiverse areas in North America. Its unique setting, where industrial landscapes meet pristine nature, offers a compelling study in conservation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"1966 Hiking Challenge",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-5 Hours",description:"The 1966 Hiking Challenge is comprised of 19 hikes that cover 66 miles of trail in the park, celebrating the park’s founding in 1966. We will offer 19 weekly ranger led hikes every Saturday at 8:00 am starting on May 11 "},{name:"Hiking Indiana Dunes",difficulty:"Easy",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Indiana Dunes is home to one of the most biologically diverse habitats in the nation. Hiking is a great way to explore these fascinating landscape. Traverse over 50 miles of trails that lead you through shorelines, dunes"}],scenicDrives:["Indiana Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Indiana Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(219) 395-1882"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The general areas of the park are open year-round. Please check individual areas for specific hours of operation.",address:"Indiana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Indiana%20Dunes%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/indu/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"george-rogers-clark-national-historical-park",name:"George Rogers Clark National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/94fe9c6087b072a2.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"George Rogers Clark National Historical Park commemorates the American victory at Fort Sackville in 1779, a pivotal moment that secured the Old Northwest Territory for the fledgling United States during the Revolutionary War. The grand memorial stands as a tribute to Clark's daring winter campaign and its lasting impact on American expansion.",fullDescription:"This park preserves the site of Fort Sackville in Vincennes, Indiana, where Lt. Col. George Rogers Clark and his frontier militia achieved a remarkable victory over British forces during the American Revolution. Clark's audacious winter march through flooded plains to capture the fort on February 25, 1779, was crucial in expanding the western boundaries of the nascent nation. The park's central feature is a massive circular memorial, housing a heroic statue of Clark and detailed murals depicting the campaign. Visitors can explore the memorial, learn about the frontier conflicts, and understand how this strategic victory shaped the future of the United States.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"George Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(812) 882-1776"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site