ent protects a sacred landscape of over 200 ancient burial mounds, including 31 shaped like animals, constructed by indigenous peoples from about 500 BCE to 1300 CE. These earthworks, primarily built by the Late Woodland cultures, served as ceremonial and burial sites, reflecting profound spiritual beliefs and connections to the natural world. Perched above the Mississippi River, the monument also offers panoramic views and protects diverse forest, prairie, and wetland ecosystems, providing a tangible link to millennia of human history and ecological heritage in the Upper Mississippi River Valley.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Effigy Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(563) 873-3491"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Park trails are open daily from dawn to dusk.",address:"Iowa, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Effigy%20Mounds%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/efmo/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"herbert-hoover-national-historic-site",name:"Herbert Hoover National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7924AC-1DD8-B71B-0BEDCDE2B64225D5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C792782-1DD8-B71B-0BCA6D30498E1D77.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C792600-1DD8-B71B-0B560B18E7EC1A12.jpg"],shortDescription:"Explore the humble beginnings of America's 31st president, Herbert Hoover, from his tiny birthplace cottage to the Quaker meetinghouse that shaped his character.",fullDescription:"Herbert Hoover National Historic Site preserves the birthplace and early home of the 31st U.S. President in West Branch, Iowa, offering a glimpse into his humble Quaker upbringing. Visitors can tour Hoover's tiny 1874 birthplace cottage, the village blacksmith shop, and the Quaker meetinghouse, all meticulously preserved to reflect the rural community that shaped his values. The site also includes the Presidential Library and Museum, the final resting place of President and Mrs. Hoover, and a reconstructed tallgrass prairie, providing a comprehensive narrative of his life and legacy from his origins to his time in public service.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Walk the Nature Trails",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Herbert Hoover National Historic Site offers over two miles of trails through the tallgrass prairie and along Hoover Creek for hiking, cross-country skiing, snowshoeing, and nature study. Trail maps are available from th"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Herbert Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(319) 643-2541"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"From April 1 - 