on:"The Hot Springs and North Mountain trails are popular since they are easy to reach and provide scenic views. You can get to these trails via Stephen’s Balustrade (grand staircase) behind the Fordyce Bathhouse, Hot Spring"},{name:"Mountain Top Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"45-75 Minutes",description:"Mountaintop trail can be linked with Canyon Trail and West Mountain trail to complete a loop around West Mountain. If hiking southward, the trail is strenuous, as it gains elevation quickly. If hiking northward, there is"},{name:"Upper Dogwood Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"25-45 Minutes",description:"Upper Dogwood trail has short rises and long stretches of flat trail. It can be combined with the Lower Dogwood Trail or the Goat Rock Trail to create a longer hike."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Hot Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(501) 620-6715"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Arlington Lawn, Grand Promenade, sidewalks on Bathhouse Row, Reserve Street, and Fountain Street, except for walk through traffic, are open from to 5:00 am to 10:00 pm, daily. Roads to Hot Springs Mountain, North Mountain and West Mountain ",address:"Arkansas and Kansas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hot%20Springs%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hosp/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"tallgrass-prairie-national-preserve",name:"Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C799F41-1DD8-B71B-0BE449FCE70F95CA.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79A5B5-1DD8-B71B-0B5E2901D9440F01.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79A6D7-1DD8-B71B-0BD8C3F1DF87DCC1.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79A80D-1DD8-B71B-0B4393C9BEF437B3.jpg"],shortDescription:"Explore one of the last remaining significant tracts of tallgrass prairie, a once-vast ecosystem that covered a large portion of North America and supported immense herds of bison.",fullDescription:"Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve protects a representative portion of the once 170-million-acre tallgrass prairie ecosystem, now less than 4% remains. This vast expanse of waving grasses, wildflowers, and rolling hills provides a glimpse into America's natural heritage, where bison graze freely. Visitors can hike scenic trails, tour the historic Spring Hill Ranch, and learn about the delicate balance of fire and grazing essential to maintaining this unique grassland.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Tallgrass Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(620) 273-8494"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Kansas, USA",googleMapsLocation