open 9:00 am - 5:00 pm eastern time.`,address:"Kentucky, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Abraham%20Lincoln%20Birthplace%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/abli/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"cumberland-gap-national-historical-park",name:"Cumberland Gap National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87BB9F-1DD8-B71B-0B03EA00319ED876.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87BA5D-1DD8-B71B-0B9E79FC9A030567.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic mountain pass through the Appalachian Mountains, a vital route for Native Americans, pioneers, and Daniel Boone's Wilderness Road.",fullDescription:"Cumberland Gap National Historical Park protects the legendary natural pass that served as a gateway to the American West for thousands of years. This critical transportation route facilitated migrations for Native Americans, later becoming the path for over 300,000 pioneers who traversed Daniel Boone's Wilderness Road into Kentucky and beyond. The park offers stunning scenic views, historic sites, and diverse ecosystems, allowing visitors to experience the landscapes that shaped American expansion and explore miles of hiking trails.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cumberland Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(606) 248-2817"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"The park is open 24 hours a day to pedestrian and cyclist, weather permitting. Some services and facilities have separate hours, however; please see descriptions of those facilities elsewhere.",address:"Kentucky and Tennessee and Virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cumberland%20Gap%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"fort-donelson-national-battlefieldfort-donelson-national-cemetery",name:"Fort Donelson National BattlefieldFort Donelson National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6903ED5B-1DD8-B71B-0B29CCAFAED7A0BF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F26FAA11-B46E-B499-F5F9ECA3A44EA1AC.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the crucial 1862 Union victory at Fort Donelson, a strategic triumph that opened Tennessee to Federal forces and propelled Ulysses S. Grant to national prominence.",fullDescription:"Fort Donelson National Battlefield preserves the site of a pivotal early Civil War battle where Union forces, led by Brigadier General Ulysses S. Grant, achieved their first major victory in February 1862. This strategic success secured vital rivers and railroads, weakening the Confederate hold on Kentucky and opening the path to Nashville. The park includes preserved earthworks, historic structures, and a national cemetery, offering visitors a poignant understanding of the battle's intensity and its significant impact on the course of the war.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Trails at Fort Donelson NB",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"45-90 Minutes",description:"Immerse yourself in the beauty of the historic site by hiking the park trails. You may see migratory birds, bald eagles, and lovely native wildflowers. Lovely views of the Cumberland River/Barkley Lake may be seen as wel"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(931) 232-5706"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Fort Donelson NB Visitor Center is open daily 8:30 am - 4:00 pm CST. Dover Hotel hours are 8:30 am - 4:00 pm CST daily.",address:"Kentucky and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Donelson%20National%20BattlefieldFort%20Donelson%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fodo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"big-south-fork-national-river-and-recreation-area",name:"Big South Fork National River and Recreation Area*",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C793E18-1DD8-B71B-0B815C3EA6EF7F23.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Encompasses a rugged gorge and pristine river system, offering extensive recreational opportunities amidst stunning sandstone cliffs, natural arches, and diverse Appalachian forests.",fullDescription:"Big South Fork National River and Recreation Area protects the free-flowing Big South Fork of the Cumberland River and its tributaries, carving a dramatic gorge through the Cumberland Plateau. This expansive area boasts an incredible array of outdoor activities, including whitewater paddling, hiking, horseback riding, and rock climbing, all set against a backdrop of towering sandstone bluffs, natural arches, and cascading waterfalls. Its diverse ecosystems support a rich variety of plant and animal life, making it a significant natural and recreational resource spanning Kentucky and Tennessee.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming","Scenic Drives","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Big Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(423) 569-9778"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters","Mule Deer","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Kentucky and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.