gest and most complex prehistoric earthwork sites in North America, featuring concentric ridges and mounds constructed by indigenous peoples between 1700 and 1100 BC. This UNESCO World Heritage site offers unparalleled insight into an advanced, sedentary society that developed sophisticated trading networks and monumental architecture long before agriculture became widespread in the region. Visitors can explore the vast earthworks and learn about the unique culture that thrived along the Mississippi floodplains.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Poverty Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(318) 926-5492"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Poverty Point National Monument is managed by the state of Louisiana. Closed on Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day and New Year's Day.",address:"Louisiana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Poverty%20Point%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/popo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"cane-river-creole-national-historical-park",name:"Cane River Creole National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/cane-river-creole-national-historical-park-louisiana.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The park preserves the unique cultural landscapes of Creole life along the Cane River, showcasing the interwoven stories of plantation owners, enslaved people, and free people of color.",fullDescription:"Cane River Creole National Historical Park encompasses two historic Creole cotton plantations, Oakland and Magnolia, offering a rare glimpse into a distinct Louisiana culture that emerged from French, Spanish, African, and Native American influences. Through preserved buildings, landscapes, and interpretive programs, the park explores the complex history of enslavement, agricultural labor, and the enduring legacy of families whose lives shaped this region for generations. It provides a poignant understanding of both the hardships and resilience of the Cane River Creoles.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cane Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(318) 356-8441"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Louisiana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cane%20River%20Creole%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/crha/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"jean-lafitte-national-historical-park-and-preservechalmette-national-cemetery",name:"Jean Lafitte National Historical Park and PreserveChalmette National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C845D16-1DD8-B71B-0B04181AABF4F0C1.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C845E7E-1DD8-B71B-0BE1AC184B0AF650.jpg"],shortDescription:"Chalmette Battlefield commemorates the decisive Battle of New Orleans in 1815, where Andrew Jackson's forces secured a crucial American victory against the British, shaping the nation's future.",fullDescription:"This section of Jean Lafitte National Historical Park and Preserve is dedicated to preserving the site of the final major battle of the War of 1812. Visitors can explore the battlefield where General Andrew Jackson's diverse army decisively defeated the British, ensuring American control of the port of New Orleans and significantly boosting national morale. Adjacent to the battlefield, Chalmette National Cemetery honors veterans from various conflicts, serving as a solemn reminder of those who served the nation. The site offers a powerful narrative of military strategy, American identity, and sacrifice.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Jean Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(504) 281-0510"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Louisiana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Jean%20Lafitte%20National%20Historical%20Park%20and%20PreserveChalmette%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/jela/chalmette-battlefield.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"new-orleans-jazz-national-historical-park",name:"New Orleans Jazz National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87C505-1DD8-B71B-0BE34A8A361477FB.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"This park celebrates the birthplace of jazz, an American art form born from the vibrant cultural melting pot of New Orleans, through live performances and educational programs.",fullDescription:"New Orleans Jazz National Historical Park is dedicated to preserving and sharing the rich history and evolution of jazz music, originating in the diverse neighborhoods of New Orleans. The park's mission is to educate and entertain visitors about this influential art form through daily ranger talks, musical demonstrations, and live performances by local jazz musicians. It delves into the cultural influences	re-African, Caribbean, European	rethat converged to create jazz, highlighting its enduring legacy and impact on global music.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"New Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(504) 589-3882"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"These are the standard hours for the French Quarter Visitor Center for New Orleans Jazz National Historical Park and the Jean Lafitte National Historical Park and Preserve at 419 Decatur St, New Orleans, LA 70130. The secondary site for New",address:"Louisiana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=New%20Orleans%20Jazz%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/jazz/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"vicksburg-national-military-parkvicksburg-national-cemetery",name:"Vicksburg National Military ParkVicksburg National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79FEFF-1DD8-B71B-0B68A290E3DBE016.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79FEFF-1DD8-B71B-0B68A290E3DBE016.jpg"],shortDescription:"Vicksburg National Military Park commemorates the pivotal 47-day Siege of Vicksburg, a critical Union victory that split the Confederacy and secured control of the Mississippi River during the Civil War.",fullDescription:"This sprawling park preserves the battlefields, fortifications, and trenches where Union and Confederate forces clashed during the decisive 1863 campaign. Visitors can drive a 16-mile tour road, explore over 1,300 monuments, markers, and restored cannon, and reflect at the USS Cairo Museum. The adjacent Vicksburg National Cemetery is the final resting place for over 17,000 Union soldiers, making it the second-largest national cemetery in the United States. The park offers an immersive experience into the strategic importance and human cost of this pivotal Civil War siege.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Vicksburg Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(601) 636-0583"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The Visitor Center is open 8:30 am to 4:30 pm Wednesday-Sunday. Tour road is open from 8:30 am to 5 pm daily with last vehicle entry at 4:40 pm.",address:"Louisiana and Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Vicksburg%20National%20Military%20ParkVicksburg%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/vick/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"el-camino-real-de-los-tejas-national-historic-trail",name:"El Camino Real de los Tejas National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E7952-1DD8-B71B-0BFB5B12C3B0E5DA.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E7D2D-1DD8-B71B-0BBC1AD8F8F8B91C.jpg"],shortDescription:"This historic trail traces the ancient route used by Native Americans, Spanish explorers, and Anglo-American settlers, linking colonial Mexico to Spanish Texas and Louisiana.",fullDescription:"El Camino Real de los Tejas National Historic Trail preserves and interprets the historic trade and travel route that served as a vital lifeline for centuries, facilitating the exploration, settlement, and evangelization of the Spanish frontier. Stretching nearly 2,600 miles through Texas and Louisiana, the trail tells stories of indigenous peoples, Spanish missionaries, soldiers, and American pioneers who shaped the cultural landscape of the Southwest. While not a continuous hiking trail, visitors can explore preserved segments and interpretive sites that reveal the significant impact of this historic pathway on regional development and cultural exchange.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on El Camino Real de los Tejas",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Travel through a diverse array of histories contained within El Camino Real de los Tejas’ 150-year life, including the Spanish struggle to missionize American Indian nations, the growth of cattle ranching in the Mexican "}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"El Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Louisiana and Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=El%20Camino%20Real%20de%20los%20Tejas%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/elte/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],maine:[{id:"acadia-national-park",name:"Acadia National Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0dd5f243102376d9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Acadia National Park protects a rugged coastline of granite peaks, historic lighthouses, and island ecosystems shaped by glaciers, offering dramatic ocean views and diverse wildlife as the first national park east of the Mississippi.",fullDescription:"Established in 1916, Acadia National Park on Mount Desert Island boasts Cadillac Mountain, the highest point on the U.S. East Coast, where visitors can witness the nation's first sunrise. Its landscape, carved by ancient glaciers, features dramatic granite cliffs, rocky beaches, and dense forests interspersed with tranquil lakes and ponds. The park offers extensive historic carriage roads, perfect for biking and walking, alongside iconic sites like Bass Harbor Head Light and the thunderous waves of Thunder Hole, preserving both natural beauty and a rich cultural heritage.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike Beehive Loop",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"This is a 1.4 mile loop trail that ascends a 450 ft cliff face. Exposed cliffs, iron rungs, and granite staircases climb this trail revealing scenic ocean views."},{name:"Hike Jesup Path and Hemlock Path Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"This forested path continues to a boardwalk with multiple interpretive signs and benches. It opens up to a meadow before looping back on an old fire road. Views of Dorr Mountain are interspersed in this walk along a birc"},{name:"Hike Perpendicular and Razorback Loop",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"The loop features a gravel shoreline path along Long Pond and a steep ascent on granite staircase of over 300 stone steps built by the CCC to reach the summit of Mansell Mountain."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Acadia Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(207) 288-3338"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Maine, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Acadia%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/acad",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"frances-perkins-national-monument",name:"Frances Perkins National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2CC7130F-941F-CA23-DB43310BB9EA5AAB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/23F044BC-D30D-0BC8-D7F62B9544DE0559.jpg"],shortDescription:"This monument honors Frances Perkins, the first woman cabinet member and architect of the New Deal, preserving her ancestral homestead in Newcastle, Maine, and her enduring legacy of social justice.",fullDescription:"Designated in 2023, the Frances Perkins National Monument commemorates the life and legacy of Frances Perkins, Secretary of Labor under President Franklin D. Roosevelt. Located at her family's historic homestead, the site provides insight into the formative experiences that shaped her pioneering work in labor rights, social security, and fair employment standards. It serves as a place for reflection on her enduring contributions to American society and the welfare of its workers, highlighting the deep roots of her commitment to social justice in rural Maine.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Frances Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(207) 385-6393"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The historic buildings of the homestead are currently closed until summer of 2025, including the Perkins’ family ancestral home known as the “Brick House”. The grounds are open from sunrise to sunset daily year-round.",address:"Maine, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Frances%20Perkins%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/frpe",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"katahdin-woods-and-waters-national-monument",name:"Katahdin Woods and Waters National Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c7a118e9345dff50.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"This vast monument in northern Maine preserves a unique blend of pristine forests, vital waterways, and views of Mount Katahdin, offering exceptional recreational opportunities and an International Dark Sky Park experience.",fullDescription:"Katahdin Woods and Waters National Monument, established in 2016, protects over 87,000 acres of Maine's North Woods, characterized by its stunning rivers, streams, and resilient forests. The monument provides critical habitat for diverse wildlife, including moose and black bears, and offers unparalleled opportunities for canoeing, kayaking, hiking, and remote camping. It also boasts exceptionally dark night skies, recognized as an International Dark Sky Park, providing a rare glimpse into an unpolluted celestial canvas while contributing to the region's conservation and recreational economy.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike Deasey Ponds Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"45-90 Minutes",description:"Hike to Deasey Ponds by taking an easy 1.4 mile (round-trip) trail located at the southern area of the monument. From the parking lot, there is an option to extend your hike to view another wetland habitat."},{name:"Hike To Orin Falls",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"3-6 Hours",description:"Hike to Orin Falls for a magnificent view of a cascading waterfall."},{name:"Hike the International Appalachian Trail",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"2-3 Days",description:"Hike a segment of the International Appalachian Trail that connects the southern section to the northern section of the monument."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Katahdin Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(207) 456-6001"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Maine, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Katahdin%20Woods%20and%20Waters%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kaww",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"saint-croix-island-international-historic-site",name:"Saint Croix Island International Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B31B6-1DD8-B71B-0BC1CE33970A8EAD.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorating one of the earliest European attempts at permanent settlement in North America, this site on the U.S.-Canada border preserves the story of French explorers in 1604 and their interactions with the Passamaquoddy people.",fullDescription:"Saint Croix Island International Historic Site marks the location of the 1604 French settlement attempt by Pierre Dugua, Sieur de Mons, and Samuel de Champlain, predating Jamestown and Plymouth. This isolated island in the St. Croix River, which forms part of the international boundary, tells a critical story of early European exploration, hardship, and the complex relationships with Native American populations. The site includes interpretive exhibits on the mainland, offering insight into the challenging winter endured by the settlers and the enduring cultural connections between France, Canada, and the United States, all while respecting the ancestral lands of the Passamaquoddy.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Saint Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(207) 454-0418"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"A park ranger will be available at the Visitor Center at the following days and times.",address:"Maine, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Saint%20Croix%20Island%20International%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sacr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"appalachian-trail-official-unit",name:"Appalachian Trail (official unit)",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg"],shortDescription:"The Appalachian Trail, a legendary 2,190-mile footpath, traverses diverse ecosystems and cultural landscapes from Georgia to Maine, offering unparalleled long-distance hiking experiences and connecting a vast network of wilderness.",fullDescription:"Managed in partnership with the Appalachian Trail Conservancy and other agencies, the Appalachian Trail (A.T.) is one of the world's longest continuously marked footpaths, celebrated for its rugged beauty and the challenge it presents to hikers. In Maine, the A.T. culminates in its most challenging sections, including the infamous Mahoosuc Notch and the ascent of Mount Katahdin, the trail's northern terminus. This iconic trail provides critical habitat corridors, preserves historical pathways, and offers millions of visitors annual opportunities for recreation, solitude, and connection with nature across 14 states.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Scenic Drives","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Appalachian Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(304) 535-6278"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes","Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Georgia and Maine, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Appalachian%20Trail%20(official%20unit)",officialWebsite:"https