tal cliffs, ideal for walking trails and wildlife observation. It stands as a symbol of enduring friendship between the two nations and a testament to Roosevelt's profound connection to this tranquil island.`,thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Roosevelt Campobello Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Roosevelt Campobello International Park, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(506) 752-2922"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open year-round from 1/2 hour before sunrise to a 1/2 hour after sunset.",address:"Maine, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Roosevelt%20Campobello%20International%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/roca",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"}],maryland:[{id:"fort-mchenry-national-monument-and-historic-shrine",name:"Fort McHenry National Monument and Historic Shrine",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6A3FE8B6-B5BB-8C96-907CC3EF8C1CBC51.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"This iconic fort famously withstood a brutal 25-hour bombardment during the War of 1812, inspiring Francis Scott Key to write 'The Star-Spangled Banner.'",fullDescription:"Fort McHenry stands as a profound symbol of American resilience and national pride, having protected Baltimore Harbor during the pivotal War of 1812. The fort's successful defense against the British navy directly led to the creation of the United States' national anthem. Visitors can explore the historic grounds, witness flag-changing ceremonies, and delve into the dramatic events that forged a young nation's identity and inspired its most enduring song.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(410) 962-4290"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $15; Commercial Entrance - Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Park registers for purchasing Star Fort Historic Zone entry or America the Beautiful Passes are open from 9:00 a.m. until 4 p.m.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20McHenry%20National%20Monument%20and%20Historic%20Shrine",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fomc",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"chesapeake-and-ohio-canal-national-historical-park",name:"Chesapeake and Ohio Canal National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EC77A-1DD8-B71B-0B67E179DDA9A05A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EC45C-1DD8-B71B-0B4EDFD56A427891.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EC77A-1DD8-B71B-0B67E179DDA9A05A.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/D2EBA333-E23E-7E6C-02B033D80810A556.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/D31F7088-9CCC-0BA9-0566A46AC1C9C147.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves 184.5 miles of a historic waterway along the Potomac River, offering a unique glimpse into 19th-century canal life and industrial heritage.",fullDescription:"The Chesapeake and Ohio Canal once served as a vital transportation artery, moving coal, agricultural products, and other goods between Cumberland, Maryland, and Washington D.C. Today, its extensive towpath, intricate lock houses, and historic structures provide an exceptional corridor for hiking, biking, and exploring both natural beauty and early American engineering. The park tells the story of early industrialization, the daily lives of canal boatmen, and the economic development of the Potomac River Valley, all while offering serene recreational opportunities.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming","Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"},{name:"Hike Along the Towpath Through the Paw Paw Tunnel",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Minutes",description:"Walk through the 3,118-foot Paw Paw Tunnel, a significant engineering feature along Mile 155 of the C&O Canal towpath. "}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Chesapeake Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 739-4200"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters","Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The towpath is open 365 days a year.",address:"Maryland and Virginia and West virginia and District of columbia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chesapeake%20and%20Ohio%20Canal%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/choh",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash.","Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"harpers-ferry-national-historical-park",name:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8294BB-1DD8-B71B-0BD70066438F7F42.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8294BB-1DD8-B71B-0BD70066438F7F42.jpg"],shortDescription:"Situated at the dramatic confluence of the Shenandoah and Potomac Rivers, this park preserves a town rich in history from early industry to John Brown's Raid and critical Civil War campaigns.",fullDescription:"Harpers Ferry witnessed pivotal moments that shaped the nation, from its role as an early industrial center with a federal arsenal to John Brown's infamous 1859 abolitionist raid that heightened national tensions. The town was also a strategic point repeatedly fought over during the Civil War. The park meticulously preserves the historic downtown, its pre-Civil War structures, and the stunning natural landscape, allowing visitors to explore museums, historic buildings, and scenic overlooks that trace themes of industry, abolition, and conflict.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike Civil War Skirmish Lines at Harpers Ferry",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1-8 Hours",description:"Did you know the park has over 20 miles of hiking trails? The trails vary from easy, riverside strolls to four-mile hikes across Civil War battlefields to eight-mile adventures on the tops of mountains."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Harpers Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(304) 535-6029"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park is open year-round with the exception of Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, and New Year's Day. Park museums/exhibits, the Visitor Center, and restroom facilities are open from 9 a.m.",address:"Maryland and Virginia and West virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harpers%20Ferry%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hafe",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"harriet-tubman-underground-railroad-national-historical-park",name:"Harriet Tubman Underground Railroad National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/harriet-tubman-underground-railroad-visitor-center.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorates the extraordinary life and enduring legacy of Harriet Tubman and the courageous network of the Underground Railroad on Maryland's Eastern Shore.",fullDescription:"This park honors Harriet Tubman, a Maryland-born enslaved woman who escaped to freedom and became a legendary conductor on the Underground Railroad, guiding hundreds to liberty. Located on the landscapes where she was born, lived, and toiled in Dorchester County, the park—along with the adjacent Harriet Tubman Underground Railroad State Park and Visitor Center—provides immersive experiences into her incredible journey and the broader stories of resistance against slavery. It powerfully highlights the profound struggle for freedom and human dignity in the face of systemic oppression.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Harriet Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(410) 221-2290"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The National Park Service jointly operates the visitor center facility for the public in partnership with the Maryland Park Service.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harriet%20Tubman%20Underground%20Railroad%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hatu",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"clara-barton-national-historic-site",name:"Clara Barton National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8477E7-1DD8-B71B-0BF1DE30138186A7.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the unique home and headquarters of Clara Barton, founder of the American Red Cross and pioneering humanitarian, in Glen Echo, Maryland.",fullDescription:"This historic site protects the final residence of Clara Barton, a remarkable nurse, teacher, and advocate who dedicated her life to humanitarian service. For the last 15 years of her life, this distinctive 38-room structure, built from salvaged lumber, served as both her home and the operational center for the nascent American Red Cross, coordinating disaster relief efforts. Visitors can tour her preserved residence to learn about her incredible contributions to public service and her foundational role in establishing one of the world's most vital aid organizations.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Clara Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 320-1400"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The unfurnished first floor of the Clara Barton National Historic Site is open for public visitation Fridays and Saturdays by guided tours at 1:00 pm, 2:00 pm, 3:00pm, and 4:00 pm. Closed Sun.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Clara%20Barton%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/clba",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"hampton-national-historic-site",name:"Hampton National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/30E70993-1DD8-B71B-0BF669D83ECFDB7A.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Showcases one of the grandest examples of late-18th-century Georgian architecture in America, reflecting immense wealth and the paradox of slavery in a young nation.",fullDescription:"Hampton National Historic Site features the magnificent Hampton Mansion, constructed between 1783 and 1790 by the Ridgely family, who became one of Maryland's wealthiest. The sprawling estate provides a vivid portrayal of post-Revolutionary War American society, contrasting the opulent lifestyle of its owners with the profound stories of enslaved African Americans and indentured servants who labored there. Visitors can explore the exquisitely preserved mansion, its formal gardens, and delve into the complex social and economic structures of the era, including the transition from slavery to paid labor.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Hampton Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(410) 962-4290"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Visitor Contact Station is open 9am – 4pm Thursday through Sunday. Grounds are open every day from dawn to dusk.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hampton%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hamp",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"thomas-stone-national-historic-site",name:"Thomas Stone National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/1045ACA0-BB5B-C428-2272CC5E8E76A87C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/10214C9E-0656-3AE1-429ED5F9A087A93A.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/10B21926-0B88-BEB9-AF5CD47AC4CEBF5D.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/10DA7147-C8C3-2AAD-C9562A7EF253D8E5.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the 18th-century home and plantation of Thomas Stone, a signer of the Declaration of Independence, in Charles County, Maryland.",fullDescription:"This site commemorates Thomas Stone, a distinguished Maryland lawyer and planter who courageously affixed his name to the Declaration of Independence, risking his life for American liberty. His preserved plantation, Haberdeventure, offers invaluable insight into the life of an 18th-century Maryland planter and politician, including the integral yet often overlooked role of enslaved people in maintaining the estate. Visitors can tour the historic house, the family cemetery, and the grounds, gaining a deeper understanding of the challenges, sacrifices, and ideals of the nation's founding generation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Long Loop: Stone Mansion",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"50-90 Minutes",description:"Hike along a more moderate trail to get to the Stone Mansion. Experience more of the natural world on the way to the Mansion."},{name:"Long Loop: Stone Family Cemetery",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"60-90 Minutes",description:"Looking for a longer, scenic stroll to the Stone family cemetery? This is a longer segment of a larger nature trail system."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Thomas Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(804) 224-1732"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Thomas Stone National Historic Site is open Saturdays and Sundays from 10:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Thomas%20Stone%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/thst",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"antietam-national-battlefieldantietam-national-cemetery",name:"Antietam National BattlefieldAntietam National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6E455943-9933-66E7-288EADAA891B7267.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6E455943-9933-66E7-288EADAA891B7267.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8085D6-1DD8-B71B-0BBFD59E69D8F9F6.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the deadliest single-day battle in American history, a pivotal turning point in the Civil War that led to the Emancipation Proclamation.",fullDescription:"Antietam National Battlefield preserves the hallowed ground where the Battle of Antietam (or Sharpsburg) was fought on September 17, 1862. This horrific engagement resulted in over 23,000 casualties in just twelve hours, marking it as the bloodiest single day in American military history. A crucial Union victory, it halted the Confederate invasion of Maryland and provided President Lincoln the strategic confidence needed to issue the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation. Visitors can tour the battlefield, the historic Pry House Field Hospital Museum, and Antietam National Cemetery, reflecting on the profound human cost and immense strategic significance of this pivotal Civil War engagement.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"The Union Advance Trail at Antietam",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"30-90 Minutes",description:"Hike the Union Advance Trail to learn about the fighting around the famous Burnside Bridge."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Antietam Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 432-5124"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The park grounds and park tour roads close at dark. (20 minutes after sunset).",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Antietam%20National%20BattlefieldAntietam%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/anti",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"monocacy-national-battlefield",name:"Monocacy National Battlefield",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80C467-1DD8-B71B-0B265910DE1D0AFA.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80C467-1DD8-B71B-0B265910DE1D0AFA.jpg"],shortDescription:'Known as "The Battle That Saved Washington," Monocacy National Battlefield preserves the site of a crucial Civil War engagement in July 1864, where Union forces delayed Confederate advances on Washington D.C.',fullDescription:"Monocacy National Battlefield commemorates the 1864 Battle of Monocacy, a Confederate attempt to capture Washington D.C. led by Lt. Gen. Jubal Early. Though a tactical Confederate victory, the fierce resistance by Union troops under Maj. Gen. Lew Wallace allowed Washington to reinforce its defenses, earning Monocacy its moniker. Visitors can explore preserved farmsteads, interpretive trails, and monuments across the battlefield, offering insights into a critical moment that shaped the Civil War's outcome.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Monocacy Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 662-3515"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds are open daily from 7:30 until 20 minutes after sunset.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Monocacy%20National%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mono/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"assateague-island-national-seashore",name:"Assateague Island National Seashore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C809F06-1DD8-B71B-0B39C9B927D2C688.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/78000DE7-F629-A6FF-17A9BD7C9CB1E0D3.jpg"],shortDescription:"Assateague Island National Seashore protects a 37-mile-long barrier island famed for its wild horses, pristine beaches, and diverse coastal ecosystems.",fullDescription:"Assateague Island National Seashore is a dynamic barrier island protecting bays, salt marshes, and maritime forests, renowned for its resident herds of wild horses. These iconic ponies roam freely across the beaches and marshes, captivating visitors. The seashore offers a range of recreational activities including camping, birdwatching, swimming, and kayaking, allowing guests to experience its unparalleled natural beauty and observe a rich array of wildlife in a protected coastal environment.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Assateague Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(410) 641-1441"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person: free (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Maryland and Virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Assateague%20Island%20National%20Seashore",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/asis/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Seashore/Lakeshore"},{id:"baltimore-washington-parkway",name:"Baltimore-Washington Parkway",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7F83E6-1DD8-B71B-0BD4AB5763204C1E.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The Baltimore-Washington Parkway is a scenic roadway administered by the National Park Service, connecting Baltimore and Washington D.C. It offers a historic, green corridor through developed areas, preserving viewsheds and providing access to recreational sites.",fullDescription:"Designed as a ceremonial gateway, the Baltimore-Washington Parkway offers a scenic, limited-access route constructed in the mid-20th century. It provides a vital transportation link between the two major cities while maintaining a park-like setting, featuring forested buffers and minimizing commercial development. The parkway gives travelers a glimpse of a protected landscape, providing access to other NPS sites like Greenbelt Park, and serves as an important component of the nation's capital park system.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Baltimore-Washington Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 344-3400"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Maryland and District of columbia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Baltimore-Washington%20Parkway",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/bawa/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"george-washington-memorial-parkway-in-1989-the-maryland-and-dc-portions-of-the-parkway-were-renamed-clara-barton-parkway-to-overcome-motorist-confusion-claude-moore-colonial-farmglen-echo-parkgreat-falls-parktheodore-roosevelt-island-national-memorial",name:"George Washington Memorial Parkway (In 1989, the Maryland and DC portions of the parkway were renamed Clara Barton Parkway to overcome motorist confusion).Claude Moore Colonial FarmGlen Echo ParkGreat Falls ParkTheodore Roosevelt Island National Memorial",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C645D155-EDA3-ED3B-6DE6F6A95D3A8A6F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C828BAC-1DD8-B71B-0B1A9857AB2A50E1.jpg"],shortDescription:"The George Washington Memorial Parkway is a scenic roadway along the Potomac River, commemorating George Washington and linking many historic, natural, and recreational sites near the nation's capital, including the Clara Barton Parkway section in Maryland.",fullDescription:"This memorial parkway offers motorists a picturesque drive through a landscape rich in history and nature, connecting various national park sites and historic locations like Mount Vernon. It provides panoramic views of the Potomac River and access to diverse recreational opportunities such as biking, hiking, and wildlife viewing. The parkway includes the Clara Barton Parkway in Maryland, known for its scenic riverine views, and serves as a gateway to cultural gems like Glen Echo Park and the natural beauty of Great Falls Park, all contributing to the greater Washington D.C. metropolitan area's green infrastructure.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"George Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(703) 289-2500"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Parkway headquarters is open Monday through Friday from 8:15 am to 4:15 pm. It is closed on weekends and holidays.",address:"Maryland and Virginia and District of columbia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=George%20Washington%20Memorial%20Parkway%20(In%201989%2C%20the%20Maryland%20and%20DC%20portions%20of%20the%20parkway%20were%20renamed%20Clara%20Barton%20Parkway%20to%20overcome%20motorist%20confusion).Claude%20Moore%20Colonial%20FarmGlen%20Echo%20ParkGreat%20Falls%20ParkTheodore%20Roosevelt%20Island%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gwmp/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"suitland-parkway",name:"Suitland Parkway",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82A965-1DD8-B71B-0B42F2CD698E11A7.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The Suitland Parkway is a historic National Park Service roadway that provides a scenic, tree-lined corridor connecting military installations and residential communities in Prince George's County, Maryland, to Washington, D.C.",fullDescription:"Constructed during World War II, the Suitland Parkway was designed to facilitate rapid movement between military bases like Andrews Field (now Joint Base Andrews) and the nation's capital. Administered by the National Park Service as part of Anacostia Park, it remains a vital artery, preserving a naturalistic setting amidst urban development. The parkway offers a unique blend of historical infrastructure and green space, providing commuters a tranquil journey and reminding visitors of its wartime origins and ongoing ecological role within the region.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Suitland Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(202) 692-6010"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open: 6:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Suitland%20Parkway",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/anac/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"captain-john-smith-chesapeake-national-historic-trail",name:"Captain John Smith Chesapeake National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C823C7D-1DD8-B71B-0B716A0012D6C0E3.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C822CB2-1DD8-B71B-0BF6D067DAF20F8C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C823C7D-1DD8-B71B-0B716A0012D6C0E3.jpg"],shortDescription:"This is the nation's first water trail, tracing Captain John Smith's 17th-century voyages of exploration and discovery across the Chesapeake Bay and its tributaries, illuminating Indigenous cultures, colonial history, and the bay's natural heritage.",fullDescription:"The Captain John Smith Chesapeake National Historic Trail commemorates Smith's explorations from 1607 to 1609, covering over 3,000 miles of waterways that cross six states. It offers unique opportunities for boating, kayaking, and hiking along designated segments, allowing visitors to experience the landscapes and waterscapes that shaped early American history. The trail connects numerous cultural sites and natural areas, fostering an understanding of the Chesapeake Bay's rich ecological diversity and the profound impact of Native American life and European settlement on the region.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:["Captain Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Captain Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(410) 260-2442"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Delaware and Maryland and Virginia and District of columbia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Captain%20John%20Smith%20Chesapeake%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cajo/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"potomac-heritage-national-scenic-trail-official-unit",name:"Potomac Heritage National Scenic Trail (official unit)",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/57DDD525-C26A-90FA-5D5DC9A304EE0246.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/580BEC61-A9CE-EDA1-98EFFD3B1E833A73.jpg"],shortDescription:"The Potomac Heritage National Scenic Trail is a network of trails celebrating the cultural and natural heritage of the Potomac River basin, offering diverse recreational opportunities across multiple states.",fullDescription:"This expansive trail system encompasses a variety of pathways for hiking, biking, and paddling, connecting historical sites, natural areas, and urban green spaces from the Allegheny Mountains to the Chesapeake Bay. It highlights George Washington's vision for westward expansion and the region's rich history, including canal systems and early settlements. The trail encourages exploration of diverse ecosystems and provides a continuous corridor for recreation and environmental stewardship, allowing visitors to experience the beauty and significance of the Potomac watershed.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Explor