onal-historic-trail",name:"Star-Spangled Banner National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9CF39797-F3D3-B3FD-5342563C148406B2.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F12D5A96-94D4-C33A-0DA8312DDC17F436.jpg"],shortDescription:`This 560-mile land and water trail traces the routes of the War of 1812's Chesapeake Campaign, commemorating events that inspired Francis Scott Key to write "The Star-Spangled Banner."`,fullDescription:"The Star-Spangled Banner National Historic Trail follows the paths of British and American forces during the pivotal War of 1812, connecting historic battlefields, museums, and waterfront communities across Maryland, Virginia, and Washington D.C. Visitors can explore sites like Fort McHenry, North Point, and Bladensburg, learning about the dramatic events that led to the birth of the national anthem. The trail offers interpretive centers, scenic driving tours, and water-based activities, immersing guests in a defining period of American history and patriotism.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Star-Spangled Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(410) 962-4290"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"