Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"8-Mile Loop Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"4-6 Hours",description:"Covering the majority of the sites on the east side of the park, the 8-Mile Loop Trail provides hikers with a good challenge and rewards them with all of the best scenic points along the mountain ridge."},{name:"Hike to Thurmont Vista: The Pink Blazed Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Thurmont Vista Overlook, the highlight of the Pink Trail, reaches a maximum elevation of 1,499 ft. A moderate hike, traversing the mountainside, this trail provides hikers an opportunity to experience the second growth f"},{name:"Hike to the Falls and Hog Rock: The Yellow Blazed Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"This loop is a moderate hike with uphill and downhill sections of the trail that include moderate to steep inclines. This trail is the longest loop (without making any variations) and features Maryland’s largest cascadin"}],scenicDrives:["Catoctin Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Catoctin Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 663-9388"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hiking trails and parking areas are open year round, dawn to dusk, but may close as a result of security or safety concerns. Consult the park's website for updates: http://www.nps.gov/cato/index.htm.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Catoctin%20Mountain%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cato/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"fort-washington-park",name:"Fort Washington Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81C3C3-1DD8-B71B-0B04CE7B7C61FE25.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves a monumental 19th-century brick fort overlooking the Potomac River, which once defended the nation's capital.",fullDescription:"Fort Washington Park preserves the only stone and masonry fort built to protect the City of Washington from naval assault. Completed in 1824 and later expanded with massive concrete batteries in the 1890s, the fort stands high on a cliff overlooking the Potomac River. Visitors can explore the historic officers' quarters, dark brick tunnels, gunpowder magazines, and dry moats. The park's expansive grounds also offer beautiful scenic river vistas, peaceful picnic areas, and bird-watching spots, alongside historic military reenactments and ranger-guided walking tours.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(771) 208-1555"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open daily, 6:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Washington%20Park",officialWebsi