e:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"National Capital Parks — East is an administrative unit that manages multiple park sites throughout the District of Columbia and Maryland. The headquarters building is located in Anacostia Park.",address:"Maryland and District of columbia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=National%20Capital%20Parks-EastAnacostia%20ParkBaltimore-Washington%20ParkwayCapitol%20Hill%20ParksCarter%20G.%20Woodson%20Home%20National%20Historic%20Site%20(also%20an%20official%20unit)Fort%20Dupont%20ParkFort%20Foote%20ParkFort%20Washington%20Park%20(also%20an%20official%20unit)Frederick%20Douglass%20National%20Historic%20Site%20(also%20an%20official%20unit)Greenbelt%20Park%20(also%20an%20official%20unit)Harmony%20Hall%20(Fort%20Washington%2C%20Maryland)Kenilworth%20Park%20and%20Aquatic%20GardensMary%20McLeod%20Bethune%20Council%20House%20National%20Historic%20Site%20(also%20an%20official%20unit)Oxon%20Cove%20Park%20and%20Oxon%20Hill%20FarmOxon%20Run%20ParkwayPiscataway%20Park%20(also%20an%20official%20unit)Suitland%20Parkway",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/nace/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"piscataway-park",name:"Piscataway Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E0595-1DD8-B71B-0BE07FAAF6BD61E8.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Protects the scenic historic views of Mount Vernon across the Potomac River, preserving pristine wetlands and native heritage.",fullDescription:"Piscataway Park was established to protect and preserve the historic, unobstructed view of the Potomac River from George Washington's Mount Vernon estate. Named after the Piscataway Nation, the park protects sacred native lands, pristine tidal wetlands, forests, and fields. It is home to bald eagles, beavers, and river otters, and features the Accokeek Foundation's demonstration farm, which showcases colonial-era agriculture. Visitors can explore scenic nature trails, fish from the public pier, and learn about the deep history of human habitation along the river.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Piscataway Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(301) 763-4600"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Piscataway Park, including Accokeek Creek Boardwalk, Farmington Landing, and Marshall Hall, is open daily for pedestrian access from 6:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m., and for vehicle access through gated areas from 9:00 a.m.",address:"Maryland, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Piscataway%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/pisc/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"appalachian-national-scenic-trail",name:"Appalachian National Scenic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg"],shortDescription:"The Appalachian Trail is a 2,190+ mile long public footpath that traverses the scenic, wooded, pastoral, wild, and culturally resonant lands of the Appalachian Mountains. Conceived in 1921, built by private citizens, and completed in 1937, today the trail is managed by the National Park Service, US Forest Service, Appalachian Trail Conservancy, numerous state agencies and thousands of volunteers.",fullDescription:"Appalachian National Scenic Trail is an official National Park Service site. The Appalachian Trail is a 2,190+ mile long public footpath that traverses the scenic, wooded, pastoral, wild, and culturally resonant lands of the Appalachian Mountains. Conceived in 1921, built by private citizens, and completed in 1937, today the trail is managed by the National Park Service, US Forest Service, Appalachian Trail Conservancy, numerous state agencies and thousands of volunteers. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Appalachian Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Appalachian National Scenic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(304) 535-6278"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in des