ational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The grounds of Minute Man National Historical Park are open daily, sunrise to sunset. Parking lot gates close at sunset.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Minute%20Man%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mima/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"new-bedford-whaling-national-historical-park",name:"New Bedford Whaling National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B58A6-1DD8-B71B-0BECE805AD9FBFDA.png",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic cobblestone streets, counting houses, and wharves of the world's 19th-century whaling capital.",fullDescription:"New Bedford Whaling National Historical Park encompasses a thirteen-block historic district in New Bedford, Massachusetts, which was once the premier whaling port in the world. The park preserves historic structures like the Seamen's Bethel, made famous in Herman Melville's Moby-Dick, the Custom House, and various merchant counting houses. Visitors can explore the rich history of the global whaling industry, learn about the city's role in the Underground Railroad, and discover the diverse communities that built this vibrant maritime hub.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"New Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(508) 996-4095"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Welcome to New Bedford Whaling National Historical Park! The Visitor Center and Garden provide a distinctive urban park experience.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=New%20Bedford%20Whaling%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/nebe/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"salem-maritime-national-historical-park",name:"Salem Maritime National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C852B17-1DD8-B71B-0BB86A151B7BE502.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/81E50923-C71A-94BD-7BAFD64C112DB4CE.jpg"],shortDescription:"The first National Historic Site, preserving Salem's historic waterfront, wharves, and early global maritime trade legacy.",fullDescription:"Salem Maritime National Historical Park preserves nine acres of historic waterfront property in Salem, Massachusetts, highlighting the city's golden age of international trade following the Revolutionary War. The park features historic wharves, the 1797 Custom House where Nathaniel Hawthorne worked, early merchant homes, and the Friendship of Salem, a replica three-masted East Indiaman. Visitors can walk the historic piers, explore grand Federalist-era buildings, and learn how Salem merchants opened trade routes to China, India, and Sumatra.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Salem Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(978) 740-1650"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Salem%20Maritime%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sama/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"boston-african-american-national-historic-site",name:"Boston African American National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/1970adec8e51a4e7.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Showcases historic sites on Beacon Hill linked to Boston's pre-Civil War free African American community and the abolitionist movement.",fullDescription:"Boston African American National Historic Site preserves fifteen pre-Civil War structures along the Black Heritage Trail on Beacon Hill. This historic neighborhood was home to one of the nation's most active free Black communities, who organized against slavery and fought for civil rights. Landmarks include the African Meeting House—the oldest surviving Black church building in the country—and the Abiel Smith School. Visitors can take guided ranger tours to learn about the courageous activists, educators, and Underground Railroad conductors who led the fight for equality.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Boston Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(617) 429-6760"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Boston African American National Historic Site rangers offer tours of the Black Heritage Trail seasonally. Please check the park calendar for available tours and programs.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Boston%20African%20American%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/boaf/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"frederick-law-olmsted-national-historic-site",name:"Frederick Law Olmsted National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C853BE9-1DD8-B71B-0B625B6B8B89F1A0.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2E0A7AF3-D77F-4B8E-E0A21BFD62266936.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F63AC0EF-A178-9C58-85C2F693D9A26848.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F65A8537-B59F-AA0C-C22A85A9C2ECCDE5.jpg"],shortDescription:`Preserves "Fairsted", the home and design office of America's premier landscape architect, Frederick Law Olmsted.`,fullDescription:`Frederick Law Olmsted National Historic Site preserves the suburban estate and professional office of the man who designed New York's Central Park, Boston's Emerald Necklace, and the US Capitol grounds. At "Fairsted" in Brookline, Massachusetts, Olmsted established the world's first full-scale professional landscape design firm. Visitors can tour the historic home, explore the drafting rooms containing thousands of plans, view the restored historic grounds, and learn how Olmsted's vision shaped American cities, public parks, and conservation efforts.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Frederick Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(617) 566-1689"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hours for the visitor center and house at Frederick Law Olmsted National Historic Site vary by season. The grounds are open year-round, dawn to dusk.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Frederick%20Law%20Olmsted%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/frla/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"john-fitzgerald-kennedy-national-historic-site",name:"John Fitzgerald Kennedy National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C820F63-1DD8-B71B-0BA806C2D92161BF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4AA5B49F-ECA2-C60D-69B4B0B6D59BAA5D.jpg"],shortDescription:"The birthplace and boyhood home of the 35th U.S. President, John F. Kennedy, in Brookline, Massachusetts.",fullDescription:"John Fitzgerald Kennedy National Historic Site preserves the modest Brookline home where John F. Kennedy was born in 1917. Restored by his mother, Rose Fitzgerald Kennedy, the house is furnished with original family belongings, books, and baby furniture. Through ranger-led tours, visitors can gain an intimate look into the early years of the future President, discovering how his family's values, education, and early experiences shaped his character, leadership style, and call to public service.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"John Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(617) 566-7937"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hours for the visitor center and house at John Fitzgerald Kennedy National Historic Site vary by season. The grounds are open year-round, dawn to dusk.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=John%20Fitzgerald%20Kennedy%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/jofi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"longfellow-house-washington-s-headquarters-national-historic-site",name:"Longfellow House–Washington's Headquarters National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3F81541A-CC01-7548-8F7723937ECD58D8.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6962D3EF-9034-D761-5B719247D52A9DDA.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the home of famed 19th-century poet Henry Wadsworth Longfellow and George Washington's Revolutionary War headquarters.",fullDescription:"Longfellow House–Washington's Headquarters National Historic Site preserves the historic Cambridge mansion that served as George Washington's primary headquarters during the Siege of Boston (1775-1776). Decades later, it became the home of beloved American poet Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, who penned many of his famous works here. The site boasts an extraordinary collection of original furnishings, artwork, and Longfellow's personal library, as well as beautifully landscaped gardens, representing a rich intersection of American literary and military history.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Longfellow Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(617) 876-4491"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hours for the visitor center and house at Longfellow House-Washington's Headquarters National Historic Site vary by season. The grounds are open year-round, dawn to dusk.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Longfellow%20House%E2%80%93Washington's%20Headquarters%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/long/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"saugus-iron-works-national-historic-site",name:"Saugus Iron Works National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C852E9B-1DD8-B71B-0BA7396E0D076BDB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/838FD1C1-064E-FCB5-82DE23B12CF51362.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/83A5CA79-099C-220B-DD37490775CD89C4.jpg"],shortDescription:"Reconstructs the first integrated ironworks in North America, highlighting 17th-century heavy industry and craftsmanship.",fullDescription:"Saugus Iron Works National Historic Site is a reconstructed 1640s industrial community situated on the Saugus River. It stands as the birthplace of the American iron and steel industry, showcasing the complex technology of giant waterwheels, blast furnaces, a forge with a massive trip hammer, and a rolling mill. Visitors can explore working reconstructions, view archaeological artifacts, walk along the nature trail, and learn about the lives of the early European ironworkers and African laborers who pioneered North American metallurgy.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Saugus Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(781) 233-0050"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Saugus%20Iron%20Works%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sair/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"springfield-armory-national-historic-site",name:"Springfield Armory National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/D351A3D5-1DD8-B71B-0B9E169EA16305E9.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/EE8C00C2-BDE4-9CC3-2686707A304148B6.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/EF74A0C2-E271-6041-2D2A097B9A5D86A4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/EF9A4AF9-EFC3-BF6E-7224498221C4EB81.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic military arsenal commissioned by George Washington, featuring the world's largest collection of historic firearms.",fullDescription:"Springfield Armory National Historic Site protects the historic arsenal and buildings that served as the primary center for US military firearm manufacturing from 1794 to 1968. Commanded by General George Washington during the Revolution, the Armory drove industrial innovation, perfecting interchangeable parts and mass production. Today, the museum houses a vast and unique collection of historic military weapons, showcasing the evolution of defense technology and the stories of the skilled workers who armed the nation's military throughout its history.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Springfield Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(413) 734-8551"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:`The Park MUSEUM & VISITOR CENTER
