oint, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(617) 819-3587"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Boston%20Harbor%20Islands%20National%20Recreation%20Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/boha/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"cape-cod-national-seashore",name:"Cape Cod National Seashore",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e714520da2ab1930.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Protects 40,000 acres of pristine sandy beaches, pine barrens, marshes, lighthouses, and cranberry bogs on Cape Cod.",fullDescription:"Cape Cod National Seashore preserves forty thousand acres of beautiful marine environments, sandy beaches, historic lighthouses, and diverse ecosystems on the outer peninsula of Cape Cod. Established by President John F. Kennedy, the park features spectacular hiking trails, pristine swimming beaches, historic lifesaving stations, and dense cedar swamps. Visitors can swim in the Atlantic, bike through coastal pine forests, explore historic sea captain homes, and experience the timeless beauty of the Massachusetts shoreline.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Atlantic White Cedar Swamp Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Find your trail to explore at Cape Cod National Seashore! One of eleven trails in the park is the Atlantic White Cedar Swamp, a 1.2-mile loop trail in Wellfleet, Massachusetts."},{name:"Hike the Pilgrim Spring Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Find your trail to explore at Cape Cod National Seashore! One of eleven trails in the park is the Pilgrim Spring Trail, a 1-mile loop trail in Truro, Massachusetts."},{name:"Hike the Doane Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Find your trail to explore at Cape Cod National Seashore! One of eleven trails in the park is the Doane Trail, a 0.6-mile loop trail that begins at Doane Rock in Eastham."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cape Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(508) 255-3421"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Beaches, parking areas, and trails are open year-round between 6 am and midnight.",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cape%20Cod%20National%20Seashore",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/caco/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Seashore/Lakeshore"},{id:"new-england-national-scenic-trail-official-unit",name:"New England National Scenic Trail (official unit)",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/62EEBADD-1DD8-B71B-0B610F999ED4F0F5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/62EEBADD-1DD8-B71B-0B610F999ED4F0F5.jpg"],shortDescription:"A 215-mile scenic trail corridor crossing Massachusetts and Connecticut, showcasing dramatic trap rock ridges and beautiful valley vistas.",fullDescription:"The New England National Scenic Trail is a 215-mile hiking trail that traverses Connecticut and Massachusetts. It follows a continuous path along the Metacomet-Monadnock-Mattabesett trail system, leading hikers through diverse landscapes including dramatic basalt trap rock ridges, quiet woodlands, farmlands, and historical communities. The trail offers unparalleled opportunities for long-distance backpacking and day hiking, with stunning panoramic vistas overlooking the Connecticut River Valley and preserving crucial open space corridors in southern New England.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"New Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(413) 253-3771"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"As the New England trail crosses private property and state land, many locations along the trail do not permit night hiking or overnight parking. Before going out on a hike, check the hours of local parks for parking and hiking regulations.",address:"Connecticut and Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=New%20England%20National%20Scenic%20Trail%20(official%20unit)",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/neen/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"washington-rochambeau-national-historic-trail",name:"Washington-Rochambeau National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3049B5BE-921E-7B15-7E1E4D6BD21442AB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/31648791-F4D9-4CA7-3CD80631C81422B7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Follows the historic 680-mile land route taken by the allied French and American armies to victory at Yorktown in 1781.",fullDescription:"The Washington-Rochambeau National Historic Trail commemorates the historic march of George Washington's American forces and Jean-Baptiste de Rochambeau's French army during the American Revolutionary War. Stretching 680 miles through nine states—including Massachusetts—the trail marks the allied path that led to the decisive siege and victory at Yorktown, Virginia. Today, visitors can explore historic taverns, campsites, and roads, discovering the incredible logistics, cooperation, and international alliance that secured American independence.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"},{name:"Become a B.A.R.K. Ranger!",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Become a B.A.R.K and P.U.R.R.K Ranger at Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Routes National Historic Trai."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Washington-Rochambeau Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(215) 597-7013"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Massachusetts and Virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Washington-Rochambeau%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/waro/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"appalachian-national-scenic-trail",name:"Appalachian National Scenic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg"],shortDescription:"The Appalachian Trail is a 2,190+ mile long public footpath that traverses the scenic, wooded, pastoral, wild, and culturally resonant lands of the Appalachian Mountains. Conceived in 1921, built by private citizens, and completed in 1937, today the trail is managed by the National Park Service, US Forest Service, Appalachian Trail Conservancy, numerous state agencies and thousands of volunteers.",fullDescription:"Appalachian National Scenic Trail is an official National Park Service site. The Appalachian Trail is a 2,190+ mile long public footpath that traverses the scenic, wooded, pastoral, wild, and culturally resonant lands of the Appalachian Mountains. Conceived in 1921, built by private citizens, and completed in 1937, today the trail is managed by the National Park Service, US Forest Service, Appalachian Trail Conservancy, numerous state agencies and thousands of volunteers. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Appalachian Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Appalachian National Scenic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(304) 535-6278"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Massachusetts, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Appalachian%20National%20Scenic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/appa/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"washington-rochambeau-revolutionary-route-national-historic-trail",name:"Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3049B5BE-921E-7B15-7E1E4D6BD21442AB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/31648791-F4D9-4CA7-3CD80631C81422B7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Follows the historic 680-mile land route taken by the allied French and American armies to victory at Yorktown in 1781.",fullDescription:"The Washington-Rochambeau National Historic Trail commemorates the historic march of George Washington's American forces and Jean-Baptiste de Rochambeau's French army during the American Revolutionary War. Stretching 680 miles through nine states—including Massachusetts—the trail marks the allied path that led to the decisive siege and victory at Yorktown, Virginia. Today, visitors can explore historic taverns, campsites, and roads, discovering the incredible logistics, cooperation, and international alliance that secured American independence.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"},{name:"Become a B.A.R.K. Ranger!",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Become a B.A.R.K and P.U.R.R.K Ranger at Was