.",fullDescription:"Accessible only by boat or seaplane, Isle Royale National Park protects a wild archipelago where nature's processes unfold with minimal human intervention. Its unique ecosystem is famous for the longest-running study of wolves and moose, providing critical insights into predator-prey dynamics. Visitors can explore its pristine forests, clear waters, and historic lighthouses, experiencing a truly immersive wilderness adventure amidst the vastness of Lake Superior.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Isle Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(906) 482-0984"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $7 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Michigan, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Isle%20Royale%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/isro/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"keweenaw-national-historical-park",name:"Keweenaw National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D1933-1DD8-B71B-0BDEE535F0C69BBC.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D1ED3-1DD8-B71B-0BCC48DDBC3BB82E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D20D1-1DD8-B71B-0B292351051EE295.jpg"],shortDescription:"Keweenaw National Historical Park preserves and interprets the story of copper mining on Michigan's Keweenaw Peninsula, a critical chapter in America's industrial history.",fullDescription:"This park commemorates the rise and decline of copper mining on the Keweenaw Peninsula, which became America's first mineral rush and a global leader in copper production during the 19th century. Through numerous heritage sites and cooperating partners, it showcases the immense challenges faced by immigrant miners, the innovative technologies developed, and the lasting impact of this industry on the landscape and culture of Michigan's Upper Peninsula. Visitors can explore historic mining towns, delve into underground mines, and learn about the diverse communities that built this industrial empire.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking into History",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Many of the Park's Heritage sites offer hiking trails."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Keweenaw Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(906) 337-3168"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Calumet Unit of Keweenaw NHP encompasses the Village of Calumet and part of Calumet Township. Park grounds (with the exception of private property and the Keweenaw Heritage Sites) are open to the public from sunrise to sunset.",address:"Michigan, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Keweenaw%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kewe/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"river-raisin-national-battlefield-park",name:"River Raisin National Battlefield Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3aa6f76ec31140fc.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:'River Raisin National Battlefield Park commemorates the battles of January 1813 during the War of 1812, which led to the rallying cry "Remember the Raisin!" and inspired Kentucky volunteers.',fullDescription:"This battlefield park preserves the site of a devastating defeat for American forces and Kentucky militiamen during the War of 1812, followed by a massacre of American prisoners by Native American allies of the British. The events here fueled a desire for revenge and significantly influenced the course of the war in the Old Northwest. Visitors can explore the interpretive center, walk the grounds where these pivotal battles occurred, and learn about the complex interactions between American, British, and Native American forces, understanding its profound impact on early American identity and military strategy.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Enjoy the River Raisin Heritage Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The River Raisin Heritage Trail The Heritage Trail has eight miles of paved hike-bike trails that provide safe and scenic routes to walk, run, cycle and in-line skate. The trail connects major historic sites, state and l"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Rive