er and trails provide insights into his expeditions, his interactions with Native American tribes, and the early history of European presence in the upper Midwest.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Father Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(906) 643-8620"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Michigan, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Father%20Marquette%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fama/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Memorial"},{id:"pictured-rocks-national-lakeshore",name:"Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B502655F-03A4-E3C2-33CEA40037E6DC09.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6714EE96-A13B-894C-6B03888F3D6641DD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6750A84D-08CD-D3C5-54DF836676E4E5FE.jpg"],shortDescription:"Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore, America's first national lakeshore, protects dramatic multicolored sandstone cliffs, pristine beaches, and dense forests along Lake Superior's rugged south shore.",fullDescription:"Stretching for over 40 miles along the southern shore of Lake Superior, Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore is renowned for its breathtaking natural beauty. Its most iconic features are the towering sandstone cliffs, sculpted by wind and waves into formations like Miner's Castle and Chapel Rock, adorned with mineral stains. Beyond the cliffs, visitors can explore vibrant sand dunes, pristine beaches, waterfalls, and dense maple-beech forests, offering abundant opportunities for kayaking, hiking, and enjoying the wild majesty of the Upper Peninsula.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Walks and Hikes Over a Mile",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1-5 Hours",description:"Longer hikes and walks lead to stunning cliff views, remote beaches, the Beaver Basin Wilderness, Au Sable Lighthouse, and many other scenic areas."},{name:"Walks and Hikes of One Mile or Less",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Pictured Rocks offers 100 miles of hiking and walking trails leading to waterfalls, beaches, forest wilderness, cultural sites, and breathtaking cliff-top views of Lake Superior. Here are some of the shorter trails."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Pictured Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(906) 387-3700"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Michigan, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pictured%20Rocks%20National%20Lakeshore",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/piro/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Seashore/Lakeshore"},{id:"sleeping-bear-dunes-national-lakeshore",name:"Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FE0D1CEF-9466-CDC6-A6A653182170C3EC.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FDFEAB66-BE14-5198-2192383739CEEA99.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FE0D1CEF-9466-CDC6-A6A653182170C3EC.jpg"],shortDescription:"Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore is celebrated for its immense freshwater sand dunes, perched high above Lake Michigan, offering spectacular views and unique ecological habitats.",fullDescription:"Located along the northwest coast of Michigan's Lower Peninsula, Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore encompasses towering bluffs, miles of sandy beaches, clear inland lakes, and ancient forests. The iconic Dune Climb allows visitors to experience the vastness of these natural formations, which are among the largest freshwater dunes in the world. The park also preserves historic farmsteads, lighthouses, and two rugged offshore islands, providing diverse recreational opportunities from hiking and biking to swimming and exploring maritime history.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Sleeping Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(231) 326-4700"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Range