 and local outdoor enthusiasts alike.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"North Country Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for North Country National Scenic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(616) 330-2456"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Michigan, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=North%20Country%20National%20Scenic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/noco/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],minnesota:[{id:"voyageurs-national-park",name:"Voyageurs National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/325E7D4A-919F-7E79-851567904D618FBF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/326AE66F-9AD6-4E13-B9F5362F632938E3.jpg"],shortDescription:"A maze of interconnected water routes, pristine lakes, and majestic forests in northern Minnesota near the Canadian border.",fullDescription:"Located near International Falls, Minnesota, Voyageurs National Park is a unique water-based wilderness of pristine lakes, interconnected waterways, and scenic boreal forests along the Canadian border. The park preserves a historic trade route used by 18th-century French-Canadian fur traders known as voyageurs. Accessible primarily by boat, visitors flock to Voyageurs for motorized boating, canoeing, kayaking, houseboat vacations, and world-class sportfishing. The park's dark skies make it an exceptional location for stargazing and viewing the northern lights.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike Blind Ash Bay Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"This narrow, winding, rocky trail exposes users to the wonders of the boreal forest. This path is great for birding, it is also frequented by deer and other wildlife. From closed in forest to post card worthy views of Ka"},{name:"Hike the Oberholtzer Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"45-80 Minutes",description:"Wander through deciduous and conifer forest as the trail twists around the edge of a cattail filled wetland and past rocks deposited by ancient glaciers. Look for signs of wildlife as you hike to two different overlooks "},{name:"Hike Echo Bay Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"40-120 Minutes",description:"This wide trail showcases the transition between deciduous and boreal forests as you pass through lowlands and rocky outcrops. With views of wetlands created by the engineering genius of the North American Beaver, and de"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Voyageurs Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(218) 283-6600"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Minnesota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Voyageurs%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/voya",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"grand-portage-national-monument",name:"Grand Portage National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7BB4A2E5-EF50-21A2-E590D580DE06718A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7C8B5E-1DD8-B71B-0BA20EF8040D873E.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves a historic 8.5-mile Ojibwe portage trail and reconstructed North West Company fur trading depot on the shores of Lake Superior.",fullDescription:"Grand Portage National Monument, located in northeastern Minnesota on the Grand Portage Ojibwe Reservation, preserves a crucial 18th-century fur trading outpost and a historic 8.5-mile portage trail. This trail bypassed the rapids of the Pigeon River to connect Lake Superior with inland waterways. The site features a reconstructed North West Company Great Hall, canoe warehouse, and Ojibwe village exhibits. Visitors can experience live history demonstrations, explore Voyageur and Ojibwe cultures, and hike the historic portage path.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike Grand Portage Trails",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Hike the Mount Rose Trail for stunning views of Grand Portage Bay and the Depot. To experience the trek the Voyageurs made as well as Indigenous people before them, hike a portion or all of Gitchi Onagamiing, the Grand P"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Grand Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(218) 475-0123"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Anishinaabe Oodena (Ojibwe Village), the grounds outside of the palisade, and the Mount Rose and Grand Portage trails are open daily year-round.",address:"Minnesota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Grand%20Portage%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/grpo",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"pipestone-national-monument",name:"Pipestone National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/DA3FB8BE-E6ED-14AC-0D31ABF5E44A43F8.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"An active cultural site where Native Americans have quarried red pipestone for prayer pipes for thousands of years.",fullDescription:"Pipestone National Monument in southwestern Minnesota preserves the historic quarries where Native Americans have extracted soft red siltstone (catlinite) to carve sacred prayer pipes for over 3,000 years. Regarded as sacred ground, the quarries remain active today, restricted exclusively to enrolled members of federally recognized tribes. The park features scenic walking trails along Winnewissa Falls, quarry pits, and a visitor center with live pipe-carving demonstrations that share the spiritual and cultural heritage of Plains tribal nations.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Pipestone Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(507) 825-5464"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Minnesota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pipestone%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/pipe",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"mississippi-national-river-and-recreation-area",name:"Mississippi National River and Recreation Area*",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D729A-1DD8-B71B-0B1F6B293E7BF9E8.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7CE12B-1DD8-B71B-0B33804962A6982D.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D7738-1DD8-B71B-0BD2B0BB8C3F591F.jpg"],shortDescription:"A 72-mile river corridor running through the Minneapolis-St. Paul metro area, preserving cultural, historic, and natural wonders of the Mississippi River.",fullDescription:"The Mississippi National River and Recreation Area is a 72-mile corridor protecting the diverse ecological, historic, and recreational resources of the Mississippi River through the Minneapolis-St. Paul metropolitan area. Rather than a single continuous park, it is a partnership park spanning multiple parks, islands, trails, and historic sites. Visitors can enjoy kayaking, fishing, hiking, and birdwatching, as well as explore historic sites like the Mill City Museum, St. Anthony Falls, and Fort Snelling.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming","Scenic Drives","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Go on a Day Hike at Coldwater Spring",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Enjoy a nice day hike and many opportunities for wildlife viewing, photography, and reflection at Coldwater Spring! Trails surround a restored prairie oak savannah and wetlands in this culturally and historically signifi"}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mississippi Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(651) 293-0200"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters","Mule Deer","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Minnesota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mississippi%20National%20River%20and%20Recreat