 Mamie Till-Mobley National Monument, established in 2023, spans sites in Mississippi and Chicago, Illinois, that are crucial to the story of Emmett Till's lynching and the subsequent civil rights movement. In Mississippi, the monument preserves the Graball Landing site on the Tallahatchie River, where Emmett's body was recovered, and the Tallahatchie County Courthouse in Sumner, where his murderers were tried and acquitted by an all-white jury. It honors Emmett's memory and his mother Mamie's historic determination to show the world the brutal reality of racial violence.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Emmett Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(601) 630-0476"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Illinois and Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Emmett%20Till%20and%20Mamie%20Till-Mobley%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/elta",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"medgar-and-myrlie-evers-home-national-monument",name:"Medgar and Myrlie Evers Home National Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/031b8c50906d0046.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the Jackson home of iconic civil rights leaders Medgar and Myrlie Evers, where Medgar was tragically assassinated in 1963.",fullDescription:"The Medgar and Myrlie Evers Home National Monument in Jackson, Mississippi, preserves the ranch-style house of civil rights activists Medgar and Myrlie Evers. Medgar Evers, the NAACP's first field secretary in Mississippi, led crucial voting rights, desegregation, and economic boycott campaigns. He was assassinated in the home's driveway on June 12, 1963, a tragedy that catalyzed the civil rights movement. The monument honors their heroic leadership and the struggles of the modern civil rights movement in Mississippi.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Medgar Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(601) 977-1600"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Our service hours are Tuesday-Saturday 9am-4pm, CLOSED from 12-1pm for lunch. Grounds of the home are open sunrise to sunset every day.",address:"Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Medgar%20and%20Myrlie%20Evers%20Home%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/memy/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"natchez-national-historical-park",name:"Natchez National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/BC2BF996-9A1D-637F-B279C39E2232C4C4.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves and interprets the history of Natchez, Mississippi, spanning European settlement, cotton plantations, slavery, and the Civil War.",fullDescription:"Natchez National Historical Park preserves the complex history of Natchez, Mississippi, a town of great wealth and deep racial disparity in the antebellum South. The park includes three main sites: Melrose Estate, a beautifully preserved 80-acre estate illustrating the lives of wealthy cotton planters and the enslaved people who made their lifestyle possible; the William Johnson House, home of a free Black barber and businessman who documented 19th-century life in his diaries; and Fort Rosalie, the site of an 18th-century French fortification along the Mississippi River.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Natchez Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(601) 446-5790"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Natchez Visitor Center is currently closed due to the installation of a new HVAC system. Melrose is open 7 days a week with guided tours of the mansion at 10:00, 11:00 am, 2:00, 3:00, and 4:00.",address:"Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Natchez%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/natc",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"shiloh-national-military-parkshiloh-national-cemetery",name:"Shiloh National Military ParkShiloh National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C17670A9-D87F-BF06-7D5D3B3C515AAFDF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C147943B-C7AC-2B13-E74CED9C388599FE.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the pivotal Battle of Shiloh fought in April 1862, one of the earliest and bloodiest major battles of the American Civil War.",fullDescription:"Shiloh National Military Park preserves the legendary battlefields of Shiloh and Corinth, which saw massive conflicts during the Civil War's western campaign in the spring of 1862. The Battle of Shiloh, fought on April 6–7, 1862, resulted in nearly 24,000 casualties and ended Confederate hopes of blocking Union advances into northern Mississippi. The park features a scenic 12.7-mile driving tour, historic log churches, Shiloh National Cemetery, and pristine Native American mounds overlooking the Tennessee River.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Shiloh Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(731) 689-5696"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds remain open daily from sunrise to sunset. The Shiloh Battlefield visitor center is open from 9:00 am - 5:00 pm daily.",address:"Mississippi and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Shiloh%20National%20Military%20ParkShiloh%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/shil",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"vicksburg-national-military-parkvicksburg-national-cemetery",name:"Vicksburg National Military ParkVicksburg National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79FEFF-1DD8-B71B-0B68A290E3DBE016.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79FEFF-1DD8-B71B-0B68A290E3DBE016.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the historic 47-day Civil War Siege of Vicksburg, which gave the Union control of the Mississippi River in July 1863.",fullDescription:"Vicksburg National Military Park preserves the site of the Siege of Vicksburg, a critical turning point of the American Civil War that culminated on July 4, 1863. By capturing Vicksburg, Union forces under General Ulysses S. Grant gained complete control of the Mississippi River, splitting the Confederacy in two. The park features over 1,300 monuments, the restored Union ironclad gunboat USS Cairo, scenic driving tours, historic earthen forts, and the Vicksburg National Cemetery, final resting place for thousands of Civil War soldiers.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Vicksburg Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(601) 636-0583"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The Visitor Center is open 8:30 am to 4:30 pm Wednesday-Sunday. Tour road is open from 8:30 am to 5 pm daily with last vehicle entry at 4:40 pm.",address:"Louisiana and Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Vicksburg%20National%20Military%20ParkVicksburg%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/vick",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"tupelo-national-battlefield",name:"Tupelo National Battlefield",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C844354-1DD8-B71B-0B1A7DB85F697FC5.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorates the Civil War Battle of Tupelo fought in July 1864, where Union forces successfully protected Sherman's supply lines.",fullDescription:"Tupelo National Battlefield is a one-acre park in Tupelo, Mississippi, that commemorates the Battle of Tupelo, fought on July 14–15, 1864. Union forces commanded by General Andrew J. Smith defeated Confederate forces led by General Nathan Bedford Forrest, successfully keeping Confederate troops from destroying the railroads supplying General William T. Sherman's march on Atlanta. The battlefield features a memorial monument, cannons, and interpretive plaques detailing the tactical significance of the battle.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Tupelo Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(662) 680-4027"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The grounds of Tupelo National Battlefield are open during daylight hours everyday of the year. The National Park Service administers the National Battlefield through the Natchez Trace Parkway.",address:"Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tupelo%20National%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/tupe",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"brices-cross-roads-national-battlefield-site",name:"Brices Cross Roads National Battlefield Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/695580FD-E79B-862E-2F5728A03FCC7970.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorates the Battle of Brices Cross Roads, a brilliant tactical Confederate victory fought in northern Mississippi on June 10, 1864.",fullDescription:"Brices Cross Roads National Battlefield Site preserves the scene of the Battle of Brices Cross Roads, fought on June 10, 1864. Confederate General Nathan Bedford Forrest led his cavalry to a spectacular victory over a much larger Union force commanded by General Samuel Sturgis. Despite the defeat, Union resistance successfully delayed Forrest's forces from attacking the vital supply lines feeding General William T. Sherman's Georgia campaign. The park features monuments, informational markers, and a historic cemetery.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Brices Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(662) 680-4027"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The grounds of the Brices Cross Roads National Battlefield Site are open sunrise to sunset everyday of the year.",address:"Mississippi, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Brices%20Cross%20Roads%20National%20Battlefield%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/brcr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"gulf-islands-national-seashore",name:"Gulf Islands National Seashore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/CECB6504-E46D-110F-AC4A39D32488193B.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Gulf Islands National Seashore is located primarily in Florida. Please refer to Florida for full information and details.",fullDescription:"This entry