and Green Tree Tavern to learn about colonial life, early agriculture, and the diverse cultural blend of French, Spanish, Native, and African peoples.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ste. Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(573) 880-7189"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Ste. Genevieve National Historical Park and the Ste.",address:"Missouri, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ste.%20Genevieve%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/stge",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"harry-s-truman-national-historic-site",name:"Harry S. Truman National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7c21eb9c5f9ea107.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the Independence and Grandview homes of Harry S. Truman, the 33rd President of the United States, showcasing his simple personal life.",fullDescription:"Harry S. Truman National Historic Site in Independence and Grandview, Missouri, preserves the homes and farm associated with the 33rd President of the United States. The primary home on Delaware Street in Independence served as the Trumans' residence from their marriage in 1919 until Harry's death in 1972, serving as the 'Summer White House' during his presidency. Left almost exactly as it was when the Trumans lived there, the site offers visitors an intimate, authentic look at the simple, down-to-earth personal life of a President who made some of the most critical decisions in modern history.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Harry Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(816) 254-9929"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Take a guided tour of the historic Truman Home. With thousands of artifacts on display, the home is an insightful glimpse into the personal lives of the Trumans.",address:"Missouri, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harry%20S.%20Truman%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hstr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"ulysses-s-grant-national-historic-site",name:"Ulysses S. Grant National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/63BEC04A-F6A2-7093-3BE80584418749FD.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C827B91-1DD8-B71B-0BA8AC33F2669F55.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C827360-1DD8-B71B-0BF9ADC1F4742F80.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C827E9B-1DD8-B71B-0B8A392673E74A10.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/63DF112F-E454-54F9-DC96A5DC6CF97FB4.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves 'White Haven,' the historic St. Louis home where Ulysses S. Grant lived and worked alongside his wife, Julia Dent Grant.",fullDescription:"The Ulysses S. Grant National Historic Site in St. Louis, Missouri, preserves 'White Haven,' the historic 19th-century farm of Julia Dent Grant's family. Ulysses S. Grant, the victorious Civil War Union general and 18th President of the United States, lived here with Julia in the 1850s, farming the land and working alongside enslaved laborers owned by his father-in-law. The park features the restored green-painted main house, outbuildings, and a modern museum, exploring Grant's military genius, his presidential legacy, and the complex realities of slavery on the estate.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ulysses Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(314) 842-1867"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Ulysses S. Grant National Historic Site’s hours of operation are 9AM – 5PM, with the last tour of the historic house at 4 pm.",address:"Missouri, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ulysses%20S.%20Grant%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/ulsg",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"wilson-s-creek-national-battlefield",name:"Wilson's Creek National Battlefield",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/g-wilsons-creek-ray-house.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the site of the Battle of Wilson's Creek, the first major Civil War conflict fought west of the Mississippi River in August 1861.",fullDescription:"Wilson's Creek National Battlefield near Springfield, Missouri, protects the site of the Battle of Wilson's Creek, fought on August 10, 1861. As the first major battle in the Civil War's western theater, it resulted in a Confederate victory and the death of Union General Nathaniel Lyon—the first Union general killed in the war. The park features a 5-mile self-guided driving tour, hiking and horseback trails, the historic Ray House (which served as a temporary hospital), and an outstanding Civil War museum.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Wilson's Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(417) 732-2662"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds are open from 1/2 hour before sunrise until 1/2 hour after sunset. This includes non-motorized and pedestrian access to the tour road.",address:"Missouri, USA",goo