ress captured the American imagination and played a vital role in keeping California united with the Union on the eve of the Civil War.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Walk on the Pony Express Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"There are a variety of walking and hiking opportunities along the Pony Express Trail. In some places, you may find a modern-day hiking trail. In other places, the historic trail has been replaced by an dirt or asphalt ro"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Pony Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"California and Missouri, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pony%20Express%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/poex/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"santa-fe-national-historic-trail",name:"Santa Fe National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/55A8E9A9-D7BB-3F66-8EE743A527AE5FED.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5591CACE-D5FE-92A5-77E2904F1E7F5AD9.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E737A-1DD8-B71B-0B11950104991FA3.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/55A8E9A9-D7BB-3F66-8EE743A527AE5FED.jpg"],shortDescription:"Traces the historic 1,200-mile international commercial highway that linked Missouri with Santa Fe, Mexico, from 1821 to 1880.",fullDescription:"The Santa Fe National Historic Trail marks the historic commercial highway that connected Missouri with Santa Fe, New Mexico (then part of Mexico, later a U.S. territory), between 1821 and 1880. Originating in Franklin and Independence, Missouri, the trail drove vital economic trade, cultural exchanges, and military operations across the Great Plains. Today, the trail links historic plazas, ruts, and pioneer grave sites, preserving the heritage of cross-cultural commerce and territorial expansion.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the Santa Fe Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:`Travel the trail today and you can almost hear the whoops and cries of "All's set!" as trail hands hitched their oxen to freight wagons carrying cargo between western Missouri and Santa Fe, New Mexico. Between 1821 and 1`}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Santa Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Missouri and New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Santa%20Fe%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https:/