:"15-60 Minutes",description:"The Clay Pit Pond is a half mile loop primitive trail with access at the Fort Clatsop Visitor Center picnic area. It is a great trail for those looking for a short but dynamic hike. The trail features a vernal pond surro"},{name:"Netul River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"20-60 Minutes",description:"Take an easy approximately 1 mile stroll along the beautiful Netul River Trail. Watch for water birds, eagles and sometimes otters! Along the way you can learn about the history of logging and commerce along the Lewis an"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis & Clark Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(402) 808-2714"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail Visitor Center is located on the Missouri River in Omaha, Nebraska.",address:"Missouri, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lewis%20%26%20Clark%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lecl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"ste-genevi-ve-national-historical-park",name:"Ste. Geneviève National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6FED42BD-930F-B92A-9CC652F2D045D60D.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5AF37243-D3D6-DC0B-C1586A7BF51A54AD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5B50A846-B89F-D5E0-723B97A886B7A67C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/E893057C-C11F-AB44-3A6937711DBF0DAB.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves Missouri's oldest European settlement, featuring an extraordinary concentration of 18th-century French colonial vertical-log architecture.",fullDescription:"Ste. Genevieve National Historical Park preserves the oldest European settlement in Missouri, established by French-Canadian settlers in the mid-18th century. Located along the Mississippi River, the historic town is famous for preserving some of the finest surviving examples of French colonial 'vertical-log' architecture (poteaux-en-terre) in North America. Visitors can tour historic homes like the Amoureux House and Green Tree Tavern to learn about colonial life, early agriculture, and the diverse cultural blend of French, Spanish, Native, and African peoples.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ste. Geneviève Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Ste. Geneviève National Historical Park, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(573) 880-7189"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Ste. Gene