e:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Little Bighorn Battlefield National Monument winter season hours begin after Labor Day, September 8th. The temporary contact station, bookstore, entrance gate, and Custer National Cemetery are open from 8:00 am to 4:00 pm.",address:"Montana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Little%20Bighorn%20Battlefield%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/libi",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"nez-perce-national-historical-park",name:"Nez Perce National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/31a3c6eb36257487.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87AFBC-1DD8-B71B-0BE33C27113E993C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87B657-1DD8-B71B-0B0970B12C65A5F7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Spans 38 sites across four states, commemorating the history, culture, and heroic survival of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) people.",fullDescription:"Nez Perce National Historical Park is a unique, multi-site park spanning 38 separate locations in Idaho, Oregon, Washington, and Montana. It honors the rich culture, ancestral heritage, and survival of the Nimíipuu (Nez Perce) people. In Montana, the park preserves critical battlefields and landmarks of the dramatic Nez Perce Flight of 1877, including the Bear Paw Battlefield, where Chief Joseph delivered his famous surrender speech ('I will fight no more forever') after a grueling 1,170-mile retreat from the U.S. Army.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Nez Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(208) 843-7001"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Summer visitor center operating hours are 8:30 am - 4:00 pm daily. In the winter, the visitor center is open 5-days a week from Tuesday - Saturday from 8:30 am - 4:00 pm.",address:"Idaho and Montana and Oregon and Washington, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Nez%20Perce%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/nepe",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"fort-union-trading-post-national-historic-site",name:"Fort Union Trading Post National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81ACEF-1DD8-B71B-0B1AD164BE2F3313.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82B4A5-1DD8-B71B-0BDFBD8B53AA8C44.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82B631-1DD8-B71B-0BB22BBC7D080D84.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the reconstructed Great Hall of the preeminent 19th-century fur trading post on the upper Missouri River.",fullDescription:"Fort Union Trading Post National Historic Site, situated on the border of North Dakota and Montana, preserves the site of the most important fur trading post on the Upper Missouri River from 1828 to 1867. Rather than a military fort, Fort Union was a peaceful, bustling commercial outpost where Assiniboine, Plains Cree, Blackfeet, Crow, and other tribes traded buffalo robes and furs for manufactured goods. Today, visitors can tour the meticulously reconstructed stone fort and Great Hall to explore the diverse history of the early American frontier trade.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(701) 572-9083"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hours are in the Central time zone.",address:"Montana and North dakota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Union%20Trading%20Post%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fous",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"grant-kohrs-ranch-national-historic-site",name:"Grant-Kohrs Ranch National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7F26F9-1DD8-B71B-0B805195D3FAF5BF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7F237D-1DD8-B71B-0B7D5D0FC50EE860.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7F2592-1DD8-B71B-0B473ACBB50D926C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/97BD6FCE-CCEB-AAD1-B663745ABDB7CE5C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/BF36CC91-07B5-850E-124A16A2D39982DD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/BFB894D0-C2B7-08B9-95B480E9905BF808.jpg"],shortDescription:"A working cattle ranch in western Montana commemorating the heritage of the open-range cattle industry and the American cowboy.",fullDescription:"Grant-Kohrs Ranch National Historic Site in Deer Lodge, Montana, preserves a massive, active working cattle ranch that represents the open-range cattle empire of the late 19th century. Once the headquarters of a million-acre livestock operation owned by 'cattle king' Conrad Kohrs, the site features a grand 1862 ranch house, original barns, chuckwagons, and historic horse-drawn farm equipment. The park honors the history of the cattle barons, cowboys, and ranching families who shaped the Western frontier.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Grant-Kohrs Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(406) 846-2070"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hours for the main historic area of Grant-Kohrs Ranch, the Visitor Center and Interpretive Ranger programs.",address:"Montana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Grant-Kohrs%20Ranch%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/grko",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"big-hole-national-battlefield",name:"Big Hole National Battlefield",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/g-big-hole-national-battlefield.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9931E00B-1DD8-B71B-0BE1335A10F923B6.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/99620D14-1DD8-B71B-0B4DFE31C3E61867.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the Nez Perce Battle of the Big Hole, a surprise U.S. Army attack and fierce Native resistance fought in August 1877.",fullDescription:"Big Hole National Battlefield near Wisdom, Montana, preserves the site of a dramatic and tragic clash fought on August 9–10, 1877. During their 1,170-mile flight to Canada, a sleeping encampment of Nez Perce families was surprised by an attack from the U.S. Army. Despite heavy losses, Nez Perce warriors mounted a fierce counterattack, saving their families from capture and allowing them to continue their escape. The park is a sacred site of remembrance, featuring hiking trails, historic tipis, and a poignant visitor center.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Big Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(406) 689-3155"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The grounds and trails at Big Hole National Battlefield are open daily from sunrise to sunset.",address:"Montana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Big%20Hole%20National%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/biho",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"bighorn-canyon-national-recreation-area",name:"Bighorn Canyon National Recreation Area",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82164F-1DD8-B71B-0BC7639533B2D456.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8214F5-1DD8-B71B-0B6BA10EEEDC25D1.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82164F-1DD8-B71B-0BC7639533B2D456.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8217B2-1DD8-B71B-0BDCD2532B8E8992.jpg"],shortDescription:"An impressive 120,000-acre playground featuring towering 1,000-foot limestone cliffs enclosing a massive 71-mile reservoir.",fullDescription:"Bighorn Canyon National Recreation Area, spanning the border of Wyoming and Montana, centers around Bighorn Lake, a massive 71-mile reservoir created by the Yellowtail Dam. The canyon features breathtaking, sheer limestone walls towering over 1,000 feet above the water. It offers unparalleled outdoor activities including boating, fishing, wild horse viewing, hiking over 27 miles of trails, and exploring several historic 19th-century ranches in a wild, rugged Western landscape.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Bighorn Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"B