 Montana, Idaho, Washington, and Oregon, carving out dramatic coulees, basalt gorges, and giant ripple marks. The trail connects numerous state parks, scenic overlooks, and geological landmarks, telling the story of one of the most powerful natural forces in Earth's history.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ice Age Floods Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(509) 237-9722"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Montana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ice%20Age%20Floods%20National%20Geologic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/iafl",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"lewis-clark-national-historic-trail",name:"Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FA29AF56-DF2B-8692-40F8422E38FB9093.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F1E53C07-CAC7-1E47-F3002F9102E59BA6.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F21788EE-E877-C00C-257B0EB8AD8F49DB.jpg"],shortDescription:"A 4,900-mile trail tracing the historic 1804–1806 Lewis and Clark Expedition from Pennsylvania to the Pacific Ocean.",fullDescription:"The Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail spans over 4,900 miles across sixteen states, tracing the outbound and inbound routes of the legendary Corps of Discovery commanded by Meriwether Lewis and William Clark. Beginning in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, and extending to the mouth of the Columbia River in Oregon and Washington, the trail commemorates their epic mapping of the American West. Today, the trail connects historic campsites, visitor centers, and river trails, encouraging outdoor recreation and the preservation of indigenous cultural landscapes.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Slough Trail",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"The South Slough Trail is a three mile loop that can be accessed from the Fort to Sea Trailhead or from near the North Netul Landing that features twist and turns and steep changes in elevation among large trees and a bo"},{name:"Clay Pit Pond Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"15-60 Minutes",description:"The Clay Pit Pond is a half mile loop primitive trail with access at the Fort Clatsop Visitor Center picnic area. It is a great trail for those looking for a short but dynamic hike. The trail features a vernal pond surro"},{name:"Netul River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"20-60 Minutes",description:"Take an easy approximately 1 mile stroll along the beautiful Netul River Trail. Watch for water birds, eagles and sometimes otters! Along the way you can learn about the history of logging and commerce along the Lewis an"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis & Clark Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(402) 808-2714"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail Visitor Center is located on the Missouri River in Omaha, Nebraska.",address:"Montana, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lewis%20%26%20Clark%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lecl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],nebraska:[{id:"agate-fossil-beds-national-monument",name:"Agate Fossil Beds National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A058F-1DD8-B71B-0B188ED102F7285F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A06A9-1DD8-B71B-0BA914D99661189C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A07B9-1DD8-B71B-0B077C3CA4979281.jpg"],shortDescription:"A peaceful valley in western Nebraska containing one of the world's most significant Miocene-epoch fossil deposits and a rich Native American artifact collection.",fullDescription:"Agate Fossil Beds National Monument, situated in the scenic Niobrara River valley of western Nebraska, is home to a world-renowned deposit of Miocene-epoch mammal fossils dating back 20 million years. The park preserves pristine mixed-grass prairie and iconic clay bluffs where early paleontologists discovered remarkable species, including the two-horned rhinoceros (Menoceras) and the land-dwelling beaver (Palaeocastor). Additionally, the visitor center houses the James H. Cook Collection of historical Lakota artifacts, which tells the story of early ranching and deep cross-cultural friendships between Cook and Chief Red Cloud.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Daemonelix Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Hike a one-mile trail back in time to the day of the paleocaster, a dry land beaver, and paleosoils, fossilized dirt from the time this landscape was formed. Look out over the vast, open table lands that define the high "},{name:"Fossil Hills Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"45-120 Minutes",description:"Hike to University (left) and Carnegie Hills. The 2.7-mile Fossil Hills Trail allows visitors to walk up to and see the famed early 1900s quarries. From these quarries, paleontologists recovered some of the world's best "}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Agate Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(308) 665-4113"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Nebraska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Agate%20Fossil%20Beds%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/agfo",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"scotts-bluff-national-monument",name:"Scotts Bluff National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AB9C7-1DD8-B71B-0BE8539018DE86B3.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/53FCDCF1-CF55-C217-5992B9D300037376.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/541B41E1-DC9B-3DAA-17952D0186B85883.jpg"],shortDescription:"Towering clay and sandstone bluffs that served as a landmark for generations of emigrants traveling on the Oregon, California, and Mormon trails.",fullDescription:"Scotts Bluff National Monument preserves over 3,000 acres of dramatic clay and sandstone bluffs rising 800 feet above the North Platte River in western Nebraska. For centuries, these massive geological formations served as a crucial landmark for Native Americans, fur traders, and over 350,000 pioneers traveling westward on the Oregon, California, and Mormon Pioneer trails. Visitors can drive the historic Summit Road (passing through three hand-carved tunnels), hike the challenging Saddle Rock Trail, explore historic wagon paths with real trail ruts, and learn about the pioneer migration in the Oregon Trail Museum and Visitor Center.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Scotts Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(308) 436-9700"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Scotts Bluff National Monument is located in the Mountain Time Zone. These hours are for the grounds, picnic area, and trails only.",address:"Nebraska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Scotts%20Bluff%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/scbl",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"homestead-national-historical-park",name:"Homestead National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6FDA06A1-E26A-D4B5-755B3521E345A790.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C13D0BC9-FBA8-3B59-8789B20F08B3AE68.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F0BD1E3B-9EFE-D2CC-EF3F29C143C3FF05.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6F655088-E758-150C-03924E546C7D6DB9.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6FDA06A1-E26A-D4B5-755B3521E345A790.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the far-reaching impacts of the Homestead Act of 1862, which granted free land to hundreds of thousands of settlers.",fullDescription:"Homestead National Historical Park, located near Beatrice, Nebraska, commemorates the monumental impact of the Homestead Act of 1862, which allowed any qualified citizen—including women, immigrants, and formerly enslaved people—to claim 160 acres of public land. The park is situated on the historic homestead claim of Daniel Freeman, widely recognized as one of the very first homesteader claimants in the nation. It features a stunning, trowel-shaped Heritage Center with interactive exhibits, the restored 1867 Freeman School, a historic log cabin, and over 100 acres of reconstructed tallgrass prairie representing the original landscape encountered by pioneers.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Walk Nature Trails at Homestead",difficulty:"Easy",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Homestead National Historical Park offers over three miles of trails through the sweeping tallgrass prairie and quiet bur oak woodland for hiking, cross-country skiing, and nature study. Trail maps are available from eit"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Homestead Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(402) 223-3514"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Trails and park grounds are open from sunrise to sunset. Picnic areas are available at the Education Center and Heritage Center.",address:"Nebraska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Homestead%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/home",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"chimney-rock-national-historic-site",name:"Chimney Rock National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/054e9f71a128dc55.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A spectacular spire of clay and sandstone rising 480 feet above the North Platte valley, the most famous landmark along the Oregon Trail.",fullDescription:"Chimney Rock National Historic Site preserves the most famous landmark along the historic westward overland trails. Rising nearly 300 feet from its conical base to a grand total of 480 feet above the North Platte River valley, this dramatic spire of clay, volcanic ash, and sandstone was mentioned in more emigrant diaries than any other geological feature. It signaled the end of the flat Great Plains and the beginning of the rugged mountain terrain. The Ethel and Christopher J. Abbott Visitor Center features hands-on exhibits, video presentations, and interactive museum displays exploring the hardships and stories of 19th-century pioneer migration.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Chimney Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Chimney Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(308) 586-2581"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Nebraska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chimney%20Rock%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/places/000/chimney-rock-national-historic-site.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"missouri-national-recreational-river",name:"Missouri National Recreational River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8375D2-1DD8-B71B-0B28DF0D3992CFAE.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83791A-1DD8-B71B-0B9235DF1A05A7B4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/176CC10B-9149-BA14-EBA7934F1BDCA490.jpg"],shortDescription:"Protects two remaining free-flowing, non-channelized segments of the mighty Missouri River along the Nebraska-South Dakota border.",fullDescription:"The Missouri National Recreational River preserves two remaining free-flowing, un-channelized, and un-impounded segments of the lower Missouri River along the border of Nebraska and South Dakota. Totaling 98 miles, these reaches exhibit the dynamic river systems, shifting sandbars, chutes, islands, and cottonwood forests that Meriwether Lewis and William Clark observed in 1804. The park offers exceptional outdoor recreation, including canoeing, kayaking, motorboating, sport fishing, and birdwatching, as well as several historic landmarks like the Meridian Bridge in Yankton and Fort Randall.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Visit Mulberry Bend Overlook",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Great view of the river and sandbars from the overlooks. A paved trail takes visitors from the parking lot to several interpretive wayside exhibits that define the natural and cultural history of the river. Continue your"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Missouri Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(605) 665-0209"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Nebraska and South dakota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Missouri%20National%20Recreational%20River",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mnrr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"niobrara-national-scenic-river",name:"Niobrara National Scenic River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7C95AD-1DD8-B71B-0B9C962E8BA62E89.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7CA02C-1DD8-B71B-0BCEA25352ED688A.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7C9E7E-1DD8-B71B-0BA958173B164D34.jpg"],shortDescription:"A pristine 76-mile liquid highway in northern Nebraska, renowned for its outstanding floating, lush canyon walls, and over 200 waterfalls.",fullDescription:"The Niobrara National Scenic River protects a spectacular 76-mile stretch of water running through a unique ecological crossroads in northern Nebraska. The river is world-famous for canoeing, kayaking, and inner tubing, flowing through a scenic, spring-fed valley carved into the Sandhills. Because six distinct ecosystems meet here—including northern boreal forest, eastern deciduous forest, and mixed-grass prairie—the valley supports a remarkably diverse array of flora and fauna. The park features over 200 waterfalls, including Smith Falls, the tallest waterfall in Nebraska, making it a peaceful oasis for nature lovers.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike to Fort Falls",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"20-60 Minutes",description:"This scenic 1.0 mile hike highlights the 45-foot-tall Fort Falls and the shores of the Niobrara National Scenic River on the Fort Niobrara National Wildlife Refuge. With free parking, this hike can be shortened to few th"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Niobrara Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(402) 376-1901"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Nebraska, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Niobrara%20National%20Scenic%20River",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/niob",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"california-national-historic-trail",name:"California National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7987-1DD8-B71B-0B3C3123ED8F686B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7CDA-1DD8-B71B-0B999C4AC7A9D37C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7E5D-1DD8-B71B-0B0A1BE6987E18AF.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7FCF-1DD8-B71B-0BA502EF5988BC47.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/56ACD550-C0CE-423B-9448A9D0ABCDD9E7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. The California National Historic Trail is over 5,000 miles long and covers portions of 10 states. Step into history along more than 1,000 miles of ruts and traces from travelers and their overland wagons.",fullDescription:"California National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. The California National Historic Trail is over 5,000 miles long and covers portions of 10 states. Step into history along more than 1,000 miles of ruts and traces from travelers and their overland wagons. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the California Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. There are a variety of hikin"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"California Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for California National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for