ia. Please check the official NPS page or search under California for details."},rangerPrograms:[],campingInfo:"Refer to main state profile.",wildlife:[],bestViewpoints:[],entranceFees:"Varies",hours:"Varies",address:"California and Nevada, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Death%20Valley%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/deat",bestTimeToVisit:"Year-round",accessibilityInfo:"Varies.",travelTips:["Select California to view comprehensive itineraries and details."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"great-basin-national-park",name:"Great Basin National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FD29737E-9549-8EF4-90C1076486DE559A.jpeg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning desert oasis featuring ancient bristlecone pines, Lehman Caves, and the towering 13,063-foot summit of Wheeler Peak.",fullDescription:"Great Basin National Park, located in eastern Nevada, is a diverse wonderland of high-altitude peaks, subterranean caverns, and ancient forests rising from the sagebrush deserts of the Great Basin. The park is home to groves of ancient bristlecone pines, some of the oldest living organisms on Earth, with individual trees exceeding 4,000 years in age. Visitors can explore the ornate limestone chambers of Lehman Caves, hike to the glacier-carved slopes of the towering 13,063-foot Wheeler Peak, stargaze in some of the darkest night skies in the country, and view Nevada's only active glacier.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Baker Creek Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"90-180 Minutes",description:"An upper easy/low moderate hike, Baker Creek is often accessible or semi-accessible year round, making a viable alternative when roads close or parking lots are crowded. 3.3 miles makes for acceptable easy distance for m"},{name:"Hike to the Wheeler Peak Bristlecone Grove",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"The Bristlecone Trail is not to be missed on a visit to the park. This 2.8-mile round-trip hike takes visitors through some of the oldest organisms on Earth, and offers opportunities for introspection as you consider you"},{name:"Hike to Lexington Arch",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"Hike 5.6 miles through shrubland to see one of the largest limestone arches in the world. Find plenty of peace on this lesser traveled trail. Lexington Arch is also the only park trail that allows dogs, so bring your pet"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Great Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(775) 234-7331"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Nevada, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Great%20Basin%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/grba",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"avi-kwa-ame-national-monument",name:"Avi Kwa Ame National Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6bcd439f9248b7e7.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves over 500,000 acres of sacred Mojave Desert lands, rugged mountain ranges, and dense Joshua tree forests in southern Nevada.",fullDescription:`Avi Kwa Ame National Monument, established in 2023, protects more than 500,000 acres of biologically rich and culturally significant lands in southern Nevada. Known in Mojave as "Spirit Mountain," Avi Kwa Ame is a sacred site of creation for ten Yuman-speaking Native American tribes and holds spiritual significance for Hopi and Paiute peoples. The monument protects stunning desert landscapes, ancient petroglyphs, historic mining areas, and one of the world's largest Joshua tree forests. It also preserves critical wildlife corridors for desert bighorn sheep and the threatened desert tortoise.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Avi Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(702) 515-5000"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Nevada, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Avi%20Kwa%20Ame%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/nationalmonuments/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"tule-springs-fossil-beds-national-monument",name:"Tule Springs Fossil Beds National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85F41B-1DD8-B71B-0B8406CBCA19D911.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"An urban paleontological site preserving thousands of Pleistocene-epoch fossils, including mammoths, lions, and camels just north of Las Vegas.",fullDescription:"Tule Springs Fossil Beds National Monument, located just north of Las Vegas, Nevada, preserves an extraordinary scientific archive of the Ice Age. The 22,650-acre monument contains one of the world's richest deposits of Pleistocene-epoch vertebrate fossils. For tens of thousands of years, lush wetlands attracted diverse megafauna. Paleontologists have uncovered fossilized remains of Columbian mammoths, extinct camels, ancient horses, saber-toothed cats, and the formidable American lion. The park provides visitors with an educational window into an ancient, water-rich Nevada desert.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Tule Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(702) 515-5450"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Tule Springs Fossil Beds National Monument is open during daylight hours. The park is closed at night.",address:"Nevada, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tule%20Springs%20Fossil%20Beds%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/tusk",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"lake-mead-national-recreation-areagrand-canyon-parashant-national-monument",name:"Lake Mead National Recreation AreaGrand Canyon–Parashant National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79D292-1DD8-B71B-0B9A850B0D5AAAFC.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79D3DE-1DD8-B71B-0B0E3FD563EACFB5.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79D824-1DD8-B71B-0BF17A042C954AA2.jpg"],shortDescription:"A massive desert playground centering on Lake Mead and Lake Mohave, offering exceptional boating, fishing, and hiking.",fullDescription:"Lake Mead National Recreation Area, the nation's first and largest national recreation area, covers 1.5 million acres of spectacular valleys, dramatic canyons, and clean water across southern Nevada and northwestern Arizona. Centering on the massive reservoirs of Lake Mead (formed by Hoover Dam) and Lake Mohave (formed by Davis Dam), the park is a premier destination for boating, jet skiing, swimming, kayaking, and world-class sportfishing. Beyond the water, visitors can explore rugged Mojave Desert trails, natural hot springs, historic railroad tunnels, and the remote, raw wilderness of Grand Canyon-Parashant National Monument.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(702) 293-8990"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Cen