 relay system along the Pony Express National Historic Trail in eight states was the most direct and practical means of east-west communications before the telegraph. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Walk on the Pony Express Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"There are a variety of walking and hiking opportunities along the Pony Express Trail. In some places, you may find a modern-day hiking trail. In other places, the historic trail has been replaced by an dirt or asphalt ro"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Pony Express Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Pony Express National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Nevada, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pony%20Express%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/poex/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],"new hampshire":[{id:"saint-gaudens-national-historical-park",name:"Saint-Gaudens National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c91620dc9091f227.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the scenic home, gardens, and active studios of Augustus Saint-Gaudens, one of America's greatest classical sculptors.",fullDescription:"Saint-Gaudens National Historical Park in Cornish, New Hampshire, preserves the home, studios, and beautiful formal gardens of Augustus Saint-Gaudens, one of America's foremost sculptors. This unique park, the only National Park Service unit in New Hampshire, was the center of the famous Cornish Art Colony. Visitors can view over 100 of Saint-Gaudens' elegant bronze artworks—including the Shaw Memorial and the Lincoln monument—explore scenic walking trails, attend live bronze-casting demonstrations, and experience the natural inspiration of the Connecticut River Valley.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Saint-Gaudens Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(603) 675-2175"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Park buildings are open Thursday through Monday from 9am to 4:30pm, Memorial Day weekend to October 31. Grounds and trails are open during daylight hours.",address:"New hampshire, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Saint-Gaudens%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/saga",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autu