ossal neoclassical sculpture designed by Frédéric-Auguste Bartholdi and gifted by the people of France in 1886. It stands as a universal symbol of freedom, hope, and democracy. The monument unit also includes historic Ellis Island, the nation's premier immigration station from 1892 to 1954, where over 12 million aspiring citizens took their first steps on American soil. Today, visitors can tour the museums on both islands, ascend to Liberty's crown, and explore deep family ancestral logs.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Statue Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(212) 363-3200"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Access to Liberty and Ellis Islands is by Statue City Cruises ferry only. For ferry departure times and ticketing information visit http://www.statuecitycruises.com or call 1-877-LADY-TIX.",address:"New jersey and New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Statue%20of%20Liberty%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/stli",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"morristown-national-historical-park",name:"Morristown National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C804A08-1DD8-B71B-0B73C1F0A0ACAD7A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C804BB2-1DD8-B71B-0BD3719BE49A0FDD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C804D28-1DD8-B71B-0B577BE90E49B5E8.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C804E96-1DD8-B71B-0B9824672DCF8A75.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B1188067-B906-30A8-AEC7161B5DB3EB7B.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9724784C-DDBF-6BEA-6F811FA1134B74B8.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the encampment sites of George Washington and the Continental Army during the brutally cold winters of the Revolutionary War.",fullDescription:"Morristown National Historical Park preserves the historic sites of the Continental Army's winter encampments during the American Revolutionary War. Under the leadership of General George Washington, Morristown served as a secure, strategic headquarters during the severe winters of 1777 and 1779–1780. The park features the Ford Mansion, which served as Washington's headquarters, the Jockey Hollow encampment area with reconstructed soldier huts, Fort Nonsense, and a premier museum housing an extensive collection of Revolutionary War manuscripts, military weapons, and artifacts.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Explore the Cross Estate Gardens and Hike to the NJ Brigade Encampment Site",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"You can go in any order once you arrive at the New Jersey Brigade Encampment Area and Cross Estate Gardens. The hike is approx. 1 mile to reach the encampment site (note: it is downhill going out, uphill coming back)."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Morristown Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(973) 539-2016"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Washington's Headquarters Museum: Open Thursday-Monday 9:30 am to 4:30 pm. Jockey Hollow Visitor Center: Open Thursday-Monday 9:30 am to 4:30 pm.",address:"New jersey, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Morristown%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/morr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"paterson-great-falls-national-historical-park",name:"Paterson Great Falls National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3F720292-1DD8-B71B-0BBF0A529555D377.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3EDC4916-1DD8-B71B-0B4282C70FC365B7.jpg"],shortDescription:"A spectacular 77-foot waterfall on the Passaic River that powered Alexander Hamilton's vision of American industrial independence.",fullDescription:"Paterson Great Falls National Historical Park centers around a spectacular 77-foot waterfall on the Passaic River in Paterson, New Jersey. In 1791, Alexander Hamilton selected these roaring falls to establish the Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures, founding Paterson as the nation's first planned industrial city. Powered by a complex system of water raceways, Paterson's mills manufactured cotton textiles, firearms, railroad locomotives, and silk. Today, the park preserves Hamilton's industrial legacy, the stunning geological falls, and the diverse history of American labor.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Paterson Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(973) 523-0370"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Grounds are open 7 days a weel and Rangers are available 9:00AM-4:30PM Wednesday-Sunday . Please call 973-523-0370 or 201-458-3475(park cell number) the day of your visit to ensure tour availability.",address:"New jersey, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Paterson%20Great%20Falls%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/pagr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"thomas-edison-national-historical-park",name:"Thomas Edison National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81E2B0-1DD8-B71B-0B55C38EB2E224A5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81E418-1DD8-B71B-0BC491FC962D191C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81E760-1DD8-B71B-0BDD84F26F839FA7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves Thomas Edison's massive laboratory complex and Queen Anne estate, where modern research and world-changing inventions were born.",fullDescription:`Thomas Edison National Historical Park in West Orange, New Jersey, preserves the industrial research laboratory complex and elegant Queen Anne mansion, "Glenmont," of America's preeminent inventor. From 1887 to 1931, Edison and his team of scientists worked here to pioneer the motion picture camera, improved phonographs, alkaline storage batteries, and early electrical grids. Visitors can tour the massive three-story library, chemistry labs, machine shops, and the Black Maria—the world's first motion picture studio—to experience the birthplace of modern research and development.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Thomas Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(973) 736-0550"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"All visitors must go to the Laboratory Complex first to obtain their entry pass and Glenmont Grounds pass. (Glenmont is currently only open on Saturday and Sunday)",address:"New jersey, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Thomas%20Edison%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/edis",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"delaware-water-gap-national-recreation-areamiddle-delaware-national-scenic-river",name:"Delaware Water Gap National Recreation AreaMiddle Delaware National Scenic River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79C4E3-1DD8-B71B-0B9EC12DFCA00E1C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79C5ED-1DD8-B71B-0BFDBB0313C6B8A7.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79C832-1DD8-B71B-0BAACF8A16A81B90.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/47B1994C-1DD8-B71B-0BC5F687A7941034.jpg"],shortDescription:"A beautiful 40-mile forested river valley cutting through the Appalachian ridge, offering exceptional boating, hiking, and camping.",fullDescription:'Delaware Water Gap National Recreation Area protects 70,000 acres of pristine forested mountains, valleys, and floodplains along the scenic Delaware River on the New Jersey and Pennsylvania border. The park is renowned for the dramatic "Gap," where the river slices through the towering ridge of the Appalachian Mountains. It encompasses the Middle Delaware National Scenic River, a 40-mile free-flowing stretch perfect for canoeing, rafting, and fishing. Visitors can hike over 100 miles of trails (including a section of the Appalachian Trail), explore historical villages, and view spectacular cascading waterfalls like Buttermilk Falls.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Easy Hike Along Hornbecks Creek",difficulty:"Easy",length:"See nps.gov",description:"An easy, one-mile out and back hike along the Lower Hornbecks Creek will lead visitors to a small waterfall, but the waterfall is only part of the view! "}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Delaware Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(570) 426-2452"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS 