nd birdwatching, as well as preserving vital coastal salt marshes and ecosystems.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Gateway Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(718) 354-4606"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Includes Bergen Beach, Floyd Bennett Field, Fort Tilden, Frank Charles Park, Hamilton Beach, Jamaica Bay Wildlife Refuge, Canarsie Pier, Jacob Riis Park, North Channel Bridge, Plumb Beach and Spring Creek.",address:"New jersey and New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Gateway%20National%20Recreation%20Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gate",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"great-egg-harbor-national-scenic-and-recreational-river",name:"Great Egg Harbor National Scenic and Recreational River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/69830EAB-1DD8-B71B-0B2995AFD827B8FB.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A 129-mile free-flowing river system in southern New Jersey, flowing through the Pinelands to the Atlantic and renowned for birding and paddling.",fullDescription:"The Great Egg Harbor National Scenic and Recreational River protects 129 miles of pristine, free-flowing water in southern New Jersey. Sourced in the state's agricultural interior, the river drains into the Atlantic Ocean, flowing through the unique, sandy pine forests of the Pinelands National Reserve. The river's dark, amber-colored waters—stained by cedar tannins—and dense coastal salt marshes support a incredibly diverse population of nesting raptors, shorebirds, and migratory waterfowl. It offers peaceful, undisturbed paddling, kayaking, and exceptional nature observation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Great Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(609) 646-4036"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Great Egg Harbor River is a 55.0-mile-long (88.5 km) river in southern New Jersey in the United States. It is one of the major rivers that traverse the largely pristine Pinelands, draining 308 square miles (800 km2) of wetlands into the",address:"New jersey, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Great%20Egg%20Harbor%20National%20Scenic%20and%20Recreational%20River",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/greg",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"middle-delaware-national-scenic-river",name:"Middle Delaware National Scenic River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79C4E3-1DD8-B71B-0B9EC12DFCA00E1C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79C5ED-1DD8-B71B-0BFDBB0313C6B8A7.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79C832-1DD8-B71B-0BAACF8A16A81B90.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/47B1994C-1DD8-B71B-0BC5F687A7941034.jpg"],shortDescription:"A beautiful 40-mile free-flowing stretch of the Delaware River famed for paddling, fishing, and spectacular Appalachian views.",fullDescription:"The Middle Delaware National Scenic River protects a clean, free-flowing 40-mile segment of the Delaware River within the Delaware Water Gap National Recreation Area. Bordering New Jersey and Pennsylvania, this protected riverway is an outstanding destination for recreational canoeing, kayaking, rafting, and sport fishing. The surrounding river valley features dense hardwood forests, scenic limestone cliffs, historic farmsteads, and exceptional birdwatching opportunities, preserving a serene natural sanctuary near major eastern metropolitan centers.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Easy Hike Along Hornbecks Creek",difficulty:"Easy",length:"See nps.gov",description:"An easy, one-mile out and back hike along the Lower Hornbecks Creek will lead visitors to a small waterfall, but the waterfall is only part of the view! "}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Middle Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(570) 426-2452"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],best