ed American independence.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"},{name:"Become a B.A.R.K. Ranger!",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Become a B.A.R.K and P.U.R.R.K Ranger at Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Routes National Historic Trai."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(215) 597-7013"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"New jersey, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Washington-Rochambeau%20Revolutionary%20Route%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/waro/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}]},l={"new mexico":[{id:"carlsbad-caverns-national-park",name:"Carlsbad Caverns National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/29C3C977-AAE6-B7FD-3C107828D704A5CB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/29A84FDD-D660-B07C-A6B12986D3123FC7.jpeg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C9EB52A-BD34-F69C-0149C92FF24E215D.jpg"],shortDescription:"A subterranean wonderland featuring over 119 caves, massive limestone chambers, and spectacular bat flights in the Chihuahuan Desert.",fullDescription:`Carlsbad Caverns National Park, nestled in the rugged Guadalupe Mountains of southeastern New Mexico, preserves a stunning subterranean world of ancient limestone caves. The park's main attraction, Carlsbad Cavern, includes the "Big Room"—the largest single cave chamber by volume in North America, filled with intricate stalactites, stalagmites, and draperies. Above ground, the park features rugged desert canyons, hiking trails, and the famous evening bat flight program, where hundreds of thousands of Brazilian free-tailed bats emerge from the cavern's natural entrance.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Explore the Cavern at Your Own Pace",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"The Big Room and Natural Entrance trails can be enjoyed at your own pace. The Big Room Trail is easy to moderate in difficulty. The Natural Entrance Trail is strenuous. Enjoy one or both to experience a variety of cave f"},{name:"Chihuahuan Desert Nature Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30 Minutes",description:"Enjoy views of the Guadalupe Mountains and explore Chihuahuan Desert plant life along this short 0.7 mile (1.1 km) loop trail."},{name:"Juniper Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3 Hours",description:"Enjoy the Chihuahuan Desert in a serene landscape along this 3.5 mile (5 km) trail."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Carlsbad Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(575) 785-2232"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Carlsbad Caverns National Park is open 24-hours a day. Visitor center hours and cave tour schedule vary throughout the year.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Carlsbad%20Caverns%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cave",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"white-sands-national-park",name:"White Sands National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7CA16410-A412-1F05-2D92EB04EEB27980.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7FE53E-1DD8-B71B-0BE4AEBECAB602AD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7FE691-1DD8-B71B-0B21F94DBD31A965.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C819076-1DD8-B71B-0BBCE3AA4AF4D2CD.jpg"],shortDescription:"The world's largest gypsum dunefield, spanning 275 square miles of glistening white sand in the Tularosa Basin.",fullDescription:"White Sands National Park preserves a dramatic landscape of rare white gypsum sand dunes in southern New Mexico's Tularosa Basin. This spectacular dunefield—the largest of its kind on Earth—glistens under the desert sun, creating a surreal and shifting environment. Visitors can hike across the wave-like dunes, sled down the soft slopes, drive the scenic Dunes Drive, and witness breathtaking sunset views. The park also protects a rich archaeological record, including some of the oldest fossilized human footprints in North America.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["White Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"White Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(575) 479-6124"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The park gates always open at 7:00 am with closing hours changing with sunset. The park closes 30 minutes after local sunset.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=White%20Sands%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/whsa",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"aztec-ruins-national-monument",name:"Aztec Ruins National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80AFE4-1DD8-B71B-0BE2EEFC92413401.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80ADDD-1DD8-B71B-0B3BC9BFA515A233.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80AEDE-1DD8-B71B-0B0832D47B050F9A.jpg"],shortDescription:"A highly preserved 900-year-old Ancestral Puebloan public building with a fully reconstructed Great Kiva.",fullDescription:"Aztec Ruins National Monument preserves an extraordinary 900-year-old ancestral Pueblo social, ceremonial, and political center. Located near the Animas River in northwestern New Mexico, the site features a massive 400-room great house constructed of exquisitely crafted sandstone blocks. The highlight of the monument is the Great Kiva, a fully reconstructed subterranean ceremonial chamber that provides visitors with an immersive window into the spiritual life and advanced architecture of the Ancestral Puebloan people.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Aztec Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 334-6174"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Aztec Ruins National Monument is open to the public from 7:00am-5:00pm, which includes the parking areas, archeological sites, picnic areas, and trails. The visitor center is open from 9:00am-5:00pm daily.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Aztec%20Ruins%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/azru",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"bandelier-national-monument",name:"Bandelier National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C830B03-1DD8-B71B-0BA1266ED9B0CE08.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C830E07-1DD8-B71B-0B19DE78174BBC7F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C830F61-1DD8-B71B-0BCA306EADAB4457.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83126D-1DD8-B71B-0B800213F33D2F6D.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C831529-1DD8-B71B-0B9E2AE36958A39C.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the ancient cliff dwellings, cavates, and petroglyphs carved into volcanic tuff canyon walls.",fullDescription:'Bandelier National Monument protects over 33,000 acres of rugged canyon and mesa country near Los Alamos, New Mexico. The park preserves the ancestral homes of Pueblo peoples, dating back over 11,000 years. Visitors can walk the main loop trail to climb wooden ladders into "cavates" (small human-carved alcoves in the soft volcanic tuff cliffs), explore the spectacular remains of Tyuonyi Pueblo on the canyon floor, and hike to the remote Alcove House situated 140 feet above the canyon floor.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Bandelier Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 672-3861"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Bandelier National Monument is open every day from dawn to dusk unless impacted by severe weather or noted for specific holidays. The hours of dawn and dusk vary throughout the year so please come prepared knowing when you may have to exit ",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bandelier%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/band",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"capulin-volcano-national-monument",name:"Capulin Volcano National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84FCB2-1DD8-B71B-0BE4C7320D95575F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84FDF7-1DD8-B71B-0B21AAE5CD8316CB.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84FF37-1DD8-B71B-0B956181F9F5E199.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8501F7-1DD8-B71B-0B83ABCF518E1D2B.jpg"],shortDescription:"An extinct cinder cone volcano rising dramatically above the northeastern plains, offering views of five states from its rim.",fullDescription:"Capulin Volcano National Monument preserves an outstanding example of an extinct, symmetrical cinder cone volcano in northeastern New Mexico. Part of the Raton-Clayton Volcanic Field, Capulin Volcano rose from the plains approximately 60,000 years ago. Today, a paved road spirals to the volcano's rim, where visitors can hike trails that go around the rim or descend into the crater itself. On clear days, the panoramic views from the summit extend across New Mexico, Colorado, Texas, Oklahoma, and Kansas.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hi