 Malpais National Monument preserves a starkly beautiful landscape of recent volcanic activity in western New Mexico. Translating to "The Badlands" in Spanish, El Malpais features diverse volcanic geology, including extensive black lava flows, cinder cones, dramatic sandstone cliffs, and intricate networks of lava tube caves. The park's rugged terrain supports ancient Douglas fir forests growing in old lava pockets, historic trade routes like the Zuni-Acoma Trail, and excellent opportunities for backcountry hiking and wild caving exploration.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the El Calderon Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"El Calderon Area at El Malpais National Monument offers easy to moderate difficulty hiking past lava tube caves, over ancient lava flows, and around the top of an extinct cinder cone. This trail can be hiked as a 3 mile "},{name:"Traverse the Lava Falls Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Take a hike on the youngest lava flow in El Malpais National Monument and in New Mexico. You will encounter numerous unique volcanic features on this one- mile cairned route."},{name:"Hike the Narrows Rim Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-6 Hours",description:"Spectacular panoramic views of the lava flows at El Malpais and surrounding landscape are the main attraction of this hike. You may also encounter ravens and other birds of prey enjoying the thermal uplifts here."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"El Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 876-2783"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=El%20Malpais%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/elma",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"el-morro-national-monument",name:"El Morro National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E4D4D-1DD8-B71B-0B9B5562C2BAACFC.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E51EC-1DD8-B71B-0BAE3175FC22CDD3.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E538A-1DD8-B71B-0B547055F3B736AB.jpg"],shortDescription:"A massive sandstone promontory featuring a shaded water pool and hundreds of historical inscriptions and petroglyphs.",fullDescription:'El Morro National Monument preserves a towering sandstone promontory that served as a reliable water source and resting place for travelers for hundreds of years. The reliable pool of water at its base attracted ancestral Puebloans, Spanish explorers, and American pioneers, who left over 2,000 inscriptions, signatures, dates, and petroglyphs carved into the soft stone faces—including the famous "Pasó por aquí" inscription of Spanish Governor Juan de Oñate. Atop the bluff, visitors can explore the ruins of Atsinna, a 13th-century pueblo that once housed hundreds of residents.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking the Headland Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"60-150 Minutes",description:"This 2-mile trail includes the Inscription Loop and takes you to the top of the El Morro bluff. It is a moderately strenuous trail with a gain of 224 ft of elevation. Much of the upper section of the trail is on uneven s"},{name:"Hike the Inscription Rock Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"This 0.75 mile (1.2 km) trail takes you to the pool and past hundreds of Spanish and American inscriptions, and prehistorical petroglyphs. The trail is paved and the main 1/2 mile loop is wheelchair-accessible with assis"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"El Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 783-4226"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Summer Hours (May 24, 2026 - October 17, 2026). Park visitor center and trails open 7 days/week.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=El%20Morro%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/elmo",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"fort-union-national-monument",name:"Fort Union National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7C1B33-1DD8-B71B-0B88C189AAA85B01.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The impressive adobe ruins of the largest 19th-century military fort in the American Southwest, situated along the Santa Fe Trail.",fullDescription:"Fort Union National Monument protects the sweeping adobe ruins of the largest frontier military fort in the American Southwest. Established in 1851, Fort Union served as a critical guardian of the Santa Fe Trail, a major military supply depot, and a key federal stronghold during the Civil War. Visitors can stroll along paved paths through the haunting adobe arches, view extensive ruts of the original Santa Fe Trail, and learn about the diverse soldiers, traders, and travelers who passed through this remote plains outpost.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 425-8025"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The monument grounds and visitor center are generally open 7 days a week, from 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM. The monument is closed Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, New Year's Day.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Union%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/foun",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"gila-cliff-dwellings-national-monument",name:"Gila Cliff Dwellings National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83FC70-1DD8-B71B-0BB95811DA8D8522.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8401BB-1DD8-B71B-0B29C3E110725BF2.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83FE38-1DD8-B71B-0B088D92FBB7939E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83FC70-1DD8-B71B-0BB95811DA8D8522.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84C55C-1DD8-B71B-0B2357EE2D760C54.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C09CF975-1DD8-B71B-0BC5FE23911C9819.jpg"],shortDescription:"Ancient cliff dwellings built within natural caves in the rugged canyons of the Gila Wilderness by the Mogollon culture.",fullDescription:"Gila Cliff Dwellings National Monument preserves the home of Mogollon peoples who inhabited the rugged canyons of southwestern New Mexico in the late 1200s. Situated inside five natural alcoves in the cliffside of the Gila River valley, these remarkably preserved stone dwellings feature 40 rooms that offer a rare glimpse into the daily lives of prehistoric desert dwellers. Surrounded by the pristine Gila Wilderness, the park provides opportunities for hiking, hot springs exploring, and learning about the Mogollon culture.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Gila Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Gila Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(575) 323-2904"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The National Monument and Visitor Center are open all year and are closed only for Thanksgiving Day (the 4th Thursday of November), Christmas Day (December 25) and New Year's Day (January 1).",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Gila%20Cliff%20Dwellings%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gicl",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"petroglyph-national-monument",name:"Petroglyph National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2425221F-94B6-50F2-382B3DB62384FB07.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/23C24E6B-E74F-FFC9-9396316C856B4228.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7DA2BC-1DD8-B71B-0B1AB38AD4FB21D7.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/10CCE75E-1DD8-B71B-0B5F2C39FA0DD048.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7DA5F9-1DD8-B71B-0B29D03420294A71.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7DA77D-1DD8-B71B-0B12E0492DEA5D29.jpg"],shortDescription:"One of the largest petroglyph sites in North America, featuring thousands of prehistoric images carved into volcanic basalt cliffs.",fullDescription:"Petroglyph National Monument, located on the western edge of Albuquerque, New Mexico, protects one of the largest ancient rock art sites in North America. Jointly managed by the National Park Service and the City of Albuquerque, the monument features an estimated 25,000 images carved into a 17-mile volcanic basalt escarpment. These petroglyphs, carved by Ancestral Puebloans and early Spanish settlers, carry deep spiritual, historical, and cultural meaning. Visitors can hike through several canyons, including Boca Negra and Rinconada, to observe these striking carvings up close.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Petroglyph Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 899-0205"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Staffed by our partner, the City of Albuquerque Open Space, this developed trail system consists of 3 short, paved trails with trail markers. Full-service restroom facility, picnic tables, shaded seating areas and a shaded patio area.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Petroglyph%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/petr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"valles-caldera-national-preserve",name:"Valles Caldera National Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/921E6FBB-90EE-2BF0-6F3D52902F3DA89D.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A magnificent 89,000-acre volcanic caldera featuring expansive high-altitude meadows, hot springs, and abundant wildlife.",fullDescription:`Valles Caldera National Preserve protects a breathtaking 13.7-mile-wide volcanic caldera in northern New Mexico's Jemez Mountains, formed by a massive eruption 1.25 million years ago. The preserve features magnificent high-altitude valleys (valles), winding trout streams, bubbling geothermal hot springs, and dense forests. Known as the "Yellowstone of the Southwest," Valles Caldera is home to New Mexico's largest herd of Rocky Mountain elk, as well as black bears, coyotes, and eagles. It offers excellent backcountry hiking, cross-country skiing, fly-fishing, and scenic drives.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking in Valles Caldera",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Hiking routes within Valles Caldera range from flat strolls through montane grasslands to steep climbs on forested lava domes. As this land was a private ranch for over 150 years, the majority of hiking routes follow old"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Valles Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 670-1612"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Valle Grande Main Entrance is located at mile marker 39.2 along New Mexico Highway 4.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Valles%20Caldera%20National%20Preserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/vall",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Preserve"},{id:"chaco-culture-national-historical-park",name:"Chaco Culture National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6766D5AC-B66E-C5E8-8FF614CA62DB2C24.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/67551208-A835-AB5A-2ABD2016B28F7A54.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6766D5AC-B66E-C5E8-8FF614CA62DB2C24.jpg"],shortDescription:"A monumental center of Ancestral Puebloan culture featuring massive sandstone great houses with advanced astronomical alignments.",fullDescription:"Chaco Culture National Historical Park preserves a monumental center of ancestral Puebloan ceremony, trade, and administration that flourished between AD 850 and 1150 in northwestern New Mexico's Chaco Canyon. The park contains some of the most sophisticated pre-Columbian architecture in North America, including Pueblo Bonito, a multi-story great house with hundreds of rooms and multiple kivas. The structures are renowned for their highly precise astronomical alignments, sophisticated engineering, and vast network of ancient roads, making it a UNESCO World Heritage Site and an International Dark Sky Park.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Chaco Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 786-7014"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"All hiking trails and archaeological sites are open from 7am until 9:00pm from May 1-October 31. The visitor center is open from 9:00am-5:00pm daily.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chaco%20Culture%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/chcu",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"manhattan-project-national-historical-park",name:"Manhattan Project National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/87DC8664-0A66-3B76-6B9BF543D9581735.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/884A2FA9-FC95-36D1-FB9C52521E3FED66.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the top-secret World War II effort to design and build the first atomic weapons in Los Alamos, Oak Ridge, and Hanford.",fullDescription:"Manhattan Project National Historical Park, jointly managed by the National Park Service and the Department of Energy, preserves key historic sites associated with the top-secret World War II project to develop the world's first atomic bombs. In Los Alamos, New Mexico, the park covers the secret laboratory town where scientists like J. Robert Oppenheimer lived and worked, featuring sites like the Fuller Lodge, the historic houses of Bathtub Row, and gun-site test facilities. The park also includes partner units in Oak Ridge, Tennessee, and Hanford, Washington, exploring the monumental scientific, social, and ethical implications of the atomic age.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hanford: Hike the White Bluffs North Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"On this trail you may explore rolling sand dunes, take in vast vistas dotted with relics of plutonium production during the Manhattan Project and Cold War, and observe a variety of wildlife."},{name:"Hanford: Hike Candy Mountain",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"The trail up Candy Mountain is a moderate 3.6 mile (5.8 km) roundtrip hike that offers sweeping views of the Tri-Cities region and a walk through time. Signs along the trail explain the impact of the ice age floods on ar"},{name:"Hanford: Hike the White Bluffs South Slope Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"6-8 Hours",description:"This trail provides one of the best views of the old Hanford town site and location of the Hanford construction camp for the Manhattan Project. This trail will reward you with expansive views of the Hanford site and oppo"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Manhattan Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 661-6277"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Hanford Visitor Center is open Monday - Friday 9:00 AM-4:00 PM. Access to the Hanford B Reactor is suspended due to major construction projects.",address:"New mexico and Tennessee and Washington, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Manhattan%20Project%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mapr",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"pecos-national-historical-park",name:"Pecos National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9E0EE978-A7FE-192D-7974097415DA9525.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9E0EE978-A7FE-192D-7974097415DA9525.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A0138357-98D1-2C34-9A916F685EFED814.jpg"],shortDescription:"A unique valley where ancient pueblo ruins, Spanish mission ruins, Civil War battlegrounds, and a historic ranch meet.",fullDescription:"Pecos National Historical Park preserves a unique cultural crossroads in a scenic valley between the Sangre de Cristo and Glorieta mountains of northern New Mexico. The park's main feature is the majestic ruins of Pecos Pueblo, which was once a powerful trade center with hundreds of inhabitants, standing alongside the towering adobe walls of a 17-century Spanish Franciscan mission church. The park also protects the Glorieta Pass Civil War Battlefield, where Union and Confederate forces clashed in 1862, and the historic Forked Lightning Ranch, once owned by actress Greer Garson.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking Glorieta Pass Battlefield",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"90-150 Minutes",description:"Hiking the 2.25-mile Glorieta Pass Battlefield Trail will lead you through part of New Mexico's Civil War history. The trail leads to sites where fighting occurred in March 1862."},{name:"Hiking the Ancestral Sites Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"40-90 Minutes",description:"This trail gives you a glimpse into the past as you pass by the ancestral sites of the Pecos Pueblo and the mission church. You will also enjoy beautiful vistas of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains and Glorieta Mesa as you "},{name:"Walking the South Pasture Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"90-150 Minutes",description:"Walking the South Pasture Loop Trail offers visitors the chance to enjoy a quieter side of the park."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Pecos Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 757-7241"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The grounds of Pecos National Historical Park are open daily from 8:00 am to 4:30 pm. The park is not open on Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, or New Years Day.",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pecos%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/peco",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"el-camino-real-de-tierra-adentro-national-historic-trail",name:"El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3e554224e7862be4.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A6A93-1DD8-B71B-0B25C2DE5079660E.jpg"],shortDescription:"A historic 1,600-mile Royal Road that connected Mexico City with Ohkay Owingeh Pueblo near San Juan, New Mexico.",fullDescription:'El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail, Spanish for "The Royal Road of the Interior Land," commemorates a monumental 1,600-mile trade route that connected Mexico City with Ohkay Owingeh Pueblo in northern New Mexico from 1598 to 1882. As the oldest and longest continuously used European-established trade route in North America, it facilitated three centuries of commerce, cultural exchange, and migration. Today, visitors can explore historic town squares, parajes (campsites), river crossings, and trail ruts across New Mexico and Texas.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Travel along El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail to experience and learn from a complicated legacy of 300 years of conflict, cooperation, and cultural exchange between a variety of empires—European a"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"El Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=El%20Camino%20Real%20de%20Tierra%20Adentro%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/elca",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"old-spanish-national-historic-trail",name:"Old Spanish National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EAEDB-1DD8-B71B-0BB4910C3A9C67C3.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EABF1-1DD8-B71B-0B7DEEAB877D5337.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EAD83-1DD8-B71B-0B52022335D76519.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EAEDB-1DD8-B71B-0BB4910C3A9C67C3.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EB2EF-1DD8-B71B-0BECD6A6AC240936.jpg"],shortDescription:"Traces the rugged 2,700-mile trade route connecting Santa Fe, New Mexico, with Los Angeles, California, active in the 19th century.",fullDescription:"The Old Spanish National Historic Trail commemorates the grueling 2,700-mile mule-pack trade route linking Santa Fe, New Mexico, with Los Angeles, California, between 1829 and 1848. Traversing highly rugged deserts and canyons across six southwestern states, this trail was the primary corridor for moving high-quality New Mexican woolen goods to California in exchange for horses and mules. Today, the trail is co-managed by the National Park Service and the Bureau of Land Management, offering scenic viewpoints, historic trail markers, and visitor centers along its path.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Old Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"California and New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Old%20Spanish%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/olsp/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"santa-fe-national-historic-trail",name:"Santa Fe National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/55A8E9A9-D7BB-3F66-8EE743A527AE5FED.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5591CACE-D5FE-92A5-77E2904F1E7F5AD9.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E737A-1DD8-B71B-0B11950104991FA3.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/55A8E9A9-D7BB-3F66-8EE743A527AE5FED.jpg"],shortDescription:"A historic 900-mile international commercial highway that connected Missouri with Santa Fe, New Mexico, from 1821 to 1880.",fullDescription:"The Santa Fe National Historic Trail protects the historic 900-mile commercial highway that linked Franklin, Missouri, with Santa Fe, New Mexico, during the 19th century. Initiated by trader William Becknell in 1821, the trail became a bustling international trade route connecting the United States with Mexico, and later a vital military highway during the Mexican-American War. Visitors can hike historic trail segments, view massive wagon-rut corridors (such as at Windlass Hill), and explore historic forts, trail camps, and historic plazas.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the Santa Fe Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:`Travel the trail today and you can almost hear the whoops and cries of "All's set!" as trail hands hitched their oxen to freight wagons carrying cargo between western Missouri and Santa Fe, New Mexico. Between 1821 and 1`}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Santa Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Missouri and New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Santa%20Fe%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/safe/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"butterfield-overland-national-historic-trail",name:"Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A0402204-FBD7-DC23-F626D64AEE7FE482.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A0402204-FBD7-DC23-F626D64AEE7FE482.jpg"],shortDescription:"Traces the expansive 19th-century overland mail and passenger route connecting the eastern United States with San Francisco.",fullDescription:"The Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail commemorates the historic 2,800-mile stagecoach route established by John Butterfield in 1857 to deliver overland mail and passengers between St. Louis, Missouri/Memphis, Tennessee, and San Francisco, California. Operating twice a week from 1858 to 1861, the stagecoaches made the arcing southern sweep in an unprecedented 25 days, fostering communication and settlement. Today, the trail crosses eight states, where visitors can explore preserved stage stations, historic trail segments, and regional visitor centers.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Butterfield Overland Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"New mexico, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Butterfield%20Overland%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/buov/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],"new york":[{id:"castle-clinton-national-monument",name:"Castle Clinton National Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/a1ed181abd8b1dbd.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A historic sandstone fort located in Battery Park that has served as a defense outpost, theater, immigration depot, and public aquarium.",fullDescription:"Castle Clinton National Monument, situated in Battery Park at the southern tip of Manhattan, is a circular sandstone fort completed in 1811 to prevent British invasion. Over the past two centuries, the structure has served several iconic roles: an entertainment venue (Castle Garden), the nation's very first immigrant landing depot (welcoming over 8 million people before Ellis Island opened), and the city's beloved public aquarium. Today, the monument serves as the ticketing center for ferries to the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island, offering visitors historical exhibits and stunning harbor views.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Castle Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(212) 329-9979"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Castle Clinton is open daily except Thanksgiving day and Christmas Day.",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Castle%20Clinton%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cacl",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"fort-stanwix-national-monument",name:"Fort Stanwix National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2ED9866F-0D28-4E68-AB932F1C54C0D14A.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A fully reconstructed Revolutionary War-era fort that successfully withstood a prolonged British siege in 1777.",fullDescription:'Fort Stanwix National Monument preserves a meticulously reconstructed star-shaped wood-and-earth fort in the heart of Rome, New York. Known during the Revolutionary War as "the fort that never surrendered," Fort Stanwix successfully withstood a crucial 21-day siege by British, German, and loyalist forces in 1777, helping secure the American victory at Saratoga. The fort also played a major role in early relations with Native Nations, serving as the signing site of the Treaty of Fort Stanwix in 1768 and 1784.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Walk the Mohawk River or the Erie Canal",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"30-180 Minutes",description:"Did you know that even urban parks have hiking trails? Explore the beautiful and historic Mohawk River and Erie Canal in this unique environment. Enjoy the quiet of nature where you never expected it; in the heart of the"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(315) 338-7730"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The cost of admission to the park is FREE. Park trails and lawn are open from sunrise to sunset everyday.",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Stanwix%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fost",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"governors-island-national-monument",name:"Governors Island National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85A2A1-1DD8-B71B-0B50FD84825F4474.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/14F6F12D-D98B-A5BE-50BF4FD9277B0DA4.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves a former military headquarters featuring two major early 19th-century fortifications, Castle Williams and Fort Jay.",fullDescription:"Governors Island National Monument protects 22 acres of historic land situated in New York Harbor, centering on Castle Williams and Fort Jay. Built between 1796 and 1811 as part of the First and Second American Systems of coastal defense, these impressive masonry fortifications protected New York City from naval attack and later served as a military prison and barracks. Today, the island is a peaceful public oasis accessible by a short ferry ride from Manhattan or Brooklyn, offering walking tours, exhibits, and stunning panoramic views of the city skyline.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Governors Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(212) 210-6866"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Fort Jay will be closed for the winter. It will re-open in April",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Governors%20Island%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gois",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"statue-of-liberty-national-monument",name:"Statue of Liberty National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85443B-1DD8-B71B-0B4E0A1FFCCDEEC0.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85443B-1DD8-B71B-0B4E0A1FFCCDEEC0.jpg"],shortDescription:"The iconic symbol of freedom standing in New York Harbor, including the historical immigrant portal of Ellis Island.",fullDescription:"Located on Liberty Island in New York Harbor, the Statue of Liberty National Monument is a colossal neoclassical sculpture designed by Frédéric-Auguste Bartholdi and gifted by the people of France in 1886. It stands as a universal symbol of freedom, hope, and democracy. The monument unit also includes historic Ellis Island, the nation's premier immigration station from 1892 to 1954, where over 12 million aspiring citizens took their first steps on American soil. Today, visitors can tour the museums on both islands, ascend to Liberty's crown, and explore deep family ancestral logs.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Statue Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(212) 363-3200"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Access to Liberty and Ellis Islands is by Statue City Cruises ferry only. For ferry departure times and ticketing information visit http://www.statuecitycruises.com or call 1-877-LADY-TIX.",address:"New jersey and New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Statue%20of%20Liberty%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/stli",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"harriet-tubman-national-historical-park",name:"Harriet Tubman National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3FE26B89-1DD8-B71B-0B7E863F6BA073DA.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3FE26B89-1DD8-B71B-0B7E863F6BA073DA.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the home, farm, and church of legendary Underground Railroad conductor Harriet Tubman in Auburn.",fullDescription:"Harriet Tubman National Historical Park in Auburn, New York, commemorates the extraordinary life and legacy of the legendary Underground Railroad conductor, abolitionist, Civil War scout, and suffragist. Established in partnership with the Harriet Tubman Home Inc. and the AME Zion Church, the park protects Tubman's personal 32-acre farm, her brick home, the Tubman Home for the Aged, and the Thompson Memorial African Methodist Episcopal Zion Church where she worshipped. It offers visitors a deep, personal window into her decades of humanitarian work and family life.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Harriet Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(315) 835-5945"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Harriet Tubman helped raise funds to build the Thompson Memorial African Methodist Episcopal Zion Church at 49 Parker Street. The church is open for self-guided tours during open hours, while the parsonage serves as a visitor contact statio",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harriet%20Tubman%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hart",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"saratoga-national-historical-park",name:"Saratoga National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3508F2F2-B4D0-C495-B4BA6182A74FA2F2.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/34E22962-E08A-6B93-19B49C826959B153.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3508F2F2-B4D0-C495-B4BA6182A74FA2F2.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C856720-1DD8-B71B-0B0C53DBAC6F43A4.jpg"],shortDescription:"The site of the critical 1777 Battles of Saratoga, considered the turning point of the American Revolutionary War.",fullDescription:"Saratoga National Historical Park preserves the rolling hills and battlegrounds where American forces defeated a British army in the autumn of 1777. This momentous victory convinced France to enter the war as an official ally, securing vital financial and military aid that ultimately led to American independence. The park features a self-guided 10-mile driving tour of the battlefield, the historic Philip Schuyler House, the unique boot monument commemorating Benedict Arnold, and the massive Saratoga Monument rising 154 feet above the Hudson River valley.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Wilkinson Trail",difficulty:"Challenging",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"Saratoga National Historical Park preserves the sites associated with the 1777 Battles of Saratoga. Follow the footsteps of those before you as you hike along hallowed ground."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Saratoga Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(518) 670-2985"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The tour road is open to vehicles Apr. 1 through Nov.",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Saratoga%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sara",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"eleanor-roosevelt-national-historic-site",name:"Eleanor Roosevelt National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7CAE9A2C-B352-222A-CCF3649457EC357F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A8D74EA7-92B0-F367-16BEFCE757BF5073.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves Val-Kill, Eleanor Roosevelt's beloved private cottage estate and the only National Historic Site dedicated to a First Lady.",fullDescription:`Eleanor Roosevelt National Historic Site preserves "Val-Kill," the tranquil stone cottage and rural estate in Hyde Park, New York, that served as the private home of First Lady, diplomat, and human rights champion Eleanor Roosevelt. Originally built as a furniture workshop for Val-Kill Industries, the property became Eleanor's sanctuary where she hosted world leaders, worked on the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, and entertained family and friends. Today, visitors can tour her modestly furnished home, walk her scenic woodland trails, and learn about her immense global legacy.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eleanor's Walk",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1 Hour",description:"Get to know Eleanor Roosevelt—wife, mother, and grandmother; Hyde Park neighbor, activist, and diplomat—as you hike the paths she walked daily for recreation and quiet contemplation. See the historic buildings that hoste"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Eleanor Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(845) 229-9422"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Eleanor Roosevelt's Val-Kill Cottage is open in the summer and fall months by guided tour only. Tickets for tours are FREE, but tour spaces are limited and distributed in-person on a first come, first served basis due to the intimate size o",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Eleanor%20Roosevelt%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/elro",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"home-of-franklin-d-roosevelt-national-historic-site",name:"Home of Franklin D. Roosevelt National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/581cd15595ed6f52.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The birthplace, home, and final resting place of Franklin D. Roosevelt, the 32nd President of the United States.",fullDescription:`The Home of Franklin D. Roosevelt National Historic Site preserves "Springwood," the historic estate in Hyde Park, New York, where FDR was born, spent his life, and lies buried. Springwood served as the political and emotional center of Roosevelt's world, where he hosted famous dignitaries like Winston Churchill and King George VI. The site includes his magnificent home, the expansive park grounds, and the Franklin D. Roosevelt Presidential Library and Museum, which houses the official papers and personal collections from his presidency.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Home Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(845) 229-9115"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $15; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"From Memorial Day to the end of October, tours of the Home of FDR are offered daily. The first tour starts at 9:30 AM.",address:"New york, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Home%20of%20Franklin%20D.%20Roosevelt%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hofr/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"kate-mullany-national-historic-site",name:"Kate Mullany National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/ae77b942f5438beb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the home of Kate Mullany, a young Irish immigrant who founded the nation's first all-female labor union in 1864.",fullDescription:"The Kate Mullany National Historic Site in Troy, New York, preserves the brick apartment home of Kate Mullany, a courageous 19-year-old Irish immigrant laundry worker. In 1864, Mullany organized and led the Collar Laundry Union, the first sustained, all-female labor union in the United States, which successfully campaigned for higher wages and safer working conditions. Her home stands as a powerful monument to the historic contributions of women, immigrants, and organized labor in shaping the industrial and social landscape of the United States.",thingsToDo:["Sightseein