4388D9102A08E.jpg"],shortDescription:"A premier long-distance hiking trail stretching over 4,800 miles across eight northern states from North Dakota to Vermont.",fullDescription:"The North Country National Scenic Trail is the longest National Scenic Trail in the United States, spanning over 4,800 miles across eight states from Vermont to North Dakota. The trail traverses a spectacular array of landscapes, including the lakes, forests, prairies, and historic canals of the American North. In North Dakota, the trail crosses rugged prairies, badlands, and water reservoirs like Lake Sakakawea, connecting hikers to the beauty and wildness of the northern Great Plains while promoting outdoor recreation and trail conservation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"North Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(616) 330-2456"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"North dakota and Vermont, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=North%20Country%20National%20Scenic%20Trail%20(official%20unit)",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/noco/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"international-peace-garden",name:"International Peace Garden",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/30ed9bb5eeb3bd9c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A massive park straddling the U.S.–Canada border, celebrating peaceful relations with formal gardens, lakes, and a Peace Chapel.",fullDescription:"The International Peace Garden is a unique 2,339-acre park located directly on the international border between North Dakota and Manitoba, Canada. Founded in 1932, the garden stands as a living symbol of peace and enduring friendship between the two nations. The beautifully landscaped grounds feature spectacular floral displays, formal gardens, peaceful lakes, the iconic Peace Towers, a multi-denominational Peace Chapel, and a conservatory housing thousands of cacti. It serves as a global gathering place for cultural festivals, concerts, and youth camps promoting peace and international cooperation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"International Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(701) 263-4390"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"North dakota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=International%20Peace%20Garden",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/inpe/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"lewis-clark-national-historic-trail",name:"Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FA29AF56-DF2B-8692-40F8422E38FB9093.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F1E53C07-CAC7-1E47-F3002F9102E59BA6.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F21788EE-E877-C00C-257B0EB8AD8F49DB.jpg"],shortDescription:"The Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail winds nearly 4,900 miles through the homelands of more than 60 Tribal nations. It follows the historic outbound and inbound routes of the Lewis and Clark Expedition of 1803-1806 from Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania to the Pacific Ocean. Follow the trail to find the people, places, and stories that make up the complex legacy of the expedition.",fullDescription:"Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. The Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail winds nearly 4,900 miles through the homelands of more than 60 Tribal nations. It follows the historic outbound and inbound routes of the Lewis and Clark Expedition of 1803-1806 from Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania to the Pacific Ocean. Follow the trail to find the people, places, and stories that make up the complex legacy of the expedition. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Slough Trail",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"The South Slough Trail is a three mile loop that can be accessed from the Fort to Sea Trailhead or from near the North Netul Landing that features twist and turns and steep changes in elevation among large trees and a bo"},{name:"Clay Pit Pond Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"15-60 Minutes",description:"The Clay Pit Pond is a half mile loop primitive trail with access at the Fort Clatsop Visitor Center picnic area. It is a great trail for those looking for a short but dynamic hike. The trail features a vernal pond surro"},{name:"Netul River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"20-60 Minutes",description:"Take an easy approximately 1 mile stroll along the beautiful Netul River Trail. Watch for water birds, eagles and sometimes otters! Along the way you can learn about the history of logging and commerce along the Lewis an"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis & Clark Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(402) 808-2714"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail Visitor Center is located on the Missouri River in Omaha, Nebraska.",address:"North dakota, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lewis%20%26%20Clark%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lecl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],"northern mariana islands":[{id:"american-memorial-park",name:"American Memorial Park",shortDescription:"Honors the American and local sacrifices made during the Marianas Campaign of World War II.",fullDescription:"American Memorial Park, located on the island of Saipan in the Northern Mariana Islands, honors those who died in the World War II Marianas Campaign, specifically the battles of Saipan and Tinian. Dedicated in 1994, the park is a living memorial that features a flag monument, a memorial wall inscribed with the names of over 5,000 military and local lives lost, and a world-class visitor center with state-of-the-art historical exhibitions. Additionally, the park contains beautiful sandy beaches, mangrove forests, sports facilities, and the Micro Beach area, making it a peaceful and educational sanctuary for both visitors and residents.",thingsToDo:["Viewing Memorial Wall","Visiting Court of Honor","Historical Museum Tour","Micro Beach Walking","Swimming and Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mangrove Forest Boardwalk",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"An accessible wooden boardwalk winding through coastal mangrove forests, perfect for bird-watching."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"American Memorial Park Visitor Center",info:"Features extensive WWII exhibits, an orientation film, and a museum store.",phone:"(670) 234-7207",hours:"9:00 AM - 4:00 PM Tuesday-Saturday"},rangerPrograms:["Marianas Campaign WWII Ranger Talk","Saipan Coastal Ecology Walk"],campingInfo:"No camping allowed on the memorial park grounds.",wildlife:["Marianas Fruit Bats","Golden White-eyes","Micronesian Starlings","Turtles","Reef fish"],bestViewpoints:["Court of Honor Monument","Micro Beach Shoreline"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Micro Beach Road, Garapan, Saipan 96950",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=American+Memorial+Park+Saipan",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/amme/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"December to April (dry season)",accessibilityInfo:"Fully accessible visitor center, restrooms, and paved paths. Mangrove boardwalk is also fully accessible.",travelTips:["Take time to watch the 20-minute historical documentary in the visitor center theater.","Respect the memorials and maintain decorum on the grounds."],nearbyAttractions:["Garapan Shopping District","Banzai Cliff","Suicide Cliff"],designation:"National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8576F5-1DD8-B71B-0B2A1CF96D1F9701.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8576F5-1DD8-B71B-0B2A1CF96D1F9701.jpg"]}],ohio:[{id:"cuyahoga-valley-national-park",name:"Cuyahoga Valley National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F7E15C5E-AF19-2C8B-7BC519C9BBDB06CC.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F7E15C5E-AF19-2C8B-7BC519C9BBDB06CC.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the scenic, winding Cuyahoga River Valley between Cleveland and Akron, featuring lush forests, waterfalls, and the historic Ohio & Erie Canal.",fullDescription:"Cuyahoga Valley National Park preserves the scenic and historic valley along the Cuyahoga River in northeastern Ohio. Nestled between the metropolitan areas of Cleveland and Akron, the park is a vital urban refuge featuring deep gorges, dense hardwood forests, beautiful waterfalls like Brandywine Falls, and a rich variety of local wildlife. It also protects the historic route of the Ohio & Erie Canal Towpath Trail, where visitors can bike, hike, or ride the scenic Cuyahoga Valley Scenic Railroad through tranquil natural landscapes.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Brandywine Gorge Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"The Brandywine Gorge Loop circles a deep ravine carved by Brandywine Cre