 African American military attaché and national park superintendent.",fullDescription:"Charles Young Buffalo Soldiers National Monument preserves the historic home Youngsholme of Colonel Charles Young in Wilberforce, Ohio. Born into slavery, Young overcame deep systemic racism to become the third African American graduate of West Point, the first Black military attaché, and the first Black superintendent of a national park (Sequoia). He also served as a legendary commander of the Buffalo Soldiers. The monument honors his distinguished military career, his leadership in civil rights, and the courage and sacrifices of the historic Buffalo Soldiers who served the nation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"497 Mile Virtual Running and Bicycling Club",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"In 1918, Colonel Charles Young completed a physically and mentally challenging 497 horseback ride from Wilberforce, Ohio to Washington D.C. in 16 days to prove his physical fitness for military combat duty. Today, we inv"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Charles Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(937) 352-6757"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Young family home, Youngsholm, has reopened after a multi-year restoration. Contact the park by email at chyo_info@nps.gov if you have questions or concerns.",address:"Ohio, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Charles%20Young%20Buffalo%20Soldiers%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/chyo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Sp