-famous for its incredible clarity, intense royal blue color, and pristine water. Visitors can drive the 33-mile Rim Drive, hike to panoramic peaks, take boat tours to Wizard Island, and marvel at the steep volcanic cliffs rising from the peaceful water.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Crater Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(541) 594-3000"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Private Vehicle $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Oregon, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Crater%20Lake%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/crla/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"john-day-fossil-beds-national-monument",name:"John Day Fossil Beds National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C848785-1DD8-B71B-0B846784B96C76EE.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8488CD-1DD8-B71B-0B29E5280AE3C784.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/36CBD01D-D6DA-164D-F767964B009FF642.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves world-class layers of fossil plants and animals in the colorful, semi-arid hills of east-central Oregon.",fullDescription:"John Day Fossil Beds National Monument in east-central Oregon is a world-class paleontological site spanning three distinct geographic units: Sheep Rock, Painted Hills, and Clarno. The monument protects beautifully preserved plant and animal fossils spanning over 40 million years of the Cenozoic Era. The striking, multi-colored clay layers of the Painted Hills unit present a vibrant landscape that changes color depending on light and moisture, while the Thomas Condon Paleontology Center provides visitor education and showcases incredible prehistoric specimens.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"John Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(541) 987-2333"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Oregon, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=John%20Day%20Fossil%20Beds%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/joda/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"oregon-caves-national-monument-and-preserve",name:"Oregon Caves National Monument and Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8507A5-1DD8-B71B-0B921718CA5479A5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C850A42-1DD8-B71B-0B1597D0AFDDF25F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2D6F089A-EA59-3EBD-76E36A701B6D8F91.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4372CFA7-A075-F5CE-F3A8B54DA0DD6A42.jpg"],shortDescription:"A majestic underground world of marble caverns carved by water, nestled deep within the Siskiyou Mountains of southwestern Oregon.",fullDescription:"Oregon Caves National Monument and Preserve, located in the Siskiyou Mountains of southwestern Oregon, features a rare and complex network of solution caves dissolved out of solid marble bedrock. Known as the Marble Halls of Oregon, the caverns are adorned with unique flowstones, stalactites, and stalagmites. Above ground, the preserve protects a pristine old-growth forest ecosystem with hiking trails, mountain streams, and the historic Oregon Caves Chateau, offering a wonderful combination of subterranean exploration and mountain recreation.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Scenic Drives","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Oregon Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(541) 592-5125"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes","Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Time