onal Historical Park, located near Astoria, Oregon, protects several historic sites associated with the Corps of Discovery's arrival at the Pacific Ocean. The park centers on Fort Clatsop, a reconstructed log fort representing the exact site where Meriwether Lewis, William Clark, and their expedition spent the wet winter of 1805-1806. Visitors can explore the fort replica, watch historical costumed demonstrations, hike coastal trails to spectacular ocean viewpoints, and learn about the interactions between the explorers and the local Clatsop and Chinook people.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks","Guided Ranger Tours","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Slough Trail",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"The South Slough Trail is a three mile loop that can be accessed from the Fort to Sea Trailhead or from near the North Netul Landing that features twist and turns and steep changes in elevation among large trees and a bo"},{name:"Kwis Kwis Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-5 Hours",description:"The Kwis Kwis Trail is a 3.6 miles one way trail that can be combined with the Fort to Sea to form loops. 2.7 miles - Fort to Sea west to 1st Kwis Kwis connector to Kwis Kwis to the Fort to Sea Trailhead. 5.3 miles - For"},{name:"Clay Pit Pond Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"15-60 Minutes",description:"The Clay Pit Pond is a half mile loop primitive trail with access at the Fort Clatsop Visitor Center picnic area. It is a great trail for those looking for a short but dynamic hike. The trail features a vernal pond surro"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(503) 861-2471"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior 