he "Father of Oregon," who generously aided early American pioneers settling the territory. The site explores the complex convergence of British fur traders, American settlers, and Native American tribes in shaping the Pacific Northwest.`,thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(360) 816-6230"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Standard operating hours for Vancouver, Washington facilities are Tuesday through Saturday, 9:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.",address:"Oregon and Washington, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Vancouver%20National%20Historic%20SiteFort%20VancouverMcLoughlin%20HouseVancouver%20National%20Historic%20Reserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fova/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"oregon-national-historic-trail",name:"Oregon National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D9108-1DD8-B71B-0BE55F68DE91648F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7D8DD1-1DD8-B71B-0B83B71E8F57AF65.jpg"],shortDescription:"Traces the historic 2,000-mile emigrant trail used by over 300,000 pioneers to journey from Missouri to the fertile Willamette Valley of Oregon.",fullDescription:"The Oregon National Historic Trail spans over 2,000 miles across six states, commemorating the massive overland migration of early American pioneers during the mid-19th century. From the 1840s to the 1860s, more than 300,000 settlers walked, rode, and drove wagons across rugged plains and mountain passes to reach the promise of fertile farmland in Oregon's Willamette Valley. Today, the trail preserves historic wagon ruts, campsites, and pioneer registers, allowing modern travelers to discover the incredible courage, hardships, and perseverance of the emigrants.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the Oregon Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Imagine yourself an emigrant headed for Oregon: would promises of lush farmlands and a new beginning lure you to leave home and walk for weeks? Travel the Oregon National Historic Trail in six states and be reminded of t"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Oregon Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Missouri and Oregon, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Oregon%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/oreg/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"california-national-historic-trail",name:"California National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7987-1DD8-B71B-0B3C3123ED8F686B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7CDA-1DD8-B71B-0B999C4AC7A9D37C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7E5D-1DD8-B71B-0B0A1BE6987E18AF.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B7FCF-1DD8-B71B-0BA502EF5988BC47.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/56ACD550-C0CE-423B-9448A9D0ABCDD9E7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. The California National Historic Trail is over 5,000 miles long and covers portions of 10 states. Step into history along more than 1,000 miles of ruts and traces from travelers and their overland wagons.",fullDescription:"California National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. The California National Historic Trail is over 5,000 miles long and covers portions of 10 states. Step into history along more than 1,000 miles of ruts and traces from travelers and their overland wagons. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the California Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Follow in the footsteps of over 250,000 emigrants who traveled to the gold fields and rich farmlands of California during the 1840s and 1850s: the greatest mass migration in American history. There are a variety of hikin"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"California Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for California National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Oregon, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=California%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cali/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"ice-age-floods-national-geologic-trail",name:"Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A003C5CE-E0EB-2AC6-FC8EDC134B1DFD19.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/0E7BEDF2-952B-D9E0-038D808912A45A65.jpeg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/D4C33AFB-B84F-E87C-E19705F8380417D1.jpeg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/0BDAB952-1DD8-B71B-0B509D5898489BD4.jpg"],shortDescription:"A scenic driving route that traces the dramatic path of catastrophic glacial floods that swept across the Pacific Northwest during the last Ice Age.",fullDescription:"The Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail is a unique, multi-state route that guides visitors through the dramatic and cataclysmic geological history of the Pacific Northwest. Between 15,000 and 18,000 years ago, massive glacial ice dams repeatedly failed, releasing torrents of floodwaters that sculpted the Columbia River Gorge, carved out the Willamette Valley, and forever transformed the landscapes of Montana, Idaho, Washington, and Oregon. Today, visitors can explore giant ripples, deep coulees, and massive erratics left behind by these incredible floods.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ice Age Floods Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Ice Age Floods National Geologic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(509) 237-9722"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Oregon, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ice%20Age%20Floods%20National%20Geologic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/iafl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],pennsylvania:[{id:"carlisle-federal-indian-boarding-school-national-monument",name:"Carlisle Federal Indian Boarding School National Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7ab97a3e41babc2b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The site of the historic Carlisle Indian Industrial School, the flagship Indian boarding school from 1879 to 1918, situated in Carlisle, Pennsylvania.",fullDescription:'The Carlisle Indian Industrial School, located on the historic grounds of the Carlisle Barracks in Carlisle, Pennsylvania, was the premier federally funded off-reservation Indian boarding school, operating from 1879 to 1918. Founded by Richard Henry Pratt, the school aimed to forcibly assimilate Native American children from over 140 tribes into Euro-American culture under the philosophy "Kill the Indian, Save the Man." Today, the site is a National Historic Landmark, serving as a solemn place of reflection, remembrance, and education regarding the complex, painful history of federal boarding school policies and their lasting impact on Indigenous families.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Carlisle Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(717) 245-3121"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Obser