htseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"First Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(302) 478-2769"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"The New Castle Court House Museum is open Wednesday - Saturday 10:00 a.m. to 3:30 p.m., and Sunday 1:30 p.m.",address:"Delaware and Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=First%20State%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/frst/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"independence-national-historical-parkgermantown-white-houseindependence-hallnational-constitution-center-partner-site-thaddeus-kosciuszko-national-memorial",name:"Independence National Historical ParkGermantown White HouseIndependence HallNational Constitution Center (partner site)Thaddeus Kosciuszko National Memorial",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C59AAC97-CCCF-45C3-79E94A603D968CC6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7AFE0115-FFFB-4779-4787C9D078489E1C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80F321-1DD8-B71B-0B30426D759187FC.jpg"],shortDescription:"The birthplace of American democracy, preserving Independence Hall, the Liberty Bell, and many historic sites in Philadelphia.",fullDescription:"Independence National Historical Park in Philadelphia preserves several of the nation's most sacred historic sites associated with the American Revolution and the founding of the United States. Centered on Independence Hall, where both the Declaration of Independence and the United States Constitution were debated and signed, the park also encompasses the iconic Liberty Bell Center, the Congress Hall, the Germantown White House, and partner sites like the National Constitution Center. Visitors can walk the historic cobblestone streets, discovering the brilliant ideas, fierce debates, and diverse people who shaped the American republic.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Independence Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(215) 965-2305"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Timed Entry Reservation - Location $1 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Independence National Historical Park operates daily from 9:00 am to 5:00 pm. Hours of operation for certain historical buildings and facilities change seasonally throughout the year.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Independence%20National%20Historical%20ParkGermantown%20White%20HouseIndependence%20HallNational%20Constitution%20Center%20(partner%20site)Thaddeus%20Kosciuszko%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/inde/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"valley-forge-national-historical-park",name:"Valley Forge National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B0AC3B46-F1F9-B2A1-4DA9CE223B32166F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/B0AC3B46-F1F9-B2A1-4DA9CE223B32166F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/477DC760-C260-8CA6-F7919D83F4973A81.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/476F62D7-DDCA-AAAE-26AFDC0A99E7F820.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the heroic, grueling 1777-1778 winter encampment of General George Washington's Continental Army.",fullDescription:"Valley Forge National Historical Park preserves the site and honors the sacrifice of the Continental Army during their grueling winter encampment from December 1777 to June 1778. Under the leadership of General George Washington, the diverse, under-supplied army endured extreme hardships, disease, and starvation, while undergoing rigorous military training under Baron von Steuben. This intensive training transformed them into a cohesive, professional fighting force that ultimately secured victory. Today, the park features reconstructed log huts, historic monuments, Washington's Headquarters, and miles of scenic multi-use trails.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Valley Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Valley Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(610) 783-1000"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds including roads, parking lots, trails, and monuments are open from 7am to dark (30 minutes after sunset) year round. Park grounds remain open on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Years Day.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Valley%20Forge%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/vafo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"allegheny-portage-railroad-national-historic-site",name:"Allegheny Portage Railroad National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C12F155-DE91-DD44-9108A954EB6F3DF6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C2C400A-AA7C-F524-8DE034A012E236DE.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/35902275-1DD8-B71B-0B41A4998139B89F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C263305-C039-15B5-BFAAAB317A69A0DA.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C3E5892-DF3F-B6CF-050662A2DEFDB59C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2CAD3742-E5F2-AEC4-AF86148FCC922056.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic 19th-century engineering marvel that lifted canal boats over the Allegheny Mountains of Pennsylvania.",fullDescription:"Allegheny Portage Railroad National Historic Site preserves the remains of the first railroad built across the formidable Allegheny Mountains. Operating from 1834 to 1857, this incredible engineering system of inclined planes and steam locomotives lifted canal boats out of the water, hauled them over the rugged mountain ridges, and lowered them back into the canal on the other side. This critical link in the Pennsylvania Main Line Canal slashed travel times between Philadelphia and Pittsburgh, fueling early trade and expansion. Today, visitors can explore the Lemon House tavern, a reconstructed engine house, and historic trail paths.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Guided Hikes",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Heritage Hikes are led by a park ranger during the summer months. The distance varies by hike. Reservations are required."},{name:"Hike or bike to the Staple Bend Tunnel",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Staple Bend Tunnel, the first railroad tunnel in America, can be accessed from the trailhead on Beech Hill Road in Mineral Point. Visitors can hike or bike approximately two miles each way to the 901 foot long tunnel"},{name:"Hike the Inclines 6 to 10 Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Inclines 6 to 10 Trail follows the route of the Allegheny Portage Railroad. The hiking sections of the trail are located on or near the original route of the Allegheny Portage Railroad. The bicycle section is located"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Allegheny Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(814) 886-6150"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Visitor Center, Engine House No. 6 Exhibit Shelter and the Lemon House are closed on Wednesdays and Thursdays.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Allegheny%20Portage%20Railroad%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/alpo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"edgar-allan-poe-national-historic-site",name:"Edgar Allan Poe National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AD5D5-1DD8-B71B-0BAF630A8D440C44.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AD5D5-1DD8-B71B-0BAF630A8D440C44.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7ADC91-1DD8-B71B-0BA4AAA889D0F489.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the Philadelphia home where legendary author Edgar Allan Poe penned some of his most famous, spine-chilling stories.",fullDescription:"Edgar Allan Poe National Historic Site preserves the only surviving Philadelphia residence of the legendary American author, who lived here with his family from 1843 to 1844. During his highly productive years in Philadelphia, Poe penned some of his most iconic and spine-chilling tales, including 'The Tell-Tale Heart', 'The Black Cat', and 'The Gold-Bug'. Today, the unfurnished brick home offers an atmospheric glimpse into Poe's brilliant, tortured mind, featuring interactive exhibits, a reading room, and the spooky cellar that inspired some of his most famous horror stories.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Edgar Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(215) 965-2305"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The site is temporarily closed for a utilities improvement project.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Edgar%20Allan%20Poe%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/edal/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"eisenhower-national-historic-site",name:"Eisenhower National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C813B84-1DD8-B71B-0B9D414CDEC98F96.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the peaceful Gettysburg farm and home of General and 34th U.S. President Dwight D. Eisenhower.",fullDescription:"Eisenhower National Historic Site preserves the beautiful home and 690-acre farm of Dwight D. Eisenhower, the 34th President of the United States and Supreme Commander of Allied Forces in Europe during World War II. Located adjacent to the Gettysburg Battlefield, the farm served as a peaceful weekend retreat for the Eisenhowers, a temporary White House during his presidency, and a venue for hosting prominent world leaders like Nikita Khrushchev. Today, visitors can tour the elegantly preserved home, explore the putting green, and discover Eisenhower's military achievements, presidency, and passion for farming.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Eisenhower Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(717) 253-5776"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The grounds are open thirty minutes before sunrise to thirty minutes after sunset. Visit https://sunrise-sunset.org/us/gettysburg-pa.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Eisenhower%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/eise/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"friendship-hill-national-historic-site",name:"Friendship Hill National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/friendship-hill-national-historic-site-pennsylvania.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the elegant stone mansion and estate of Albert Gallatin, the brilliant and longest-serving U.S. Secretary of the Treasury.",fullDescription:"Friendship Hill National Historic Site preserves the beautiful country estate of Albert Gallatin, the Swiss-born diplomat, senator, and brilliant U.S. Secretary of the Treasury who served under Presidents Jefferson and Madison. Nestled along the Monongahela River in southwestern Pennsylvania, the site centers on Gallatin's historic stone and brick mansion, which he built in 1789. The park explores Gallatin's massive contributions to the young republic—including financing the Louisiana Purchase and funding the Lewis and Clark Expedition—while offering quiet hiking trails through the surrounding forests.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Friendship Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(724) 329-2501"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Park Grounds and Trails are open daily from sunrise to sunset, all year. Please note: While the park is open Sunrise to Sunset - the Gallatin House Visitor Center may be closed.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Friendship%20Hill%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/frhi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"hopewell-furnace-national-historic-site",name:"Hopewell Furnace National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/ED657889-A026-6AB1-4CA4B6E1D598793B.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves a beautifully restored 19th-century cold-blast iron furnace community, showcasing early American industrial life.",fullDescription:"Hopewell Furnace National Historic Site preserves a wonderfully complete and restored example of a 19th-century cold-blast charcoal iron furnace community. Founded in 1771 by Mark Bird, Hopewell Furnace produced essential iron products, including cannon and ammunition for the Continental Army during the Revolutionary War, and stove plates and castings for the growing nation. The park's historic buildings—including the furnace, waterwheel, charcoal house, tenant homes, and the ironmaster's mansion—provide a vivid, immersive look into the work, community, and technology of the early American industrial revolution.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Hopewell Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(610) 582-8773"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Hopewell Furnace National Historic Site is open Wednesday through Sunday from 9:00AM to 5:00PM. On days the park is closed, its historic buildings, parking lot and visitor center are unavailable for use, but its trails (which interconnect w",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hopewell%20Furnace%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hofu/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"steamtown-national-historic-site",name:"Steamtown National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8C4271D7-DA2A-91D0-352FDDC192DB531B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8C4271D7-DA2A-91D0-352FDDC192DB531B.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/891601BD-FEEB-C958-5613E65A5D4C2938.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5DDC254B-A747-5596-7F1FD318A51135B3.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves and interprets the history of steam railroading in America, featuring active steam locomotives and historic yards in Scranton.",fullDescription:"Steamtown National Historic Site, located in the heart of downtown Scranton, Pennsylvania, is a living history museum dedicated to preserving and celebrating the legacy of American steam railroading. Built on the historic yards of the Delaware, Lackawanna and Western Railroad, the site features a massive collection of steam locomotives, passenger cars, and freight cars. Visitors can tour an active roundhouse, watch restoration work in progress, explore exhibits on rail technology and social history, and ride a real steam-powered train through the scenic Lackawanna Valley.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Steamtown Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(570) 445-1898"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"The park is open daily from 9:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Steamtown%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/stea/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"gettysburg-national-military-parkgettysburg-national-cemetery",name:"Gettysburg National Military ParkGettysburg National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/C5B413E6-FD8C-00FC-60191939A57FE501.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C819787-1DD8-B71B-0B0D052F23017472.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the site of the pivotal American Civil War Battle of Gettysburg and the Soldiers' National Cemetery where Lincoln delivered the Gettysburg Address.",fullDescription:"Gettysburg National Military Park protects and interprets the sacred ground of the Battle of Gettysburg, fought from July 1 to 3, 1863. Regarded as the turning point of the American Civil War, this massive battle ended General Robert E. Lee's second invasion of the North and resulted in over 51,000 casualties. The park also encompasses the Soldiers' National Cemetery, the final resting place for thousands of Union soldiers and the site where President Abraham Lincoln delivered his legendary Gettysburg Address in November 1863. Today, visitors can explore the battlefield through guided auto tours, monuments, and educational museum exhibits.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Gettysburg Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(717) 334-1124"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"The battlefield and roads are open thirty minutes before sunrise to thirty minutes after sunset. Visitors can plan their visit and obtain a listing of sunrise and sunset times by day in Gettysburg, PA at: https://sunrise-sunset.org/us/getty",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Gettysburg%20National%20Military%20ParkGettysburg%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gett/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"fort-necessity-national-battlefield",name:"Fort Necessity National Battlefield",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F78F68EB-E4CB-1C1A-9A36EE67336D8B60.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the site of the opening battle of the French and Indian War, fought in 1754 under the command of George Washington.",fullDescription:"Fort Necessity National Battlefield preserves the site of the Battle of Fort Necessity, fought on July 3, 1754, in southwestern Pennsylvania. This historic clash between French forces, allied Native Americans, and a young George Washington commanding a small Virginia militia marked the opening battle of the French and Indian War (Seven Years' War). Today, the park features a reconstructed circular stockade fort, the historic Mount Washington Tavern along the National Road, and educational exhibits exploring the geopolitical rivalries and global consequences of this frontier battle.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(724) 329-5805"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Entrance to Fort Necessity National Battlefield is allowed from sunrise until sunset. The park visitor center and Mount Washington Tavern museum have specific hours.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Necessity%20National%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fone/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"benjamin-franklin-national-memorial",name:"Benjamin Franklin National Memorial",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/4cd9e40719a3d9f3.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Honors the legendary inventor, statesman, and founding father Benjamin Franklin with a massive marble statue inside Philadelphia's Franklin Institute.",fullDescription:"The Benjamin Franklin National Memorial features a monumental, 20-foot-tall white marble statue of Benjamin Franklin carved by sculptor James Earle Fraser. Located inside the historic rotunda of The Franklin Institute in Philadelphia, the memorial serves as the nation's premier tribute to the legendary scientist, writer, inventor, and founding father. Designated as an affiliated unit of the National Park Service, the memorial celebrates Franklin's incredible legacy of public service, diplomatic achievements, and scientific discoveries that helped shape modern America.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Benjamin Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(215) 448-1200"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Timed Entry Reservation - Location $1 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Independence National Historical Park operates daily from 9:00 am to 5:00 pm. Hours of operation for certain historical buildings and facilities change seasonally throughout the year.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Benjamin%20Franklin%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/inde/learn/historyculture/places-benjaminfranklinnationalmemorial.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Memorial"},{id:"flight-93-national-memorial",name:"Flight 93 National Memorial",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/04B92119-CFAE-61AB-5B200ECBAE73814B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/0A7DF941-EFE4-1A34-F3BC6DA004CC105E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/04B92119-CFAE-61AB-5B200ECBAE73814B.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85D65B-1DD8-B71B-0B159FDB3CF53843.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85D548-1DD8-B71B-0B8157AABD7A0D42.jpg"],shortDescription:"A solemn memorial honoring the forty passengers and crew members of United Airlines Flight 93 who thwarted a terrorist attack on September 11, 2001.",fullDescription:"Flight 93 National Memorial is a deeply moving, sacred tribute located in Shanksville, Pennsylvania, at the crash site of United Airlines Flight 93. On September 11, 2001, forty brave passengers and crew members coordinated a heroic defense, fighting back against hijackers to prevent an attack on the U.S. Capitol. The memorial features the monumental Tower of Voices housing forty wind chimes, the elegant white marble Wall of Names tracing the flight's path, a visitor center detailing the morning's events, and the serene, protected crash site itself, offering a quiet space for reflection and gratitude.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Flight Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(814) 893-6322"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Park grounds and trails are open 7 days a week, 365 days a year, from sunrise to sunset. Harsh winter weather may result in delayed openings and closures.",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Flight%2093%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/flni/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Memorial"},{id:"johnstown-flood-national-memorial",name:"Johnstown Flood National Memorial",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/375DC9EB-E925-0C6E-A3260A803419E0B0.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2ACE78E3-DE22-8056-46CBE5BB9E96122D.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the devastating 1889 Johnstown Flood, caused by the collapse of the South Fork Dam, which claimed over 2,200 lives.",fullDescription:"Johnstown Flood National Memorial preserves the remains of the historic South Fork Dam, whose catastrophic collapse on May 31, 1889, unleashed a massive 20-million-ton wall of water down the Little Conemaugh River valley. The resulting flood devastated the industrial city of Johnstown, Pennsylvania, claiming 2,209 lives in one of the worst civil disasters in American history. The memorial protects the dam ruins, the historic clubhouse of the exclusive South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club, and features a visitor center with a powerful documentary film and exhibits on the disaster and the massive relief efforts.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Carriage Road Nature Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Carriage Road Nature Trail follows the carriage road that the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club members would have taken after they crossed the top of the South Fork Dam on their journey from South Fork to the Club"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Johnstown Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(814) 886-6171"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:`Hours of Operations
The Visitor Center is closed on Wednesdays and Thursdays. The Visitor Center is open Fridays through Tuesdays from 9:00 a.m.`,address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Johnstown%20Flood%20National%20Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/jofl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Memorial"},{id:"thaddeus-kosciuszko-national-memorial",name:"Thaddeus Kosciuszko National Memorial",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7ADDD2-1DD8-B71B-0B128C61D6236420.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7ADF1B-1DD8-B71B-0BDD41F7E4C51CD8.jpg"],shortDescription:"Honors the life and contributions of the brilliant Polish military engineer and champion of human rights who helped win the American Revolution.",fullDescription:"Thaddeus Kosciuszko National Memorial is the smallest unit of the National Park System, preserving the Philadelphia boarding house where the Polish patriot and military engineer resided from 1797 to 1798. Kosciuszko played a critical role in the American Revolutionary War, designing essential fortifications at West Point and Saratoga. A passionate advocate for freedom and human rights, his will directed that his American estate be used to free and educate enslaved people. The memorial features beautifully restored rooms and exhibits celebrating his military genius and lifelong dedication to liberty.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Thaddeus Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing ma