onal Scenic Trail in the United States, spanning over 4,800 miles across eight northern states from North Dakota to Vermont.",fullDescription:"The North Country National Scenic Trail is the longest National Scenic Trail in the United States, spanning over 4,800 miles across eight states from Vermont to North Dakota. The trail traverses a spectacular array of landscapes, including the lakes, forests, prairies, and historic canals of the American North. Today, the trail connects hikers to the beauty and wildness of the northern Great Plains while promoting outdoor recreation and trail conservation.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"North Country Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for North Country National Scenic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(616) 319-7906"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=North%20Country%20National%20Scenic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/noco/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"washington-rochambeau-revolutionary-route-national-historic-trail",name:"Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3049B5BE-921E-7B15-7E1E4D6BD21442AB.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/31648791-F4D9-4CA7-3CD80631C81422B7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Follows the historic 680-mile land route taken by the allied French and American armies to victory at Yorktown in 1781.",fullDescription:"The Washington-Rochambeau National Historic Trail commemorates the historic march of George Washington's American forces and Jean-Baptiste de Rochambeau's French army during the American Revolutionary War. Stretching 680 miles through nine states—including Massachusetts—the trail marks the allied path that led to the decisive siege and victory at Yorktown, Virginia. Today, visitors can explore historic taverns, campsites, and roads, discovering the incredible logistics, cooperation, and international alliance that secured American independence.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"},{name:"Become a B.A.R.K. Ranger!",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Become a B.A.R.K and P.U.R.R.K Ranger at Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Routes National Historic Trai."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(215) 597-7013"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Pennsylvania, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Washington-Rochambeau%20Revolutionary%20Route%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/waro/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"}],"puerto rico":[{id:"san-juan-national-historic-site",name:"San Juan National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AF5C5-1DD8-B71B-0B40514DB1A21024.jpg?maxwidth=800&autorotate=false",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7AF5C5-1DD8-B71B-0B40514DB1A21024.jpg"],shortDescription:"San Juan National Historic Site protects the massive 16th-century fortifications of Castillo San Felipe del Morro and Castillo San Cristóbal, which guarded the entrance to San Juan Bay. These imposing structures are UNESCO World Heritage sites representing centuries of military history and Spanish colonial influence in the Caribbean.",fullDescription:`Established in 1949, this site encompasses the most iconic Spanish colonial defenses in the Americas, including the massive Castillo San Felipe del Morro, the smaller Castillo San Cristóbal, and sections of the historic city walls. These fortifications were built by the Spanish empire to protect the strategic port of San Juan from European rivals and pirates, playing a pivotal role in the defense of the Caribbean for over 400 years. The site features intricate military architecture, expansive parade grounds, dungeon tunnels, and gun batteries that overlook the Atlantic Ocean and the harbor entrance.

The site is characterized by its dramatic cliffside geography, constructed from local limestone and volcanic rock. Beyond its military significance, the site serves as a vital cultural bridge, preserving the architectural legacy of the Spanish presence in the Caribbean. Visitors can walk the massive walls, explore the labyrinthine tunnels of San Cristóbal, and observe how these fortifications integrated with the natural landscape to form one of the most formidable defensive systems ever constructed in the New World.`,thingsToDo:["Explore the six levels of Castillo San Felipe del Morro","Walk the expansive tunnels and trenches of Castillo San Cristóbal","Stroll the Paseo de la Princesa along the historic city walls","Participate in a guided ranger-led architectural history walk","Photograph the iconic lighthouse atop El Morro"],hikingTrails:[{name:"The Esplanade Walk",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A scenic, grassy walk from the main entrance of Old San Juan across the vast field leading to the gates of El Morro."},{name:"City Walls Perimeter Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A walk following the historic fortifications from San Cristóbal toward the harbor, offering ocean views and historic cannon emplacements."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Castillo San Cristóbal Visitor Center",info:"The primary visitor center provides orientation, park maps, and introductory films about the history of the site.",phone:"787-729-6777",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Junior Ranger Program","Fortress History Walking Tours","Military Engineering Demonstrations"],campingInfo:"There is no camping allowed within the San Juan National Historic Site.",wildlife:["Green Iguanas","Brown Pelicans","Magnificent Frigatebirds","Feral Cats (part of a protected community program)"],bestViewpoints:["The Main Plaza of El Morro overlooking the Atlantic Ocean","The highest tier of San Cristóbal looking over the San Juan Bay"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"San Juan National Historic Site is open year-round except for Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, and New Year's Day.",address:"501 Calle Norzagaray, San Juan, PR 00901",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=San+Juan+National+Historic+Site+Visitor+Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/saju/",bestTimeToVisit:"December through April offers the driest and most pleasant temperatures for outdoor exploration.",accessibilityInfo:"Much of the site features steep inclines, uneven historic masonry, and narrow stairways which may be challenging for mobility devices; however, main level areas are paved and partially accessible.",travelTips:["Wear comfortable walking shoes with good traction for uneven stone surfaces.","Carry plenty of water and sunscreen, as there is very little shade on the fort ramparts.","Use the free Old San Juan trolley for transport between the two main fort locations."],nearbyAttractions:["La Fortaleza","Catedral Basilica Menor de San Juan Bautista","Museo de las Américas"],designation:"National Historic Site"}],"rhode island":[{id:"blackstone-river-valley-national-historical-park",name:"Blackstone River Valley National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7BD04E-1DD8-B71B-0B3A6A73F3DA415B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7BD04E-1DD8-B71B-0B3A6A73F3DA415B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Celebrates the birthplace of the American Industrial Revolution, preserving the historic rivers, mill towns, and canals of the valley.",fullDescription:"Blackstone River Valley National Historical Park protects the historic sites and landscapes of the Blackstone Valley, where Samuel Slater's mill initiated the American Industrial Revolution in 1790. This unique park features a collection of historic mill villages, canals, and river corridors in Massachusetts and Rhode Island. Visitors can learn about the rapid transformation from agriculture to manufacturing, explore scenic waterways, bike along the canal paths, and discover the rich social and economic history of the industrial age.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Winter Hikes",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"60-90 Minutes",description:"Join a Park Ranger for weekly hikes at varying locations in the Blackstone River Valley from December 19, 2026-February 13, 2027. Hikes start at 10:00 AM on Saturday mornings. See listing for details."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Blackstone Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(401) 725-8638"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:`The park grounds are open from Sunrise to Sunset. The headquarters are located at Slater Mill
