(401) 725-8638`,address:"Massachusetts and Rhode island, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Blackstone%20River%20Valley%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/blrv/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"touro-synagogue-national-historic-site",name:"Touro Synagogue National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82D5FA-1DD8-B71B-0B69EBF05C11248B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82D49E-1DD8-B71B-0BAAB1938F2C837E.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the oldest surviving synagogue building in the United States, representing a historic milestone of religious freedom in America.",fullDescription:"Touro Synagogue National Historic Site, located in Newport, Rhode Island, preserves the oldest standing synagogue building in the United States, dedicated in 1763. Designed by colonial architect Peter Harrison, the synagogue stands as a majestic symbol of religious liberty and constitutional freedom. It was the recipient of President George Washington's famous 1790 letter 'To the Hebrew Congregation in Newport,' which declared that the government of the United States gives 'to bigotry no sanction, to persecution no assistance.' Today, the site features guided tours, exhibits on colonial Jewish history, and a celebration of American civil rights.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Touro Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochure