unior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"We are currently open Tuesday through Saturday. We are closed for Thanksgiving, Christmas, and New Year's Day.",address:"Kansas and South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Brown%20v.%20Board%20of%20Education%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/brvb/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"fort-sumter-and-fort-moultrie-national-historical-park",name:"Fort Sumter and Fort Moultrie National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/E989D519-950E-C3AC-84F007692983681A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/5940E3D9-BA8F-CC70-B7721D361AA47CC3.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic Charleston sea forts where the Civil War began and 171 years of coastal defense.",fullDescription:"Fort Sumter and Fort Moultrie National Historical Park in Charleston Harbor preserves the historic sea forts that defined American coastal defense for nearly two centuries. On April 12, 1861, Confederate batteries opened fire on Union-occupied Fort Sumter, marking the official beginning of the American Civil War. Nearby, Fort Moultrie on Sullivan's Island represents 171 years of military engineering, famously repelling British forces during the Revolutionary War in 1776 and serving actively through World War II.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(843) 883-3123"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Fort Moultrie represents the entire history of static seacoast defenses in the United States, from the American Revolution through World War II. The site includes a visitor center with museum exhibits.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Sumter%20and%20Fort%20Moultrie%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fosu/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"reconstruction-era-national-historical-park",name:"Reconstruction Era National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2A9BB4D0-E4A7-FF36-F56B844AF05762CD.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2AC7081E-EDCC-EEAB-E5A97C90C6032D0E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2AB2A46F-F7E2-CBF4-A9D7876454D9F09C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2A9BB4D0-E4A7-FF36-F56B844AF05762CD.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the monumental history, community sites, and stories of the Reconstruction Era following the American Civil War.",fullDescription:"Reconstruction Era National Historical Park, located in Beaufort County, South Carolina, preserves key sites associated with the transformative post-Civil War Reconstruction Era. Centering on the historic Beaufort region, the park documents the struggles and triumphs of newly freed African Americans during this period of monumental social and political change. Notable sites include the Penn School, established in 1862 as one of the first schools for freed slaves, the historic Brick Baptist Church, and the Emancipation Oak site where the Emancipation Proclamation was read to local citizens.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Capers Creek Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"30-60 Minutes",description:"Walk in the footsteps of Martin Luther King Jr. and the students of the Penn School on the Capers Creek Trail. This 0.25 mile trail through the woods of Saint Helena Island leads to a sweeping view of the marshes."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Reconstruction Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(843) 962-0039"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds are open daily from sunrise to sunset. In addition, the park maintains several visitor centers and contact stations throughout the region.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Reconstruction%20Era%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/reer/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"charles-pinckney-national-historic-site",name:"Charles Pinckney National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C86B25A-1DD8-B71B-0B3451518A1D565B.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C86B25A-1DD8-B71B-0B3451518A1D565B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves Snee Farm, the historic coastal plantation home of Charles Pinckney, a primary framer of the U.S. Constitution.",fullDescription:"Charles Pinckney National Historic Site in Mount Pleasant, South Carolina, protects 28 acres of Snee Farm, a remnant of the coastal plantation owned by Charles Pinckney. Pinckney was a prominent early American statesman, South Carolina governor, and a key contributor to the drafting of the United States Constitution. The site features an 1828 lowcountry cottage housing exhibits on Pinckney's life, the framing of the Constitution, the history of Snee Farm, and the experiences of the enslaved Gullah-Geechee people who lived and worked on the land.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Charles Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(843) 577-0242"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The historic home (Visitor Center) is open seasonally. For the 2026 season, the Visitor Center will be open five days a week Wednesday through Sunday from 9:00 a.m.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Charles%20Pinckney%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/chpi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"ninety-six-national-historic-site",name:"Ninety Six National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79EFE2-1DD8-B71B-0BE1C97AF62D94F2.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79F20F-1DD8-B71B-0BA401E232EAEA6D.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79F515-1DD8-B71B-0BEFDF920A9D5DAA.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves a strategic colonial trading post and the site of two pivotal Revolutionary War battles in western South Carolina.",fullDescription:"Ninety Six National Historic Site protects the historic frontier trading post of Ninety Six, South Carolina, which played a strategic role in early colonial history and the Revolutionary War. The site witnessed the first land battle of the war south of New England in 1775 and the dramatic 1781 siege led by General Nathanael Greene against loyalist defenders. Today, visitors can hike beautiful, peaceful trails to see the extraordinarily well-preserved Star Fort, historic road traces, the colonial town site, and the historic Kosciuszko mine.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ninety Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(864) 543-4068"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds are open from sunrise to sunset. There are eight parking spots located outside of the front gate for early and late visitors.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ninety%20Six%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/nisi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"kings-mountain-national-military-park",name:"Kings Mountain National Military Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C868F03-1DD8-B71B-0BB8654AD4FFDC70.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C868D10-1DD8-B71B-0BE0B8A78B9F1CF6.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C868F03-1DD8-B71B-0BB8654AD4FFDC70.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the pivotal Battle of Kings Mountain, a decisive Patriot victory that turned the tide of the Revolutionary War.",fullDescription:'Kings Mountain National Military Park protects the forested ridge in South Carolina where the Battle of Kings Mountain was fought on October 7, 1780. In this critical Revolutionary War encounter, American Patriots (including the legendary Overmountain Men) decisively defeated Loyalist forces led by British Major Patrick Ferguson. The crushing victory was celebrated as a major turning point in the southern campaign, described by Thomas Jefferson as the "turn of the tide of success" for the American independence movement.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Kings Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kings Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(864) 936-7921"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Park Grounds (Restrooms, Parking Lot and Trails) Open Daily 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kings%20Mountain%20National%20Military%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/kimo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"cowpens-national-battlefield",name:"Cowpens National Battlefield",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7971FE-1DD8-B71B-0B317547012C304A.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C797105-1DD8-B71B-0B8DE06D4BE4FA23.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7971FE-1DD8-B71B-0B317547012C304A.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C79767D-1DD8-B71B-0B0AD92A2C7655DB.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates Brigadier General Daniel Morgan's brilliant 1781 tactical victory over the British army.",fullDescription:"Cowpens National Battlefield preserves the site of the Battle of Cowpens, a masterpiece of military tactics fought on January 17, 1781. Under the masterly leadership of Brigadier General Daniel Morgan, a diverse force of American Continentals and militia achieved a stunning double-envelopment victory over Lieutenant Colonel Banastre Tarleton's elite British forces. This decisive battle crippled British troops in the South, boosting Patriot morale and ultimately paving the way for the British surrender at Yorktown.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cowpens Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(864) 461-2828"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The visitor center parking lot is open 9:00 am - 5:00 pm. The front gate closes at 5:00 pm.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cowpens%20National%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cowp/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"overmountain-victory-national-historic-trail",name:"Overmountain Victory National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FC4E6536-9169-719C-95D5D511D4B94145.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FC814FC2-C8CC-2A03-F3ECFEBA4AF17DC4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FCC70016-F44D-3B7D-C3F901933DDD81B3.jpg"],shortDescription:'Traces the historic route taken by the "Overmountain Men" Patriots in 1780 to victory at the Battle of Kings Mountain.',fullDescription:'The Overmountain Victory National Historic Trail spans 330 miles across Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina, and Tennessee. It traces the historic trail taken by the frontier "Overmountain Men" Patriots who marched over the Blue Ridge Mountains in the autumn of 1780 to confront and defeat Loyalist forces at the Battle of Kings Mountain. Today, the trail connects modern hikers and travelers to the courage and perseverance of early American settlers who helped shape the outcome of the Revolutionary War.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Overmountain Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Overmountain Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(864) 936-7921"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Overmountain Victory National Historic Trail can be visited in any season. It includes sections of walkable pathway, a Commemorative Motor Route, affiliated historic sites and museums, and wayside exhibits.",address:"North carolina and South carolina and Tennessee and Virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Overmountain%20Victory%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/ovvi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"eutaw-springs-battlefield",name:"Eutaw Springs Battlefield",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d8ccd7290469abe4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorates the historic 1781 Battle of Eutaw Springs, the last major engagement of the Revolutionary War in South Carolina.",fullDescription:"Eutaw Springs Battlefield, located near Eutawville, South Carolina, preserves the site of the Battle of Eutaw Springs, fought on September 8, 1781. This brutal encounter was the last major Revolutionary War battle in South Carolina. Although tactically inconclusive, the heavy losses suffered by British forces under Colonel Alexander Stewart forced their retreat to Charleston, effectively breaking British military control over the southern backcountry. The site is managed as an affiliated area of the National Park Service in cooperation with local historical trusts.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Eutaw Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(803) 776-4396"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"South carolina, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Eutaw%20Springs%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/places/eutaw-springs-battlefield.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"historic-camden-revolutionary-war-site",name:"Historic Camden Revolutionary War Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d7081f7106c93bca.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic site of British-occupied Camden during the Revolutionary War and its major battlefields.",fullDescription:"Historic Camden Revolutionary War Site in South Carolina is an affiliated unit of the National Park Service that preserves the historic garrison and revolutionary-era town of Camden. Occupied by British forces from 1780 to 1781 under Lord Cornwallis, Camden was a critical military hub in the South. The site features reconstructed historic houses, colonial farm structures, archaeological remains, and the nearby battlefields of Camden and Hobkirk's Hill, telling the stories of early southern settlement and the struggles of the Revolutionary War.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Historic Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(803) 432-0215"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],ent