up the complex legacy of the expedition.",fullDescription:"Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail is an official National Park Service site. The Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail winds nearly 4,900 miles through the homelands of more than 60 Tribal nations. It follows the historic outbound and inbound routes of the Lewis and Clark Expedition of 1803-1806 from Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania to the Pacific Ocean. Follow the trail to find the people, places, and stories that make up the complex legacy of the expedition. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Slough Trail",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"1-3 Hours",description:"The South Slough Trail is a three mile loop that can be accessed from the Fort to Sea Trailhead or from near the North Netul Landing that features twist and turns and steep changes in elevation among large trees and a bo"},{name:"Clay Pit Pond Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"15-60 Minutes",description:"The Clay Pit Pond is a half mile loop primitive trail with access at the Fort Clatsop Visitor Center picnic area. It is a great trail for those looking for a short but dynamic hike. The trail features a vernal pond surro"},{name:"Netul River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"20-60 Minutes",description:"Take an easy approximately 1 mile stroll along the beautiful Netul River Trail. Watch for water birds, eagles and sometimes otters! Along the way you can learn about the history of logging and commerce along the Lewis an"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis & Clark Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Lewis & Clark National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(402) 661-1804"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Rang