fortifications, and scenic hiking trails overlooking the spectacular tri-state region.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cumberland Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(606) 248-2817"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"The park is open 24 hours a day to pedestrian and cyclist, weather permitting. Some services and facilities have separate hours, however; please see descriptions of those facilities elsewhere.",address:"Kentucky and Tennessee and Virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cumberland%20Gap%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"manhattan-project-national-historical-park",name:"Manhattan Project National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/87DC8664-0A66-3B76-6B9BF543D9581735.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/884A2FA9-FC95-36D1-FB9C52521E3FED66.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the top-secret scientific effort during World War II that developed the world's first atomic weapons.",fullDescription:'Manhattan Project National Historical Park is a multi-site park co-managed by the NPS and the Department of Energy, documenting the top-secret World War II scientific breakthrough that produced the atomic bomb. In Oak Ridge, Tennessee—known as the "Secret City"—thousands of scientists, engineers, and workers labored in massive, hidden facilities to enrich uranium. The park preserves historic facilities and tells the complex stories of scientific innovation, wartime sacrifice, and the geopolitical and ethical legacies of the atomic age.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hanford: Hike the White Bluffs North Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"On this trail you may explore rolling sand dunes, take in vast vistas dotted with relics of plutonium production during the Manhattan Project and Cold War, and observe a variety of wildlife."},{name:"Hanford: Hike Candy Mountain",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"The trail up Candy Mountain is a moderate 3.6 mile (5.8 km) roundtrip hike that offers sweeping views of the Tri-Cities region and a walk through time. Signs along the trail explain the impact of the ice age floods on ar"},{name:"Hanford: Hike the White Bluffs South Slope Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"6-8 Hours",description:"This trail provides one of the best views of the old Hanford town site and location of the Hanford construction camp for the Manhattan Project. This trail will reward you with expansive views of the Hanford site and oppo"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Manhattan Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(865) 482-1949"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"]