 the site of the pivotal, bloody 1862 Civil War Battle of Shiloh and the Shiloh National Cemetery.",fullDescription:"Shiloh National Military Park protects the sacred ground where the Battle of Shiloh was fought on April 6-7, 1862. It was one of the earliest and most devastating major conflicts of the American Civil War, resulting in nearly 24,000 casualties and opening the way for Union forces to gain control of Western Tennessee. The park preserves the beautifully forested battlefield, the Shiloh Indian Mounds National Historic Landmark, and the Shiloh National Cemetery, offering visitors a deep, solemn look at the war's immense human cost.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Shiloh Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(731) 689-5696"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park grounds remain open daily from sunrise to sunset. The Shiloh Battlefield visitor center is open from 9:00 am - 5:00 pm daily.",address:"Mississippi and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Shiloh%20National%20Military%20ParkShiloh%20National%20Cemetery",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/shil/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"fort-donelson-national-battlefieldfort-donelson-national-cemetery",name:"Fort Donelson National BattlefieldFort Donelson National Cemetery",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/6903ED5B-1DD8-B71B-0B29CCAFAED7A0BF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F26FAA11-B46E-B499-F5F9ECA3A44EA1AC.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the 1862 Battle of Fort Donelson, where Union General Ulysses S. Grant secured a historic victory.",fullDescription:"Fort Donelson National Battlefield preserves the site of the historic Battle of Fort Donelson, fought in February 1862 along the Cumberland River in Tennessee. The Union victory here, commanded by General Ulysses S. Grant, was a major strategic breakthrough, capturing a key Confederate fort, forcing the surrender of over 12,000 Confederate soldiers, and opening the Cumberland River for Union advances. The park preserves the earthen fort, river batteries, the historic Dover Hotel, and the Fort Donelson National Cemetery.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Trails at Fort Donelson NB",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"45-90 Minutes",description:"Immerse yourself in the beauty of the historic site by hiking the park trails. You may see migratory birds, bald eagles, and lovely native wildflowers. Lovely views of the Cumberland River/Barkley Lake may be seen as wel"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(931) 232-5706"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Fort Donelson NB Vis