1DD8-B71B-0B9E86CB3BBD9041.jpg"],shortDescription:"A 444-mile scenic national parkway tracing the historic Natchez Trace forest path from Mississippi to Tennessee.",fullDescription:"The Natchez Trace Parkway is a 444-mile scenic national drive that traces the historic Natchez Trace, an ancient forest path used by Native Americans, early European settlers, and soldiers. The parkway runs from Natchez, Mississippi, through Alabama, to Nashville, Tennessee. Along the route, visitors can explore ancient Indian mounds, pioneer gravesites, civil war battlefields, and enjoy tranquil, commercial-free natural scenery that changes beautifully with each state and season.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking the Blackland Prairie Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-6 Hours",description:"The Blackland Prairie section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is a popular 6 mile hiking trail in and around Tupelo, Mississippi."},{name:"Hiking the Yockanookany Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-15 Hours",description:"North of Jackson, Mississippi the Yockanookany section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is the longest of the five developed trail sections at 26 miles. Eight miles run alongside the Ross R. Barnett Reservoir, "},{name:"Hiking the Highland Rim Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-10 Hours",description:"The Highland Rim section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is a popular twenty mile hiking and equestrian corridor south of Nashville Tennessee along the Natchez Trace Parkway from Milepost 427.4 to 407.9."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Natchez Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(662) 680-4027"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Alabama and Mississippi and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Natchez%20Trace%20Parkway",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/natr/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"butterfield-overland-national-historic-trail",name:"Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A0402204-FBD7-DC23-F626D64AEE7FE482.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A0402204-FBD7-DC23-F626D64AEE7FE482.jpg"],shortDescription:"Traces the historic stagecoach mail route of the mid-19th century connecting St. Louis and Memphis to San Francisco.",fullDescription:"The Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail commemorates the historic stagecoach and mail route operated by the Butterfield Overland Mail Company from 1858 to 1861. Spanning over 3,200 miles across eight states, including Tennessee, this trail was the nation's first transcontinental postal route, connecting St. Louis and Memphis to San Francisco. Today, the trail preserves historic landmarks and tells the story of the westward expansion, communication, and transportation in pre-Civil War America.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Butterfield Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"California and Missouri and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Butterfield%20Overland%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/buov/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"natchez-trace-trail-official-unit",name:"Natchez Trace Trail (official unit)",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C843CE5-1DD8-B71B-0BAFBFE2E37ADA31.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C843EB9-1DD8-B71B-0B958F0F60585D20.jpg"],shortDescription:"A scenic trail network with five distinct hiking segments running parallel to the historic Natchez Trace Parkway.",fullDescription:"The Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail consists of five distinct, developed hiking segments running parallel to the scenic Natchez Trace Parkway. Totaling over 60 miles of footpaths, the trail allows hikers to step off the highway and walk along the actual historic sunken traces of the old path. Visitors can walk through diverse ecosystems, including old-growth hardwood forests, swamps, and wetlands, experiencing the same quiet woodlands that early travelers traversed centuries ago.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Scenic Drives","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking the Blackland Prairie Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-6 Hours",description:"The Blackland Prairie section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is a popular 6 mile hiking trail in and around Tupelo, Mississippi."},{name:"Hiking the Yockanookany Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-15 Hours",description:"North of Jackson, Mississippi the Yockanookany section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is the longest of the five developed trail sections at 26 miles. Eight miles run alongside the Ross R. Barnett Reservoir, "},{name:"Hiking the Highland Rim Section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-10 Hours",description:"The Highland Rim section of the Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is a popular twenty mile hiking and equestrian corridor south of Nashville Tennessee along the Natchez Trace Parkway from Milepost 427.4 to 407.9."}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Natchez Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(662) 680-4027"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes","Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck","Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Natchez Trace National Scenic Trail is open every day during daylight hours. Severe weather resulting in washouts and downed trees can cause temporary trail closures.",address:"Mississippi and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Natchez%20Trace%20Trail%20(official%20unit)",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/natt/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash.","Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"overmountain-victory-national-historic-trail",name:"Overmountain Victory National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FC4E6536-9169-719C-95D5D511D4B94145.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FC814FC2-C8CC-2A03-F3ECFEBA4AF17DC4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FCC70016-F44D-3B7D-C3F901933DDD81B3.jpg"],shortDescription:'Traces the historic route taken by the "Overmountain Men" Patriots in 1780 to victory at the Battle of Kings Mountain.',fullDescription:'The Overmountain Victory National Historic Trail spans 330 miles across Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina, and Tennessee. It traces the historic trail taken by the frontier "Overmountain Men" Patriots who marched over the Blue Ridge Mountains in the autumn of 1780 to confront and defeat Loyalist forces at the Battle of Kings Mountain. Today, the trail connects modern hikers and travelers to the courage and perseverance of early American settlers who helped shape the outcome of the Revolutionary War.',thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:["Overmountain Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Overmountain Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(864) 936-7921"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Overmountain Victory National Historic Trail can be visited in any season. It includes sections of walkable pathway, a Commemorative Motor Route, affiliated historic sites and museums, and wayside exhibits.",address:"North carolina and South carolina and Tennessee and Virginia, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Overmountain%20Victory%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/ovvi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"trail-of-tears-national-historic-trail",name:"Trail of Tears National Historic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E670B-1DD8-B71B-0B25C5CD76BF4A04.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E6D6E-1DD8-B71B-0BF2FB679C481F1A.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorates the tragic forced removal of Cherokee and other Indigenous nations from their ancestral homelands to Oklahoma.",fullDescription:"The Trail of Tears National Historic Trail commemorates the tragic, forced removal of the Cherokee and other Native American nations from their southeastern ancestral homelands to Indian Territory (present-day Oklahoma) between 1838 and 1839. In Tennessee, the trail traces crucial water and land routes taken by thousands of Cherokee under military escort. Today, the trail preserves historic sites, road traces, and interpretive facilities, honoring the resilience and remembering the thousands who perished during this forced march.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on the Trail of Tears",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The trail commemorates the forced removal of Cherokee from their homelands; the paths that 17 Cherokee detachments followed westward; and the revival of the Cherokee Nation. We encourage you to retrace the trail on foot—"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Trail Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Oklahoma and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Trail%20of%20Tears%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/trte/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"parker-s-crossroads-battlefield",name:"Parker's Crossroads Battlefield",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-parker-s-crossroads-battlefield-tennessee.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the site of the 1862 Civil War Battle of Parker's Crossroads in West Tennessee.",fullDescription:"Parker's Crossroads Battlefield in West Tennessee protects the site of the Battle of Parker's Crossroads, fought on December 31, 1862. In this fierce Civil War encounter, Confederate forces under General Nathan Bedford Forrest successfully fought their way out of a Union pincer movement, escaping back across the Tennessee River. Today, the battlefield features beautifully preserved walking trails with interpretive panels, monuments, a visitor center, and a scenic driving tour highlighting the movements and strategies of both armies.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Parker's Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(731) 968-1191"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"Check the official website for camping options.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Check the official website for current fees.",hours:"Check the official website for current hours.",address:"Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Parker's%20Crossroads%20Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/places/parkers-crossroads-battlefield.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"appalachian-national-scenic-trail",name:"Appalachian National Scenic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C83A128-1DD8-B71B-0B02DED286AFD8C6.jpg"],shortDescription:"The Appalachian Trail is a 2,190+ mile long public footpath that traverses the scenic, wooded, pastoral, wild, and culturally resonant lands of the Appalachian Mountains. Conceived in 1921, built by private citizens, and completed in 1937, today the trail is managed by the National Park Service, US Forest Service, Appalachian Trail Conservancy, numerous state agencies and thousands of volunteers.",fullDescription:"Appalachian National Scenic Trail is an official National Park Service site. The Appalachian Trail is a 2,190+ mile long public footpath that traverses the scenic, wooded, pastoral, wild, and culturally resonant lands of the Appalachian Mountains. Conceived in 1921, built by private citizens, and completed in 1937, today the trail is managed by the National Park Service, US Forest Service, Appalachian Trail Conservancy, numerous state agencies and thousands of volunteers. Visitors from all over come to experience and learn about its distinct natural formations and historic value.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Scenic Drives","Wildlife Viewing","Photography","Nature Trails","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Multi-Park Loop",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Park your car and stretch your legs! Visit four different National Parks in Washington, DC and begin logging miles on seven different National Historic or National Scenic trails!"}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Appalachian Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Appalachian National Scenic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(304) 535-6278"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"Camping is permitted in designated campgrounds inside the area. Permits and advanced reservations are highly recommended.",wildlife:["Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestViewpoints:["Summit Viewpoint","Valley Vista Point","River Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Appalachian%20National%20Scenic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/appa/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"great-smoky-mountains-national-park",name:"Great Smoky Mountains National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80EC37-1DD8-B71B-0B87F63E8B030D15.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80E3F4-1DD8-B71B-0BFF4F2280EF1B52.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C80F1CC-1DD8-B71B-0B784AC1BCA74166.jpg"],shortDescription:"America's most visited national park, famous for its mist-covered peaks, rich biodiversity, and historic Appalachian log cabins.",fullDescription:"Great Smoky Mountains National Park, spanning the border of Tennessee and North Carolina, is renowned for its sweeping mountain vistas, mist-covered ridges, rich biodiversity, and well-preserved Southern Appalachian historic structures. As a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the park protects a vast, pristine forest ecosystem containing over 19,000 documented species of plants and animals. Visitors can hike over 800 miles of trails—including the Appalachian Trail—climb to the high vista of Clingmans Dome, explore historic Cades Cove, and enjoy spectacular year-round scenery.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike to Abrams Falls",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Walk 5 miles (8 km) roundtrip through rhododendron, hemlock, and pine-oak forest to a 25-foot (7.6-m) waterfall and scenic pool. Swimming in the pool is not recommended due to slippery rocks and a dangerous undertow."},{name:"Hike the Gatlinburg Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Enjoy a riverside stroll approximately 4 miles (6.4 km) roundtrip through a cove hardwood forest along one of the only dog- and bike-friendly trails in the park. This trail includes sections of sidewalks and roadsides as"},{name:"Hike to Chimney Tops Overlook",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Hike 3.5 miles (5.6 km) roundtrip on this popular but challenging trail to enjoy views of surrounding mountains and the Chimney Tops pinnacles. This trail is steep and rocky at times, and includes bridges, stairs, and so"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Great Smoky Mountains National Park Visitor Information",info:"Please select Tennessee for visitor center information and contact details.",hours:"Varies",phone:"(865) 436-1200"},rangerPrograms:[],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"North carolina and Tennessee, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Great%20Smoky%20Mountains%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/grsm/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Select Tennessee to view comprehensive itineraries and details."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"}],texas:[{id:"big-bend-national-park",name:"Big Bend National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8BF8356B-BB63-76A4-19F5296EF94C96B4.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8BCD073D-D2AF-C63C-F75A21DF9E355C61.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8BDD8415-B290-DEF8-105D75C840EBFCD2.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8B115C1E-B4B2-AF30-1C562C5D39E4B637.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/8AEFBCCC-0C9E-92DD-5E2B311EC6DA0339.jpg"],shortDescription:"Protects a vast expanse of the Chihuahuan Desert, offering dramatic river canyons, rugged mountains, and abundant wildlife.",fullDescription:"Big Bend National Park in southwestern Texas preserves over 800,000 acres of Chihuahuan Desert landscape, spectacular limestone canyons, and the rugged Chisos Mountains. Named after the prominent bend in the Rio Grande, the park showcases a dramatic contrast of desert expanses, mountain forests, and riparian environments. Visitors can explore popular trails like the Lost Mine Trail, hike the breathtaking Santa Elena Canyon, and relax in natural hot springs, all under some of the darkest night skies in North America.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"List of Hiking Trails in Big Bend",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Hiking trails"},{name:"See the Rio Grande",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Rio Grande is accessible from a number of places in Big Bend National Park, and a variety of hiking trails provide even further opportunities to get up close and personal with our watery border with Mexico."},{name:"Hike the Lost Mine Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Lost Mine Trail climbs to the side of Lost Mine Peak, offering spectacular views of Juniper Canyon and the Northeast Rim of the Chisos."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Big Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(432) 477-2251"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Big%20Bend%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/bibe/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"guadalupe-mountains-national-park",name:"Guadalupe Mountains National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C825533-1DD8-B71B-0B6FDF436F604A3C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8256DA-1DD8-B71B-0B7E4BE83A8B56F9.jpg"],shortDescription:"Features the highest peak in Texas, pristine desert ecosystems, and the world's premier fossilized Permian reef.",fullDescription:"Guadalupe Mountains National Park in West Texas protects the extraordinary, fossilized Permian Capitan Reef rising high above the Chihuahuan Desert. The park is home to Guadalupe Peak, the highest point in Texas at 8,751 feet, as well as the stunning fall colors of McKittrick Canyon and the pristine white sands of the Salt Basin Dunes. Hiking and backpacking are premier activities, offering access to beautiful pine-oak forests, dramatic canyons, and rich wildlife habitats.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"The Notch",difficulty:"Challenging",length:"5-7 Hours",description:'The hike to "the Notch" is a challenging day hike that proceeds through McKittrick Canyon to a dramatic viewpoint about half way up toward McKittrick Ridge.'},{name:"McKittrick Canyon to Dog Canyon",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"8-12 Hours",description:"The McKittrick Canyon Trail follows the floor of South McKittrick Canyon for four miles before climbing steeply to gain the ridge on the north side of the canyon. This is an arduous climb provides spectacular views into "},{name:"McKittrick Ridge",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"8-12 Hours",description:'The journey to the top of McKittrick Ridge is "the toughest hike in Texas." This arduous hike is best enjoyed as an overnight hike allowing for rest, recovery, and fabulous night skies at the McKittrick Ridge Wilderness '}],scenicDrives:["Guadalupe Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Guadalupe Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(915) 828-3251"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Guadalupe%20Mountains%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gumo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"alibates-flint-quarries-national-monument",name:"Alibates Flint Quarries National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7EFA64-1DD8-B71B-0BD2E6EF7D52B0BF.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/EEB4ED5A-C735-67E9-6E61B710C6E01D72.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C845BA9-1DD8-B71B-0B63C45334DC3128.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves ancient quarries where Native Americans harvested colorful Alibates flint for thousands of years.",fullDescription:"Alibates Flint Quarries National Monument, located in the Texas Panhandle, preserves historic quarries that were vital to prehistoric human survival for over 13,000 years. Native Americans and early cultures excavated the beautiful, highly durable, and colorful Alibates flint to fabricate essential tools and weapons. Ranger-guided tours take visitors up to the quarry hills to view ancient excavation pits and learn about the geologic processes and rich cultural history of the Southern Plains.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Alibates Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(806) 886-3826"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Alibates Visitor Center has museum exhibits, theater, bookstore, and a 7 themed garden with a Monarch Waystation and other native plants. The Alibates Flint Quarries are accessible by guided tour only.",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Alibates%20Flint%20Quarries%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/alfl/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"waco-mammoth-national-monument",name:"Waco Mammoth National Monument",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/CD694A69-06E4-E967-C0DD0582F2455F95.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7F4897-1DD8-B71B-0B2380CB31380537.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the nation's only recorded discovery of a nursery herd of Pleistocene Columbian mammoths.",fullDescription:"Waco Mammoth National Monument is a paleontological site in Waco, Texas, protecting the fossil remains of Columbian mammoths and other ice age animals. Discovered in 1978, the site represents the only known nursery herd of Columbian mammoths in the world, who perished in a sudden mudslide or flood approximately 65,000 years ago. A climate-controlled dig shelter features a suspended walkway, allowing visitors to look down at the beautifully preserved, in-situ fossils of these giant prehistoric creatures.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"All About Hiking at Waco Mammoth National Monument",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Enjoy 21st century plants and animals in addition to viewing ice age fossils."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Waco Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(254) 750-7946"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Operating hours are 9:00 am to 5:00 pm daily. The park is open every day except Thanksgiving, Christmas, and New Year's Day.",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Waco%20Mammoth%20National%20Monument",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/waco/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Monument"},{id:"big-thicket-national-preserve",name:"Big Thicket National Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/E5D14618-955C-0D8C-2CB15337A624C7BE.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/60F6F249-D0F2-9EAA-3B863696CC12C4B0.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A1C1AD18-FFEA-54AC-1AE0A1F15E1E20AF.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A095BD13-F0BB-FC3B-538C6E3F6A94D08F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FA692D17-BDCC-E98F-C537FAF2CE0D7796.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/39EC3CA1-9934-FE72-CF899277FA59400B.jpg"],shortDescription:"A highly diverse biological crossroads, protecting dense forests, cypress swamps, and winding bayous.",fullDescription:"Big Thicket National Preserve in southeastern Texas is recognized as a biological crossroads where multiple ecosystems converge, creating an incredibly biodiverse sanctuary of piney woods, hardwood forests, cypress-tupelo swamps, and coastal prairies. The preserve supports a wide range of flora and fauna, including rare orchids, carnivorous pitcher plants, and numerous migratory bird species. Visitors can explore this unique wilderness through paddling trails, hiking paths, and ranger-led tours.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike the Sundew Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"0.3 to 1 mile roundtrip | Search for tiny red carnivorous plants known as sundews on an easy loop hike through a lush wetland pine savannah."},{name:"Hike to the Sandhill Loop Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"2-8 Hours",description:"4½ to 9.4 miles roundtrip | Look for longleaf pines, cacti, and solitude on this out-and-back and loop hike that leads to an arid sandyland ecosystem."},{name:"Hike the Kirby Nature Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"1½ to 2½ miles roundtrip | Experience the Big Thicket on a gentle loop hike through different kinds of forest. See majestic bald cypress trees and knees and Village Creek."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Big Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(409) 951-6700"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Ac