enter",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(830) 868-7128"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park is open every day except Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year's Day.",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lyndon%20B.%20Johnson%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lyjo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"palo-alto-battlefield-national-historical-park",name:"Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C791D5F-1DD8-B71B-0B9B92E13DDE689F.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Commemorates the site of the first major battle of the U.S.-Mexican War in 1846.",fullDescription:"Palo Alto Battlefield National Historical Park in Brownsville, Texas, preserves the site of the Battle of Palo Alto, fought on May 8, 1846. This clash was the first major battle of the U.S.-Mexican War, a conflict that dramatically reshaped the map of North America. The park is the only NPS unit dedicated primarily to documenting the war, offering peaceful walking trails across the historic coastal prairie, interpretive exhibits, and a film detailing the perspectives and legacies of both nations.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hiking the Battlefield Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"45-90 Minutes",description:"Walk in the footsteps of history! Along your half-mile walk you will see a landscape very much like the one experienced by soldiers in 1846."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Palo Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(956) 541-2785"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Park grounds are open daily from 8 am to 5 pm. Visitor center hours of operation differ from park hours of operation and are dependent upon staffing.",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Palo%20Alto%20Battlefield%20National%20Historical%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/paal/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"blackwell-school-national-historic-site",name:"Blackwell School National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/41BC060B-9362-F9FE-AD6294CE7DF1B02A.jpg",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Preserves the historic schoolhouse commemorating the era of de facto segregation of Mexican American students in Texas.",fullDescription:"The Blackwell School National Historic Site in Marfa, Texas, preserves the 1909 schoolhouse that served as the primary educational facility for the town's Mexican and Mexican American students during the era of de facto segregation. Operating until 1965, the school is a powerful national symbol of cultural identity, resilience, and the struggles for equality in the American public education system. The site features photographs, memories, and personal objects donated by former students.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Blackwell Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(432) 426-3224"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The unit is designated but not yet established and has very limited services. Open every Saturday and Sunday 12:00 to 4:00 p.m.",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Blackwell%20School%20National%20Historic%20Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/blsc/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"fort-davis-national-historic-site",name:"Fort Davis National Historic Site",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7DCD39-1DD8-B71B-0BEE09E6D3EF298C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7DD030-1DD8-B71B-0BCFFA4A826F038F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7DD1B0-1DD8-B71B-0B75AFDB9100B067.jpg"],shortDescription:"One of the best-preserved western frontier military posts, active during the Indian Wars from 1854 to 1891.",fullDescription:"Fort Davis National Historic Site, nestled in a scenic canyon of the Davis Mountains in West Texas, is one of the nation's most outstanding examples of a southwestern frontier military post. Serving from 1854 to 1891, the fort protected emigrants, mail coaches, and freight wagons on the Trans-Pecos trail, and was home to the legendary Buffalo Soldiers. Today, visitors can tour meticulously restored historic buildings, hike scenic mountain trails, and experience vivid history demonstrations.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Historical Exhibits","Ranger Talks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(432) 426-3224"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists no camping at this site.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:`Daily Hours are 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM
