opular local destination for motorized boating, jet skiing, fishing, swimming, and camping, offering an unexpected aquatic escape in a semi-arid landscape rich in geological and pioneer history.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(806) 857-3151"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake%20Meredith%20National%20Recreation%20Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lamr/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"padre-island-national-seashore",name:"Padre Island National Seashore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F771AB23-E1E9-0B08-C7765B725C3EB41F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F7900E38-FCAE-4EEA-D5B1AAA5DBC5E9FD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/F7C767DB-BA6D-C805-366B872B0E9445E3.jpg"],shortDescription:"The longest stretch of undeveloped barrier island in the world, protecting pristine beaches and nesting sea turtles.",fullDescription:"Padre Island National Seashore protects 70 miles of pristine coastline, dunes, and salt flats along the Gulf of Mexico, representing the longest undeveloped barrier island in the world. The park separating the Gulf from the Laguna Madre is a critical sanctuary for over 380 bird species and serves as a vital nesting ground for the endangered Kemp's ridley sea turtle. Visitors can enjoy primitive beach camping, beachcombing, outstanding windsurfing, and public turtle hatchling releases.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Kayaking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Padre Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(361) 949-8068"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $25; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Padre%20Island%20National%20Seashore",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/pais/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Seashore/Lakeshore"},{id:"rio-grande-wild-and-scenic-river",name:"Rio Grande Wild and Scenic River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7F000DA5-F0C4-D048-1EF137BD2F80CB3C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7F000DA5-F0C4-D048-1EF137BD2F80CB3C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C842FEA-1DD8-B71B-0B9996D58060FC66.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C842B92-1DD8-B71B-0BCA4078E79F2F65.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C842887-1DD8-B71B-0BB8F65C9EBB928E.jpg"],shortDescription:"Protects a free-flowing, remote segment of the Rio Grande winding through spectacular desert canyons.",fullDescription:"The Rio Grande Wild and Scenic River protects a remote 196-mile stretch of the legendary border river, winding through towering limestone canyons, rugged desert rapids, and lush riparian corridors in West Texas. Accessible primarily by multi-day raft or canoe trips, this pristine river wilderness offers deep isolation, spectacular geological formations, and exceptional birdwatching, showcasing the true wild character of the American Southwest.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails","Kayaking","Swimming","Scenic Drives","Outdoor Exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"See the Rio Grande",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"See nps.gov",description:"The Rio Grande is accessible from a number of places in Big Bend National Park, and a variety of hiking trails provide even further opportunities to get up close and personal with our watery border with Mexico."}],scenicDrives:["Park Scenic Loop (12 miles)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Rio Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(432) 477-2251"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs","Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Herons","Bald Eagles","Osprey","River Otters","Mule Deer","Foxes","Coyotes","Diverse Bird Species","Small Mammals"],bestView