ail commemorates the historic stagecoach and mail route operated by the Butterfield Overland Mail Company from 1858 to 1861. Spanning over 3,200 miles across eight states, including Texas, this trail was the nation's first transcontinental postal route, connecting St. Louis and Memphis to San Francisco. Today, the trail preserves historic landmarks and tells the story of the westward expansion, communication, and transportation in pre-Civil War America.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Butterfield Overland Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for Butterfield Overland National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Texas, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Butterfield%20Overland%20National%20Historic%20Trail",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/buov/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild and comfortable weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor contact center, primary educational exhibits, and several viewpoints are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Review the official NPS website prior to travel for weather warnings and temporary trail closures.","Pack plenty of water, use sun protection, and ensure you pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Trails"},{id:"el-camino-real-de-tierra-adentro-national-historic-trail",name:"El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3e554224e7862be4.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A6A93-1DD8-B71B-0B25C2DE5079660E.jpg"],shortDescription:"Traces the historic 1,600-mile trade route connecting Mexico City to Santa Fe, New Mexico.",fullDescription:"El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail, or the Royal Road of the Interior Lands, traces the historic 1,600-mile trade route that connected Mexico City with Santa Fe, New Mexico, from 1598 to 1881. It was the oldest and longest Spanish colonial trail in North America, facilitating trade, cultural exchange, and migration across the American Southwest. Today, the trail protects historic river crossings, old plazas, and natural landmarks along the Rio Grande corridor.",thingsToDo:["Historical Exhibits","Guided Ranger Tours","Photography","Visitor Center Film","Educational Walks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hike on El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Travel along El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail to experience and learn from a complicated legacy of 300 years of conflict, cooperation, and cultural exchange between a variety of empires—European a"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro Visitor Information",info:"The contact point for El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail, offering exhibits, maps, pamphlets, and helpful ranger guidance for planning your visit.",phone:"(505) 988-6098"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Interpretive Walks","Junior Ranger Badge Activities"],campingInfo:"No campgrounds are available within the historic site grounds. Campgrounds and hotel lodging are located in nearby towns.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Main Commemorative Plaza","Visitor Center Overlook"],entrance