 limestone carved by frost-wedging and rainwater. The park is a series of massive amphitheaters carved into the eastern edge of the Paunsaugunt Plateau, offering breathtaking, high-contrast vistas of orange, pink, and white rock. Popular trails like the Queens Garden and Navajo Loop descend into the canyon, offering an immersive hike among towering spires.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Go For a Winter Hike",difficulty:"Challenging",length:"See nps.gov",description:"Many hikes are available to visitors during the winter season, in both the front and backcountry, but might require some additional equipment. For much of the winter the most popular trails are so icy that steep sections"},{name:"Walk the Rim Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1-6 Hours",description:"The Rim Trail stretches from its beginning in the south at Bryce Point 5.5 miles (8.9 km) north to Fairyland Point. As it follows the edge of the Bryce Amphitheater area of the park, it connects to Inspiration Point, Sun"},{name:"Day Hikes",difficulty:"Easy",length:"See nps.gov",description:"From easy walks along the edge of the famous Bryce Amphitheater using the Rim Trail to more strenuous adventures surrounded by the hoodoos below on the Peekaboo Loopthe park provides a variety of options for a variety of"}],scenicDrives:["Bryce Scenic Drive"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bryce Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(435) 834-5322"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor 