xplore historic petroglyphs carved by early indigenous people, and harvest fresh fruit from heritage orchards.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Capitol Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(435) 425-3791"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Utah, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Capitol%20Reef%20National%20Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/care/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn (mild weather)",accessibilityInfo:"The visitor center and several designated viewing platforms/trails are fully wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check the official NPS website before visiting for current weather and conditions.","Carry plenty of drinking water and pack out all trash."],nearbyAttractions:[],designation:"National Park"},{id:"zion-national-park",name:"Zion National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/68BFC1AC-BF96-629F-89D261D78F181C64.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/68ED8C2C-A9D3-90B2-BBC96E7A21BC3744.jpg"],shortDescription:"A spectacular canyon of towering cream, pink, and red sandstone cliffs, famous for world-class slot canyon hikes.",fullDescription:"Zion National Park in southwestern Utah is defined by the massive, steep-walled Zion Canyon, carved by the Virgin River. The canyon features soaring sandstone cliffs of Navajo sandstone, hanging gardens, and world-renowned trails. Hikers from around the globe come to challenge the dizzying heights of Angels Landing or wade through the cold waters of the Virgin River in the towering narrows of The Narrows, experiencing some of the most dramatic canyon scenery in the world.",thingsToDo:["Sightseeing","Photography","Guided Walks","Wildlife Viewing","Hiking","Nature Trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Upper Emerald Pools Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Upper Emerald Pools Trail is a moderate, 1 mile (1.6 km) trail that can be accessed via the Kayenta Trail, Lower Emerald Pools Trail, or Middle Emerald Pools Trail. Climb up stone steps to a sandy path that leads through"},{name:"Sand Bench",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-4 Hours",description:"The Sand Bench Trail travels atop the remains of an ancient rockfall. This trail leads hikers along the Virgin River and then ascends a landslide that dammed the Virgin River 8,000 years ago and created a lake that exten"},{name:"Lower Emerald Pool Trail",difficulty:"See nps.gov",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"Lower Emerald Pool trail is a rough, paved trail that leads through pinyon-juniper woodland to the Lower Emerald Pool and ephemeral waterfalls.The pools are fragile and an essential water source for wildlife. Do not go i"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Zion Visitor Center",info:"The main visitor contact point, providing maps, informational brochures, and educational displays.",phone:"(435) 772-3256"},rangerPrograms:["Daily Ranger-led Educational Talks","Junior Ranger Activity Programs"],campingInfo:"NPS lists camping here; see nps.gov for campgrounds and reservations.",wildlife:["Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","Songbirds","Coyotes"],bestViewpoints:["Main Overlook","Visitor Center Observation Deck"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"Utah, USA",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Zion%20N