oker T. Washington was born into slavery in 1856. The landscape serves as a living classroom, providing insight into the harsh realities of enslaved life and the journey of Washington from bondage to international renown as the founder of Tuskegee Institute.",thingsToDo:["Tour the farmyard","Interpretive farm demonstrations","Junior Ranger program","Guided landscape tours"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Plantation Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A loop through the fields and woodlands of the historic farmstead."},{name:"Jack-O-Lantern Branch Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A shorter, wooded path exploring the park's riparian areas."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Visitor Center",info:"Main building housing exhibits and bookstore.",phone:"(540) 682-0173",hours:"10:00 AM - 4:00 PM daily"},rangerPrograms:["Enslaved Life presentations","Plantation agriculture demonstrations"],campingInfo:"No camping permitted within the monument.",wildlife:["Eastern bluebird","White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red fox"],bestViewpoints:["Plantation Trail Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:`The park grounds and restrooms are open daily from 9:00 am-5:00 pm. Visitor Center Hours:
