Yorktown Battlefield tour","Archaeological site visits"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Yorktown Tour Road Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"7.0 miles",description:"A path tracing the redoubts and siege lines of the battle."},{name:"Jamestown Island Drive Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"5.0 miles",description:"Loop trail through forest and tidal marsh habitats."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Colonial Parkway",length:"23 miles",description:"A beautiful, park-like road connecting Yorktown and Jamestown."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Yorktown Visitor Center",info:"Comprehensive hub for both sides of the park.",phone:"757-898-2410",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Revolutionary War talks","Jamestown history walks"],campingInfo:"No camping available within the park.",wildlife:["Bald eagle","Wild turkey","White-tailed deer","Osprey"],bestViewpoints:["York River overlook"],entranceFees:"Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"The Historic Jamestowne Visitor Center is open. The Historic Jamestowne ENTRANCE GATE opens 8:45 a.m.",address:"1000 Colonial Pkwy, Yorktown, VA 23690",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Colonial+National+Historical+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/colo/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring or early fall",accessibilityInfo:"Many paths are paved and flat; some terrain is challenging.",travelTips:["Start your trip early","Allow two days to see everything","Check the ferry schedule if crossing the river"],nearbyAttractions:["Williamsburg historic area","Busch Gardens"],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"maggie-l-walker-national-historic-site",name:"Maggie L. Walker National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6550f894a987d762.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/7798EDED-C0A7-D333-9FDBE01E4C7AF4B1.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/77B88318-C9A9-A069-00B4BCEA3EC6BE63.jpg"],shortDescription:"The home of Maggie L. Walker, the first woman in the United States to charter and serve as president of a bank.",fullDescription:"Located in Richmond's historic Jackson Ward neighborhood, this site preserves the home of a pioneering African American businesswoman and community leader. Walker overcame racial and gender barriers to empower her community through economic and social activism in the early 20th century.",thingsToDo:["Guided house tours","Museum exhibits on Walker's life","Walking tours of Jackson Ward","Educational workshops"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Visitor Center",info:"Located within the museum building.",phone:"(804) 226-5040",hours:"9:00 AM - 4:30 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Life of Maggie Walker talk","Women's history discussions"],campingInfo:"No camping available.",wildlife:["Urban birds","Squirrels"],bestViewpoints:["Historic street architecture of Jackson Ward"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Group tours by appointment only Tuesday-Thursday.",address:"600 N 2nd St, Richmond, VA 23219",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Maggie+L+Walker+National+Historic+Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mawa/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Year-round",accessibilityInfo:"First floor is accessible; upper floors may have limitations.",travelTips:["Check tour times on the website","Park in nearby garages","Combine with other Richmond historic sites"],nearbyAttractions:["Virginia State Capitol","Black History Museum of Virginia"],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"manassas-national-battlefield-park",name:"Manassas National Battlefield Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85AA4D-1DD8-B71B-0B4C0BA37FFF3157.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85AA4D-1DD8-B71B-0B4C0BA37FFF3157.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85AB57-1DD8-B71B-0BA5BF69AE90BFA0.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C85ADDA-1DD8-B71B-0B6455162B9B5E4B.jpg"],shortDescription:"Site of two major Civil War battles where the fields of conflict now serve as a memorial to the fallen.",fullDescription:"Manassas was the site of the first and second major battles of the American Civil War, often referred to as Bull Run. The rolling fields and woods once filled with the smoke of war are now preserved as a place to understand the human cost of the conflict and the tactical decisions that shaped the early war years.",thingsToDo:["Hiking battlefield trails","Guided battlefield tours","Museum exhibit viewing","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Henry Hill Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A loop around the site of the first battle's primary engagement."},{name:"Stone Bridge Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"Explores the area around the famous stone bridge landmark."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Battlefield Tour Road",length:"12 miles",description:"Auto tour with stops at key battle markers."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Henry Hill Visitor Center",info:"Orientation center with film and exhibits.",phone:"(703) 361-1339",hours:"8:30 AM - 5:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Civil War infantry demonstrations","Battlefield guided walks"],campingInfo:"No camping available.",wildlife:["Red fox","White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Blue heron"],bestViewpoints:["Henry Hill"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park is open daily from dawn until dusk.",address:"6511 Sudley Rd, Manassas, VA 20109",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Manassas+National+Battlefield+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mana/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and autumn",accessibilityInfo:"Visitor center is fully accessible; trails are mostly gravel.",travelTips:["Carry water in summer","Use the map for the auto tour","Stay on marked trails"],nearbyAttractions:["Old Town Manassas","Prince William Forest Park"],designation:"National Battlefield Park"},{id:"richmond-national-battlefield-park",name:"Richmond National Battlefield Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/93B9809F-9451-4BF3-655139E821CF9EAD.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/93B9809F-9451-4BF3-655139E821CF9EAD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/D024856B-F8B9-A0BC-0BEFC5493CA5E3DD.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/9408CD8B-C04C-ED9A-D2FA76ACC396CE38.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/CF298A1F-D9B6-66CF-D2FB7953724156D4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/817DB1C4-F8F3-DA1A-FAC43EC136019B12.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the sites of the Civil War battles fought to defend the Confederate capital of Richmond.",fullDescription:"Richmond served as the capital of the Confederacy, making it the primary objective of Union forces for four years. The park is composed of 13 separate units scattered throughout the Richmond area, detailing the intense struggle for the city during the Peninsula Campaign and the Seven Days Battles.",thingsToDo:["Visiting the Chimborazo Medical Museum","Hiking fortifications","Battlefield tours","Historical site research"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Drewry's Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Short walk to historic river fortifications overlooking the James River."},{name:"Cold Harbor Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"Explores the site of the brutal 1864 battle."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Chimborazo Medical Museum",info:"Visitor center and history of Civil War medical practices.",phone:"804-226-1981",hours:"9:00 AM - 4:30 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Medical history talk","Battlefield strategy walk"],campingInfo:"No camping allowed.",wildlife:["Songbirds","White-tailed deer","Gray squirrel"],bestViewpoints:["Drewry's Bluff over the river"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Trails and Battlefield Park Grounds: park outdoor spaces, trails, and battlefields are open and accessible daily, sunrise to sunset.",address:"3215 E Broad St, Richmond, VA 23223",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Richmond+National+Battlefield+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/rich/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring",accessibilityInfo:"Most sites are outdoor walking areas; visitor center is accessible.",travelTips:["The park units are spread out; use a GPS","Check the website to prioritize sites","Expect traffic in the Richmond area"],nearbyAttractions:["Virginia Museum of Fine Arts","Maymont"],designation:"National Battlefield Park"},{id:"petersburg-national-battlefield",name:"Petersburg National Battlefield",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/309783F2-9C50-ACAE-8AA9EC87229E6E7F.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/309783F2-9C50-ACAE-8AA9EC87229E6E7F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/68E83D96-A535-0547-E5569986CAD7AD5E.jpg"],shortDescription:"Site of the longest military operation of the Civil War, a grueling 9-month siege that led to the end of the conflict.",fullDescription:"The Siege of Petersburg fundamentally changed the course of the Civil War. It involved a complex network of trenches, artillery, and mines stretching miles around the city. The park preserves these unique earthworks and provides a sobering look at trench warfare.",thingsToDo:["Tour the Crater","Hiking the earthworks trail","Cycling the battlefield tour road","Living history programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eastern Front Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"Loops through original battlefield earthworks and trenches."},{name:"The Crater Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Short interpretive path to the site of the infamous mine explosion."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Battlefield Tour Road",length:"33 miles",description:"A long auto tour covering the major siege lines."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Eastern Front Visitor Center",info:"Main contact station.",phone:"804-732-3531",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["The Crater explosion talk","Siege life presentations"],campingInfo:"No camping available.",wildlife:["Wild turkey","White-tailed deer","Owls"],bestViewpoints:["The Crater viewing platform"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Eastern Front Unit includes our main visitor center and a four mile, 8-stop tour road which includes the Fort Stedman and Crater battlefields. During Spring and Summer, the tour road closes at sunset or 8:00 pm (whichever is earlier).",address:"1539 Hickory Hill Rd, Petersburg, VA 23803",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Petersburg+National+Battlefield",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/pete/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and autumn",accessibilityInfo:"Tour roads are accessible; hiking trails are packed dirt/gravel.",travelTips:["Start at the visitor center for the film","Bring plenty of water for the tour road","Consider a bicycle for the tour road"],nearbyAttractions:["Old Towne Petersburg","Pamplin Historical Park"],designation:"National Battlefield"},{id:"arlington-house-the-robert-e-lee-memorial",name:"Arlington House, The Robert E. Lee Memorial",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/1E8E848E-FB49-785E-92A1CF5486BE6539.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/1E8E848E-FB49-785E-92A1CF5486BE6539.jpg"],shortDescription:"The historic home of Robert E. Lee and his family, located within Arlington National Cemetery.",fullDescription:"Arlington House is a powerful site that tells the stories of the Lee family, the enslaved people who lived and worked on the estate, and the transition of the property into a national cemetery during the Civil War. It remains a somber site of reflection and history.",thingsToDo:["Touring the historic mansion","Viewing the slave quarters","Walking the estate grounds","Visiting the memorial"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Arlington House Welcome Center",info:"Entrance to the house and grounds.",phone:"703-235-1530",hours:"9:00 AM - 4:30 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Estate history talk","Enslaved workers' legacy program"],campingInfo:"No camping available.",wildlife:["Squirrels","Urban birds"],bestViewpoints:["View of the National Mall from the front lawn"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Arlington House and grounds, the museum, and the Enslaved People's Quarters-North and the Enslaved People's Quarters-South are open daily from 9:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.",address:"Arlington National Cemetery, Arlington, VA 22211",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Arlington+House+Robert+E+Lee+Memorial",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/arho/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for garden blooms",accessibilityInfo:"Accessible via shuttle from the visitor center.",travelTips:["Allow extra time for security screening","Wear comfortable walking shoes","Respect the sanctity of the cemetery"],nearbyAttractions:["Tomb of the Unknown Soldier","Pentagon Memorial"],designation:"National Memorial"},{id:"wolf-trap-national-park-for-the-performing-arts",name:"Wolf Trap National Park for the Performing Arts",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/76f1e1f9b9a456d3.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C806D30-1DD8-B71B-0B1348005ED7DA7D.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C806EB0-1DD8-B71B-0B61464724F6810A.jpg"],shortDescription:"The only national park dedicated to presenting the performing arts, featuring an iconic outdoor amphitheater.",fullDescription:"Nestled in Vienna, Virginia, Wolf Trap is a unique partnership between the National Park Service and the Wolf Trap Foundation. It provides a natural setting for world-class musical and theatrical performances, blending cultural enrichment with woodland park trails.",thingsToDo:["Attending live performances","Hiking park trails","Picnicking before shows","Exploring nature exhibits"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wolf Trap Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"Scenic loop through the park’s wooded areas."},{name:"Meadow Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.8 miles",description:"Flat trail through open meadow sections."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Wolf Trap Filene Center",info:"Main box office and info center.",phone:"(703) 255-1800",hours:"Box office hours vary"},rangerPrograms:["Nature walks before performances"],campingInfo:"No camping permitted.",wildlife:["Deer","Fox","Songbirds","Chipmunks"],bestViewpoints:["Terrace overlook of the meadow"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Wolf Trap National Park for the Performing Arts is open to the public from sunrise to dusk every day. During the performance season, the park is closed to public visitation which is not performance related during park festivals and Filene C",address:"1551 Trap Rd, Vienna, VA 22182",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wolf+Trap+National+Park+for+the+Performing+Arts",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/wotr/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer performance season",accessibilityInfo:"Fully accessible venue and parking.",travelTips:["Arrive early for picnic areas","Check the performance schedule","Use public transport if possible"],nearbyAttractions:["Tysons Corner","Great Falls Park"],designation:"National Park for the Performing Arts"},{id:"prince-william-forest-park",name:"Prince William Forest Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C296690-966E-C826-4C3A165926F3FF3C.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2BF7C2B0-B235-05A1-FBB42AA6D4FF2F12.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2CC4CB95-9AAB-3605-2B4310626DFC55C3.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2CF207B2-E547-BB54-2448E779D1A531A0.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C6E9306-A162-3470-716D9C443F9427D1.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2C296690-966E-C826-4C3A165926F3FF3C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C82F519-1DD8-B71B-0B60F81DB269AB7E.jpg"],shortDescription:"The largest protected natural area in the Washington, D.C. metropolitan region, offering expansive hiking and camping.",fullDescription:"Prince William Forest Park preserves 15,000 acres of Piedmont forest. Historically a site for Civilian Conservation Corps camps and a training ground for the Office of Strategic Services (OSS) during WWII, it now serves as a quiet refuge for outdoor enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Camping in historic cabins","Hiking miles of trails","Mountain biking","Fishing in Quantico Creek"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Birch Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.3 miles",description:"Scenic path following Quantico Creek."},{name:"South Valley Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"10.0 miles",description:"A long, rewarding hike through deep forest."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Scenic Drive",length:"7 miles",description:"Road winding through the heart of the forest."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Visitor Center",info:"Entrance station with maps and exhibits.",phone:"703-221-7181",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["OSS history talks","Nature discovery hikes"],campingInfo:"Full-service campgrounds and historic cabin rentals available.",wildlife:["Wild turkey","White-tailed deer","Box turtle","Barred owl"],bestViewpoints:["Quantico Creek overlooks"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Prince William Forest Park grounds are open daily from sunrise to sunset for recreation use.",address:"18170 Park Entrance Rd, Triangle, VA 22172",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Prince+William+Forest+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/prwi/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for fall colors",accessibilityInfo:"Selected accessible trails near the visitor center.",travelTips:["Book camping well in advance","Bring plenty of water","Check the website for road closures"],nearbyAttractions:["National Museum of the Marine Corps","Quantico National Cemetery"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"harpers-ferry-national-historical-park",name:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8294BB-1DD8-B71B-0BD70066438F7F42.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8294BB-1DD8-B71B-0BD70066438F7F42.jpg"],shortDescription:"A historic town at the confluence of the Potomac and Shenandoah Rivers, famous for John Brown's Raid.",fullDescription:"Located primarily in West Virginia, significant portions of the park extend into Virginia. Harpers Ferry was the site of John Brown's 1859 raid, an event that polarized the nation before the Civil War. It is also an important stop on the Appalachian Trail, sitting amidst dramatic river gorges.",thingsToDo:["Walking the historic lower town","Appalachian Trail hiking","Exploring Civil War museums","River rafting"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Maryland Heights Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"4.5 miles",description:"Provides the iconic cliffside view of the historic town."},{name:"Jefferson Rock Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Short walk to a famous overlook point."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Harpers Ferry Visitor Center",info:"Shuttle pickup point and orientation center.",phone:"304-535-6029",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["John Brown's Raid talk","Civil War infantry demonstrations"],campingInfo:"Nearby commercial camping only; none in the park.",wildlife:["Bald eagle","White-tailed deer","Various raptors","Black bear"],bestViewpoints:["Jefferson Rock","Maryland Heights"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park is open year-round with the exception of Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, and New Year's Day. Park museums/exhibits, the Visitor Center, and restroom facilities are open from 9 a.m.",address:"171 Shoreline Dr, Harpers Ferry, WV 25425",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harpers+Ferry+National+Historical+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hafe/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for the vibrant foliage in the river valley",accessibilityInfo:"Shuttle is accessible; lower town streets are steep cobblestone.",travelTips:["Park at the visitor center and use the free shuttle","Wear sturdy footwear for cobblestone streets","Check the weather, as mountain storms occur"],nearbyAttractions:["Antietam National Battlefield","Shenandoah River"],designation:"National Historical Park"}],washington:[{id:"mount-rainier-national-park",name:"Mount Rainier National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/1FD67031-C1A8-4462-6B815FF24500A576.jpeg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/69651BDC-C0D0-7128-B1DAD5D83D03963C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/1FD67031-C1A8-4462-6B815FF24500A576.jpeg"],shortDescription:"An iconic active stratovolcano rising 14,410 feet above sea level, surrounded by ancient forests and subalpine wildflower meadows.",fullDescription:"Established in 1899, Mount Rainier is the most glaciated peak in the contiguous United States, spawning five major rivers. The park encompasses 236,381 acres of diverse ecosystems, ranging from old-growth temperate rainforests to the harsh, wind-swept summit. Geological activity remains a central feature, as the volcano is considered one of the most dangerous in the world due to its high probability of future eruptions and potential for massive lahars.",thingsToDo:["Mountain climbing","Wildflower viewing","Backpacking","Cross-country skiing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Skyline Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"5.5 miles",description:"A iconic loop offering panoramic views of Mount Rainier, glaciers, and vibrant subalpine meadows."},{name:"Grove of the Patriarchs",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A flat trail leading to an island of ancient, massive Douglas fir, hemlock, and cedar trees."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Paradise Valley Road",length:"19 miles",description:"Winds from the Nisqually Entrance up to the Paradise area, offering incredible views of the mountain and forests."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Henry M. Jackson Memorial Visitor Center",info:"Located in the Paradise area, offering exhibits, maps, and information.",phone:"360-569-2211",hours:"Open daily 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM; closed winter weekdays vary."},rangerPrograms:["Guided snowshoe walks","Junior Ranger activities","Evening campfire programs"],campingInfo:"Several campgrounds including Cougar Rock, Ohanapecosh, and White River; reservations strongly recommended via Recreation.gov.",wildlife:["Black bear","Elk","Mountain goat","Hoary marmot"],bestViewpoints:["Paradise","Sunrise","Reflection Lakes"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"39000 State Route 706 E, Ashford, WA 98304",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mount+Rainier+National+Park+Visitor+Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mora/",bestTimeToVisit:"July to early September for hiking; winter for snow sports.",accessibilityInfo:"Accessible restrooms and parking at visitor centers; some paved trails at Longmire and Paradise are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Always check road status in winter","Bring layers for rapidly changing weather","Stay on marked trails to protect sensitive alpine vegetation"],nearbyAttractions:["Mount St. Helens National Volcanic Monument","Packwood"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"north-cascades-national-park",name:"North Cascades National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A599D-1DD8-B71B-0BBDC12BEC5107B5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A599D-1DD8-B71B-0BBDC12BEC5107B5.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A5CC2-1DD8-B71B-0BC0F615561921EE.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7A5FF6-1DD8-B71B-0BAAFB88D0A3DC08.jpg"],shortDescription:"A vast, rugged wilderness of jagged peaks, blue glaciers, and pristine alpine lakes in Washington's northern mountains.",fullDescription:"Known as the 'American Alps,' North Cascades National Park contains the most expansive glacial system in the lower 48 states. The park's dramatic topography is a result of millions of years of tectonic activity and glacial erosion, creating a landscape of extreme vertical relief. The region is largely roadless, providing a haven for diverse wildlife and quiet solitude.",thingsToDo:["Mountaineering","Backcountry camping","Wildlife photography","Boating on Ross Lake"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cascade Pass Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"7 miles",description:"A series of switchbacks leading to spectacular mountain vistas and high-alpine scenery."},{name:"Maple Pass Loop",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.2 miles",description:"Offers breathtaking views of the surrounding jagged peaks and alpine foliage."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"North Cascades Highway (SR 20)",length:"30 miles",description:"Crosses the heart of the park with access to viewpoints like Diablo Lake Overlook."}],visitorCenter:{name:"North Cascades Visitor Center",info:"Exhibits, films, and trip planning.",phone:"(360) 854-7200",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM during summer season."},rangerPrograms:["Guided wilderness hikes","Environmental education talks"],campingInfo:"Multiple backcountry campsites require permits; front-country camping available at Newhalem and Colonial Creek.",wildlife:["Grizzly bear","Gray wolf","Mountain goat","Pika"],bestViewpoints:["Diablo Lake Overlook","Washington Pass Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"72800 State Route 20, Newhalem, WA 98283",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=North+Cascades+Visitor+Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/noca/",bestTimeToVisit:"July through September for snow-free trails.",accessibilityInfo:"Limited; visitor centers are accessible, but most backcountry areas are rugged.",travelTips:["Carry the 10 essentials","Permits required for overnight stay","Be prepared for total self-sufficiency"],nearbyAttractions:["Ross Lake National Recreation Area","Stehekin"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"olympic-national-park",name:"Olympic National Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B20CD-1DD8-B71B-0B9ACC145EFE6B99.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B20CD-1DD8-B71B-0B9ACC145EFE6B99.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B1F36-1DD8-B71B-0B367264CE3B4EE8.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7B1A8C-1DD8-B71B-0B15D930BD8214F5.jpg"],shortDescription:"Encompassing three distinct ecosystems: rugged Pacific coastline, temperate rainforest, and glacier-capped mountain peaks.",fullDescription:"Olympic National Park is a UNESCO World Heritage site known for its ecological diversity. The park protects nearly one million acres on the Olympic Peninsula, ranging from the subalpine meadows of Hurricane Ridge to the lush Hoh Rain Forest and the wild, rocky shoreline of the Pacific Coast.",thingsToDo:["Tide pooling","Rainforest exploration","Mountain hiking","Hot springs soaking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hoh River Trail to Five Mile Island",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"10.6 miles",description:"Meanders through the lush, moss-draped canopy of one of the world's most pristine temperate rainforests."},{name:"Hurricane Hill",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.2 miles",description:"Provides stunning views of the Olympic Mountains and the Strait of Juan de Fuca."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Hurricane Ridge Road",length:"17 miles",description:"Climbs from sea level to 5,242 feet, providing mountain vistas."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Olympic National Park Visitor Center",info:"Located in Port Angeles, serves as the main hub for park information.",phone:"360-565-3130",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["Coast walks","Rainforest nature talks"],campingInfo:"16 campgrounds; reservations for many available via Recreation.gov.",wildlife:["Olympic Marmot","Roosevelt elk","Bald eagle","Black bear"],bestViewpoints:["Hurricane Ridge","Ruby Beach","Kalaloch"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $30; Per Person $15 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"3002 Mount Angeles Road, Port Angeles, WA 98362",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Olympic+National+Park+Visitor+Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/olym/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late June to September for dry weather.",accessibilityInfo:"Select trails and visitor centers are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Check tide tables before visiting coast","Pack rain gear","Respect wild animal safety distances"],nearbyAttractions:["Lake Crescent","Sol Duc Falls"],designation:"National Park"},{id:"klondike-gold-rush-national-historical-park",name:"Klondike Gold Rush National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A661A44B-B5CB-52FC-4A498850C76193B3.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7BBBA0-1DD8-B71B-0BBE49A649E59052.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/A661A44B-B5CB-52FC-4A498850C76193B3.jpg"],shortDescription:"Explores the 1897 Gold Rush era and its historical impact on the Pacific Northwest.",fullDescription:"The Seattle unit of this park is located in the historic Pioneer Square district. It tells the story of the gold seekers who traveled through Seattle on their way to the Klondike region of Alaska and the Yukon. It serves as a gateway to understanding the massive social and economic changes triggered by the stampede for gold.",thingsToDo:["Museum exhibits","Historic walking tours","Interactive gold rush media"],hikingTrails:[],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Klondike Gold Rush Visitor Center",info:"Located in the Cadillac Hotel in Seattle's Pioneer Square.",phone:"206-220-4240",hours:"Wednesday through Sunday 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["History talks","Gold mining demonstration"],campingInfo:"No camping available.",wildlife:["Urban birds"],bestViewpoints:["Historic Pioneer Square"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The park is open Wednesday through Sunday 10 am to 5 pm.",address:"319 2nd Ave S, Seattle, WA 98104",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Klondike+Gold+Rush+National+Historical+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/klse/",bestTimeToVisit:"Year-round.",accessibilityInfo:"Fully accessible facility.",travelTips:["Use public transit as parking is difficult","Visit the nearby Smith Tower","Allow 1-2 hours for the museum"],nearbyAttractions:["Pike Place Market","Seattle Central Library"],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"san-juan-island-national-historical-park",name:"San Juan Island National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81D512-1DD8-B71B-0BB9DE02CB2C84A4.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81D512-1DD8-B71B-0BB9DE02CB2C84A4.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C81D6F9-1DD8-B71B-0B458C357549D644.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorating the 1859 'Pig War' boundary dispute between the United States and Great Britain.",fullDescription:"Located on San Juan Island, this park preserves the sites of the American and English camps used during the tense border standoff over the San Juan Islands. The park offers insight into military life in the 1800s and features stunning coastal scenery.",thingsToDo:["Whale watching","Historical site touring","Bird watching","Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Young Hill Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2 miles",description:"Climbs to a summit with panoramic views over the San Juan Archipelago."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"English Camp Visitor Center",info:"Historical exhibits and park information.",phone:"360-378-2240",hours:"Daily 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM (seasonal)."},rangerPrograms:["Living history demonstrations","Guided nature walks"],campingInfo:"No camping.",wildlife:["Orca whales","Bald eagles","Red fox"],bestViewpoints:["Cattle Point Lighthouse","Young Hill"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"4668 Cattle Point Road, Friday Harbor, WA 98250",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=San+Juan+Island+National+Historical+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sajh/",bestTimeToVisit:"May to September.",accessibilityInfo:"Accessible paths at both American and English camps.",travelTips:["Take the Washington State Ferry","Check whale watching schedules","Bring binoculars"],nearbyAttractions:["Friday Harbor","Lime Kiln Point State Park"],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"fort-vancouver-national-historic-site",name:"Fort Vancouver National Historic Site",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6b68fe0ccd02e650.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A reconstructed Hudson's Bay Company fur trading post and military site from the 1800s.",fullDescription:"Fort Vancouver served as the headquarters for the Hudson's Bay Company's Columbia Department from 1825 to 1849. Later, it became a major U.S. Army post. The site includes a reconstructed fort, an aviation museum at Pearson Air Field, and the Vancouver Land Bridge.",thingsToDo:["Tour the fort","Aviation museum visit","Living history programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Vancouver Land Bridge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Pedestrian crossing over the highway linking the fort to the Columbia River."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Vancouver Visitor Center",info:"Orientation center and museum store.",phone:"360-816-6230",hours:"Tuesday through Saturday 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["Blacksmith demonstrations","Fur trade history talks"],campingInfo:"No camping.",wildlife:["Deer","Raptors"],bestViewpoints:["Columbia River waterfront"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Standard operating hours for Vancouver, Washington facilities are Tuesday through Saturday, 9:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.",address:"612 E Reserve St, Vancouver, WA 98661",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Vancouver+National+Historic+Site",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/fova/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Summer.",accessibilityInfo:"Grounds and reconstructed buildings are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Visit during the summer for artisan demos","Explore Pearson Air Museum","Walk the Land Bridge"],nearbyAttractions:["Waterfront Renaissance Trail","Officers Row"],designation:"National Historic Site"},{id:"lake-chelan-national-recreation-area",name:"Lake Chelan National Recreation Area",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d32c91114893a320.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Gateway to the North Cascades, centered around the spectacular, deep Lake Chelan.",fullDescription:"Lake Chelan is one of the deepest lakes in the U.S. The recreation area serves as the primary gateway to the remote Stehekin valley and the North Cascades National Park. Accessible primarily by boat or hiking, it offers water-based recreation and rugged mountain access.",thingsToDo:["Boating","Hiking","Fishing","Stehekin valley tour"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Stehekin River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"10 miles (sections vary)",description:"Beautiful forest trail along the Stehekin River."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Golden West Visitor Center",info:"Visitor information for the Stehekin area.",phone:"(360) 854-7200",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (seasonal)."},rangerPrograms:["Guided historical walks"],campingInfo:"Multiple sites including Stehekin Landing; boat-in camping.",wildlife:["Black bear","Osprey","Mountain goat"],bestViewpoints:["Stehekin Landing"],entranceFees:"Free",hours:"Year-round",address:"15000 Stehekin Valley Rd, Stehekin, WA 98852",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Chelan+National+Recreation+Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lach/",bestTimeToVisit:"June to September.",accessibilityInfo:"Limited; ferry access is partially accessible.",travelTips:["Book Lady of the Lake ferry in advance","Prepare for no cell service","Stay bear aware"],nearbyAttractions:["North Cascades National Park","Stehekin Pastry Company"],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"lake-roosevelt-national-recreation-area",name:"Lake Roosevelt National Recreation Area",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/68871BB5-A7B3-84F9-501CCC59BFEE3F05.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/68871BB5-A7B3-84F9-501CCC59BFEE3F05.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/68C3D081-C647-EDDE-2C63B92D4FBBC5A7.jpg"],shortDescription:"A 130-mile long lake formed by the Grand Coulee Dam, offering vast recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"Lake Roosevelt covers over 130 miles behind the Grand Coulee Dam. It is a hub for boating, fishing, and camping across diverse landscapes ranging from desert-like conditions to forests. The area is also significant for its cultural connection to regional Indigenous tribes.",thingsToDo:["Boating","Camping","Fishing","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Fort Spokane Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2 miles",description:"Historic tour of the military fort site."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Spokane Visitor Center",info:"Displays regarding regional history.",phone:"(509) 754-7800",hours:"Daily 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM (seasonal)."},rangerPrograms:["Living history","Nature walks"],campingInfo:"Multiple developed campgrounds like Kettle Falls and Seven Bays.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Mule deer","Kokanee salmon"],bestViewpoints:["Grand Coulee Dam area"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"40001 Highway 25 N, Davenport, WA 99122",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Roosevelt+National+Recreation+Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/laro/",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer for water sports.",accessibilityInfo:"Some campgrounds and beaches are accessible.",travelTips:["Water levels vary by season","Wear a life jacket","Check boat launch status"],nearbyAttractions:["Grand Coulee Dam","Spokane"],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"ross-lake-national-recreation-area",name:"Ross Lake National Recreation Area",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c4d9d1b4136b4791.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A reservoir area sandwiched between North Cascades national park units, known for scenic beauty.",fullDescription:"Created by the Skagit River Hydroelectric Project, Ross Lake is a key connector within the North Cascades complex. It is a pristine, blue-water destination popular with boaters, anglers, and backcountry hikers.",thingsToDo:["Kayaking","Fishing","Hiking","Backpacking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"East Bank Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"30 miles (total)",description:"Follows the eastern edge of Ross Lake with stunning water views."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"North Cascades Visitor Center",info:"Serves all three North Cascades units.",phone:"(360) 854-7200",hours:"Summer 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["Wilderness orientation"],campingInfo:"Numerous boat-in campsites and back-country hike-in sites.",wildlife:["Mountain lion","Osprey","Deer"],bestViewpoints:["Diablo Lake Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free",hours:"24/7",address:"72800 State Route 20, Newhalem, WA 98283",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ross+Lake+National+Recreation+Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/rola/",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer.",accessibilityInfo:"Limited; mostly wild terrain.",travelTips:["Permits required for camping","Be prepared for cold water","Pack out all waste"],nearbyAttractions:["North Cascades National Park","Skagit River"],designation:"National Recreation Area"},{id:"ebey-s-landing-national-historical-reserve",name:"Ebey's Landing National Historical Reserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84BF6A-1DD8-B71B-0B478117375417E0.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84BF6A-1DD8-B71B-0B478117375417E0.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C84C278-1DD8-B71B-0B90C49906FF109C.jpg"],shortDescription:"A protected landscape featuring 19th-century farmscapes and coastal bluffs on Whidbey Island.",fullDescription:"This reserve, established to protect a unique rural landscape, includes historic farms, a community town, and panoramic bluff trails. It offers a look into the history of early settlement on Whidbey Island.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the bluff","Bird watching","Historic architecture viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Ebey's Landing Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.6 miles",description:"Stunning loop trail along high sea bluffs and sandy beach."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Coupeville Visitor Center",info:"Information about the reserve.",phone:"(360) 678-6084",hours:"Daily 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["History guided walks"],campingInfo:"No camping within the reserve (nearby State Parks available).",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Harbor seals"],bestViewpoints:["Ebey's Bluff"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"162 Cemetery Road, Coupeville, WA 98239",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ebey's+Landing+National+Historical+Reserve",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/ebla/",bestTimeToVisit:"Year-round.",accessibilityInfo:"Limited; gravel trails.",travelTips:["Check ferry schedules","Bring a windbreaker","Respect private farmland"],nearbyAttractions:["Fort Casey State Park","Coupeville"],designation:"National Historical Reserve"},{id:"manhattan-project-national-historical-park",name:"Manhattan Project National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/42f2a7dfd19b2b39.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/884A2FA9-FC95-36D1-FB9C52521E3FED66.jpg"],shortDescription:"Preserves the sites associated with the development of the first atomic bomb at Hanford.",fullDescription:"The Hanford unit focuses on the B Reactor, the world's first full-scale plutonium production reactor. It tells the complex story of the scientific, social, and geopolitical impacts of the Manhattan Project.",thingsToDo:["B Reactor tours","Historic building site tours"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hanford: Hike the White Bluffs North Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2-4 Hours",description:"On this trail you may explore rolling sand dunes, take in vast vistas dotted with relics of plutonium production during the Manhattan Project and Cold War, and observe a variety of wildlife."},{name:"Hanford: Hike Candy Mountain",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1-2 Hours",description:"The trail up Candy Mountain is a moderate 3.6 mile (5.8 km) roundtrip hike that offers sweeping views of the Tri-Cities region and a walk through time. Signs along the trail explain the impact of the ice age floods on ar"},{name:"Hanford: Hike the White Bluffs South Slope Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"6-8 Hours",description:"This trail provides one of the best views of the old Hanford town site and location of the Hanford construction camp for the Manhattan Project. This trail will reward you with expansive views of the Hanford site and oppo"}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Manhattan Project Visitor Center",info:"Information on tours.",phone:"509-376-1647",hours:"Tuesday through Saturday 9:00 AM - 3:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["Guided B Reactor tours"],campingInfo:"No camping.",wildlife:["Local desert species"],bestViewpoints:["B Reactor site"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"The Hanford Visitor Center is open Monday - Friday 9:00 AM-4:00 PM. Access to the Hanford B Reactor is suspended due to major construction projects.",address:"2000 Logston Blvd, Richland, WA 99354",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Manhattan+Project+National+Historical+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/mapr/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall.",accessibilityInfo:"Accessible facilities for tours.",travelTips:["Tours require advance reservation","Bring photo ID for site entry","Dress for field conditions"],nearbyAttractions:["Hanford Reach National Monument","Richland Riverfront"],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"lewis-and-clark-national-historical-park",name:"Lewis and Clark National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/54612AA3-0734-3D97-137865DFB9918D11.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/54612AA3-0734-3D97-137865DFB9918D11.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/57362028-C232-556B-6335D32C2A3C2B29.jpg"],shortDescription:"Commemorating the Corps of Discovery's arrival at the Pacific Ocean and winter stay at Fort Clatsop.",fullDescription:"This park encompasses sites in Oregon and Washington, honoring the expedition's winter at Fort Clatsop (1805-1806). It interprets the cultural encounter with the Chinookan people and the scientific achievements of the expedition.",thingsToDo:["Fort replica tour","Canoe demonstrations","Nature hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Fort to Sea Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"6.5 miles",description:"Follows the trail once used by the explorers to reach the Pacific."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Clatsop Visitor Center",info:"Extensive museum and replica fort.",phone:"503-861-2471",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["Gun powder demonstrations","History talks"],campingInfo:"No camping at the fort; nearby state parks exist.",wildlife:["Elk","River otters","Salmon"],bestViewpoints:["Netul River"],entranceFees:"Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:`Winter Hours:
