Open 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.`,address:"92343 Fort Clatsop Rd, Astoria, OR 97103",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lewis+and+Clark+National+Historical+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/lewi/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Summer.",accessibilityInfo:"Replica fort is wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Allow 2-3 hours","Visit the salt works site","Check for seasonal events"],nearbyAttractions:["Astoria Column","Maritime Museum"],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"nez-perce-national-historical-park",name:"Nez Perce National Historical Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/31a3c6eb36257487.webp",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87AFBC-1DD8-B71B-0BE33C27113E993C.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C87B657-1DD8-B71B-0B0970B12C65A5F7.jpg"],shortDescription:"Explores the history and culture of the Nimiipuu people and their relationship with the landscape.",fullDescription:"This park spans across four states, commemorating the Nez Perce people. The Washington sites highlight key locations in the 1877 flight of the Nez Perce and traditional village sites. It emphasizes tribal perspectives and survival.",thingsToDo:["Cultural site touring","Museum visits","History learning"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Dayton Site trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Short interpretive walking loop."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Nez Perce National Historical Park Visitor Center",info:"Main interpretive hub.",phone:"208-843-7001",hours:"Daily 8:30 AM - 4:00 PM."},rangerPrograms:["Culture talks"],campingInfo:"No camping.",wildlife:["Bighorn sheep","Elk"],bestViewpoints:["Spalding area"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Summer visitor center operating hours are 8:30 am - 4:00 pm daily. In the winter, the visitor center is open 5-days a week from Tuesday - Saturday from 8:30 am - 4:00 pm.",address:"39063 US-95, Lapwai, ID 83540",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Nez+Perce+National+Historical+Park+Visitor+Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/nepe/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall.",accessibilityInfo:"Visitor centers are fully accessible.",travelTips:["Visit multiple park units","Respect cultural sites","Check the website for specific WA locations"],nearbyAttractions:["Lewiston","Hells Canyon"],designation:"National Historical Park"}],"west virginia":[{id:"new-river-gorge-national-park-and-preserve",name:"New River Gorge National Park and Preserve",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E8DAE-1DD8-B71B-0B9B0DDABB90EA30.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E8DAE-1DD8-B71B-0B9B0DDABB90EA30.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E8F1F-1DD8-B71B-0B03B737CD7AE489.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E91DA-1DD8-B71B-0B508B9CB06C681E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E95AB-1DD8-B71B-0BB9F0FCDF99420B.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C7E96DD-1DD8-B71B-0B515196575EBFE2.jpg"],shortDescription:"Home to one of the oldest rivers in the world, this park features a massive steel arch bridge and rugged canyon terrain perfect for rock climbing and whitewater rafting.",fullDescription:"New River Gorge National Park and Preserve protects over 70,000 acres of the New River, one of the oldest rivers on the planet. The park is characterized by its deep canyon, diverse forests, and the iconic New River Gorge Bridge, which rises 876 feet above the river. Geologically, the area is rich in sandstone cliffs and coal-bearing rock formations that fueled the regional industrial revolution, leaving behind a fascinating cultural history of boom-town coal camps.",thingsToDo:["Whitewater rafting on the New River","Rock climbing on world-class sandstone cliffs","Mountain biking on the Arrowhead Trails","Walking the catwalk under the New River Gorge Bridge"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Long Point Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.2 miles",description:"A forest trek leading to a dramatic rocky outcrop offering an unparalleled view of the New River Gorge Bridge."},{name:"Endless Wall Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.4 miles",description:"Follows the cliff edge through hemlock forests and rhododendron thickets with stunning overlooks of the gorge."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Fayette Station Road",length:"8 miles",description:"A winding, narrow road that takes you down into the heart of the gorge, crossing the river near the historic town of Fayette."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Canyon Rim Visitor Center",info:"Located near the New River Gorge Bridge, offering exhibits and information.",phone:"(304) 465-0508",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM daily"},rangerPrograms:["Guided Bridge Catwalk Tours","Interpretive Coal Mine History Talks"],campingInfo:"The park offers several primitive campgrounds such as Grandview Sandbar, Stone Cliff, and Army Camp, available on a first-come, first-served basis.",wildlife:["Black bears","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Peregrine falcons"],bestViewpoints:["Canyon Rim Overlook","Grandview Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"162 Visitor Center Rd, Lansing, WV 25862",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Canyon+Rim+Visitor+Center+New+River+Gorge",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/neri/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for rhododendron blooms or October for peak fall foliage.",accessibilityInfo:"The Canyon Rim Visitor Center and associated boardwalks are fully ADA accessible.",travelTips:["Check water levels before planning a whitewater rafting trip.","Bring sturdy hiking boots for rocky terrain.","Pack rain gear as weather in the gorge can change rapidly."],nearbyAttractions:["Babcock State Park","Hawks Nest State Park"],designation:"National Park and Preserve"},{id:"harpers-ferry-national-historical-park",name:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8294BB-1DD8-B71B-0BD70066438F7F42.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C8294BB-1DD8-B71B-0BD70066438F7F42.jpg"],shortDescription:"A historic town located at the confluence of the Potomac and Shenandoah Rivers, famous for John Brown's 1859 abolitionist raid.",fullDescription:"Harpers Ferry is a place of profound American history, serving as a key site for industrialization, the Civil War, and the struggle for civil rights. The town was the site of John Brown's raid on the federal arsenal in 1859, a pivotal event leading to the Civil War. Visitors can explore preserved 19th-century buildings, hike through the Water Gap, and trace the steps of diverse historical figures.",thingsToDo:["Exploring Lower Town historic district","Hiking to Jefferson Rock","Visiting the John Brown Museum","Watching blacksmithing demonstrations"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Maryland Heights Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"6.2 miles",description:"A challenging hike that ascends the mountain to provide a panoramic view of the historic town and river confluence."},{name:"Loudoun Heights Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.5 miles",description:"Rugged forest trail that offers quiet solitude and overlooks of the Shenandoah River."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park Visitor Center",info:"Shuttle access to historic town and main museum.",phone:"304-535-6029",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM daily"},rangerPrograms:["Living History Demonstrations","John Brown's Raid Guided Walk"],campingInfo:"No camping is permitted within the park boundaries; private campgrounds are available in the surrounding Harpers Ferry area.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red foxes","Great blue herons","Osprey"],bestViewpoints:["Jefferson Rock","The Point"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $20; Per Person $10 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Harpers Ferry National Historical Park is open year-round with the exception of Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Day, and New Year's Day. Park museums/exhibits, the Visitor Center, and restroom facilities are open from 9 a.m.",address:"171 Shoreline Drive, Harpers Ferry, WV 25425",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harpers+Ferry+National+Historical+Park+Visitor+Center",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/hafe/",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for mild temperatures and historical reenactment events.",accessibilityInfo:"Shuttle buses are wheelchair accessible; some historic cobblestone streets in Lower Town may be challenging.",travelTips:["Use the park shuttle to reach the historic town.","Wear comfortable walking shoes.","Bring water as there are limited facilities on the mountain trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Appalachian Trail Conservancy Headquarters","Bolivar Heights Battlefield"],designation:"National Historical Park"},{id:"bluestone-national-scenic-river",name:"Bluestone National Scenic River",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C800E49-1DD8-B71B-0BD0956F0EFE00F0.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C800E49-1DD8-B71B-0BD0956F0EFE00F0.jpg"],shortDescription:"A protected, rugged river corridor offering remote fishing, hiking, and primitive scenic beauty.",fullDescription:"The Bluestone National Scenic River flows through a deep, rugged gorge in southern West Virginia. This park unit preserves the natural, cultural, and historic integrity of the river, which remains largely undeveloped. The area is a sanctuary for flora and fauna and serves as a quiet retreat for those seeking wilderness-style experiences, accessible primarily by trail or river craft.",thingsToDo:["Fly fishing for smallmouth bass","Canoeing and kayaking in Class I and II waters","Hiking along the Bluestone Turnpike Trail","Wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bluestone Turnpike Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"10.5 miles",description:"An old historic roadbed that follows the river, perfect for long-distance hiking or trail running."},{name:"Little Bluestone Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"A trail that winds through historic farmsteads and connects to the river corridor."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Sandstone Visitor Center",info:"Serves as the information hub for the New River Gorge and Bluestone area.",phone:"(304) 465-0508",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM daily"},rangerPrograms:["River Ecology Field Studies","Historic Cabin Cultural Programs"],campingInfo:"Primitive backcountry camping is allowed along the river by permit only; there are no developed campgrounds.",wildlife:["River otters","Beavers","Great horned owls","Timber rattlesnakes"],bestViewpoints:["Pipestem State Park overlook","Bluestone Riverbank"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"259 Turner Rd, Sandstone, WV 25985",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sandstone+Visitor+Center+Bluestone",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/blue/",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer for river recreation or autumn for migration views.",accessibilityInfo:"Limited; most of the park is rugged terrain intended for experienced hikers and boaters.",travelTips:["Ensure you have a map, as cell service is non-existent in the canyon.","Practice Leave No Trace principles.","Wear life jackets when on the water."],nearbyAttractions:["Pipestem Resort State Park","Bluestone State Park"],designation:"National Scenic River"},{id:"gauley-river-national-recreation-area",name:"Gauley River National Recreation Area",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/55b94e559a088859.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Famous for high-adrenaline whitewater rafting, this park features a wild, free-flowing river with intense rapids.",fullDescription:"The Gauley River is world-renowned for its intense whitewater, challenging rafters and kayakers with over 100 rapids in a 25-mile stretch. When the Summersville Dam releases water in the fall, this river provides some of the most technical and exhilarating rafting in North America. Beyond the water, the recreation area preserves a deep, scenic canyon and lush, temperate rainforest environments.",thingsToDo:["World-class whitewater rafting","Fishing for trout and bass","Rock climbing on the upper gorge","Backcountry camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Woods Ferry Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2 miles",description:"Leads to the river and provides access to historic swimming and fishing spots."},{name:"Confluence Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A short but beautiful walk to the spot where the Gauley and Meadow Rivers meet."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Canyon Rim Visitor Center",info:"Information shared with New River Gorge facilities.",phone:"(304) 465-0508",hours:"9:00 AM - 5:00 PM daily"},rangerPrograms:["Whitewater Safety Education","Gauley River Natural History Lectures"],campingInfo:"Primitive camping is available at the Gauley Tailwaters; reservations required for high-demand season.",wildlife:["Black bears","Coyotes","Wild turkeys","Copperheads"],bestViewpoints:["Summersville Dam Overlook","Swiss Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"162 Visitor Center Rd, Lansing, WV 25862",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Summersville+Dam+Gauley+River",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/gari/",bestTimeToVisit:"September-October during the scheduled 'Gauley Season' dam releases.",accessibilityInfo:"Minimal accessibility; designed for active, mobile visitors.",travelTips:["Only experienced rafters should attempt the Upper Gauley.","Stay clear of dam release warning sirens.","Always wear a helmet and PFD."],nearbyAttractions:["Summersville Lake","Carnifex Ferry Battlefield State Park"],designation:"National Recreation Area"}],wisconsin:[{id:"apostle-islands-national-lakeshore",name:"Apostle Islands National Lakeshore",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2E8C5E69-C194-1A0A-1DE7866FA06F95E1.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2E8C5E69-C194-1A0A-1DE7866FA06F95E1.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2E9C107A-B16A-51C0-56D38A61F9AE0F4F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2F1C950B-C5AF-097D-71693A8260754C0E.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2F266113-B885-CCC5-FE97FEC5F1467F64.jpg"],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Lake Superior, this park encompasses 21 islands and 12 miles of mainland coast known for historic lighthouses and sea caves.",fullDescription:"Apostle Islands National Lakeshore protects a unique landscape of red sandstone sea caves, old-growth forests, and the largest collection of historic lighthouses in the National Park System. Formed by glacial activity, these islands represent a fragile ecosystem where Lake Superior's harsh environment meets rich, diverse terrestrial habitats. The islands have served as homes to indigenous Ojibwe people, commercial fishermen, and brownstone quarrymen, leaving behind a complex cultural landscape layered over ancient geological formations.",thingsToDo:["Sea kayaking through the mainland sea caves","Cruising to see historic lighthouses","Camping on wilderness islands","Sailing or motorboating between the archipelago"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lakeshore Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.5 miles (one way)",description:"A scenic path along the mainland cliff tops providing access to the famous sea caves."},{name:"Sand Island Lighthouse Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2 miles (round trip)",description:"A trail leading through a historic forest setting to the 1881 Sand Island Lighthouse."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Bayfield Visitor Center",info:"Located in the historic Old Bayfield County Courthouse.",phone:"(715) 779-3398",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM (seasonal variations)"},rangerPrograms:["Lighthouse Tours","Evening Campfire Programs"],campingInfo:"Camping is available on 18 of the 21 islands and at one mainland site; reservations are required via recreation.gov.",wildlife:["Black bear","Bald eagle","White-tailed deer","River otter"],bestViewpoints:["Myers Beach Overlook","Raspberry Island Lighthouse Tower"],entranceFees:"Check nps.gov for current fees.",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"415 Washington Ave, Bayfield, WI 54814",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=415+Washington+Ave,+Bayfield,+WI+54814",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/apis/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"July through September for warmer water and island boat accessibility.",accessibilityInfo:"Limited; visitor center is ADA accessible, but island trails are generally rugged and not paved.",travelTips:["Check the marine forecast daily as Lake Superior weather changes rapidly.","Bring extra layers as temperatures on the water are significantly cooler than inland.","Pack out all trash as there are no trash facilities on the islands."],nearbyAttractions:["Big Top Chautauqua","Madeline Island","Bayfield Maritime Museum"],designation:"National Lakeshore"},{id:"saint-croix-national-scenic-riverway",name:"Saint Croix National Scenic Riverway",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FDF3ACBD-9525-5BD1-4CEC071C9F2BD6DE.png",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/FDF3ACBD-9525-5BD1-4CEC071C9F2BD6DE.png","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/2E6C3E7F-04F7-827F-7BBC3A193868ECD5.jpg"],shortDescription:"Spanning 252 miles of pristine water, the St. Croix and Namekagon Rivers offer world-class paddling, fishing, and boating experiences.",fullDescription:"The Saint Croix National Scenic Riverway protects two of the cleanest and most scenic rivers in the upper Midwest. Designated as part of the National Wild and Scenic Rivers System, this park provides critical habitat for endangered species and a recreational corridor for millions. The riverway follows the border between Wisconsin and Minnesota, winding through diverse landscapes ranging from deep pine forests and dramatic rock formations to lush wetland floodplains.",thingsToDo:["Canoeing and kayaking the river corridors","Tubing in the lower reaches","Fishing for smallmouth bass and walleye","Wildlife photography along the riverbanks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Sandstone Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"Short trail providing views of the St. Croix River valley."},{name:"Namekagon Fen Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1 mile",description:"Boardwalk trail through a unique peatland wetland habitat."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"River Road Drive",length:"15 miles",description:"Follows the river valley north of St. Croix Falls, offering river views and autumn foliage."}],visitorCenter:{name:"St. Croix River Visitor Center",info:"Located in St. Croix Falls, Wisconsin.",phone:"715-483-2274",hours:"Daily 9:00 AM - 4:30 PM (varies by season)"},rangerPrograms:["Junior Ranger Program","River Safety Clinics"],campingInfo:"Primitive riverside camping is available on a first-come, first-served basis; some islands require permits.",wildlife:["Bald eagle","Trumpeter swan","Blanding's turtle","Black bear"],bestViewpoints:["Lion's Park Overlook","St. Croix Falls Overlook"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"401 N. Hamilton Street, St. Croix Falls, WI 54024",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=401+N.+Hamilton+Street,+St.+Croix+Falls,+WI+54024",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/sacn/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water levels and warm weather.",accessibilityInfo:"Select landings and the visitor center are wheelchair accessible.",travelTips:["Always wear a PFD when on the water.","Check water levels via the NPS website before planning your paddle.","Use bug spray during the peak summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Interstate State Park","Osceola & St. Croix Valley Railway"],designation:"National Scenic Riverway"},{id:"ice-age-national-scenic-trail",name:"Ice Age National Scenic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/E538F76C-CB06-6007-549113D6D1A7E4D5.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/E538F76C-CB06-6007-549113D6D1A7E4D5.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4BFD5658-FDF3-925A-C6567EFBD3EE583F.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4C819772-A9EE-C3A8-AF1DE3A9792A0186.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4DA3812B-CC5C-10BE-B6FA1E31E0945783.jpg","https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/4DCAE51C-C051-C792-DAECDB1F7EA51D75.jpg"],shortDescription:"A 1,200-mile footpath located entirely within Wisconsin, tracing the terminal moraine of the last continental glacier.",fullDescription:"The Ice Age National Scenic Trail is one of the premier long-distance trails in America, specifically designed to highlight the glacial landscape left behind 15,000 years ago. The trail wanders through massive end moraines, glacial lakes, drumlins, and eskers, showcasing how ice shaped the state's geography. Managed in partnership with the Ice Age Trail Alliance, the trail connects various state parks, forests, and public lands across Wisconsin.",thingsToDo:["Thru-hiking or section hiking","Snowshoeing in the winter","Birdwatching in prairie remnants","Geological sightseeing at moraine sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Holy Hill Segment",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5 miles",description:"Features high ridges and panoramic views near the famous Holy Hill basilica."},{name:"Kettle Moraine State Forest Segment",difficulty:"Difficult",length:"10 miles",description:"Rugged terrain through steep glacial hills and deep kettle depressions."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ice Age Trail Alliance Office/Visitor Info",info:"Located in Cross Plains, WI; provides maps and planning assistance.",phone:"(608) 798-8700",hours:"Mon-Fri 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Guided Mammoth Hikes","Trail Building Workshops"],campingInfo:"Camping varies by segment; many sections utilize designated campgrounds in State Parks or private lands.",wildlife:["Sandhill crane","Wild turkey","Red fox","White-tailed deer"],bestViewpoints:["Gibraltar Rock","Holy Hill Observation Tower"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"211 Main St, Cross Plains, WI 53528",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=211+Main+St,+Cross+Plains,+WI+53528",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/iatr/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for fall colors or spring for wildflower displays.",accessibilityInfo:"Accessibility varies; some segments near visitor hubs are surfaced, but much of the trail is rugged.",travelTips:["Use the 'Ice Age Trail Guidebook' for detailed section information.","Stay hydrated as many segments do not have reliable water sources.","Wear blaze orange during Wisconsin's hunting seasons."],nearbyAttractions:["Devil's Lake State Park","Kettle Moraine State Forest"],designation:"National Scenic Trail"},{id:"north-country-national-scenic-trail",name:"North Country National Scenic Trail",image:"https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C834D56-1DD8-B71B-0B74388D9102A08E.jpg",photoGallery:["https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/structured_data/3C834D56-1DD8-B71B-0B74388D9102A08E.jpg"],shortDescription:"The longest National Scenic Trail in the U.S., traversing over 4,800 miles across eight states, including a significant stretch through northern Wisconsin.",fullDescription:"The North Country National Scenic Trail (NCT) is a monumental effort to link the Appalachian Trail to the Lewis and Clark Trail through the heart of the northern United States. In Wisconsin, the trail winds through the Chequamegon-Nicolet National Forest, passing through some of the most remote and pristine landscapes in the state. The trail offers hikers a deep-woods experience characterized by vast wetlands, dense pine and hardwood forests, and glacial landforms.",thingsToDo:["Long-distance backpacking","Backcountry camping","Cross-country skiing","Wildlife tracking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Copper Falls Segment",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4 miles",description:"Winds through beautiful river gorges and hemlock-hardwood forests."},{name:"Chequamegon Forest Segment",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"12 miles",description:"Remote wilderness trail through rolling hills and around small woodland lakes."}],scenicDrives:[],visitorCenter:{name:"NCT Headquarters (Lowell, MI)",info:"National administrative office; for Wisconsin, check local US Forest Service offices.",phone:"651-290-3030",hours:"Mon-Fri 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM"},rangerPrograms:["Trail Maintainer Volunteer Days","NCT Hike 100 Challenge"],campingInfo:"Dispersed camping is allowed in many National Forest sections along the trail; verify local regulations.",wildlife:["Moose","Gray wolf","Black bear","Common loon"],bestViewpoints:["Brownstone Falls Overlook","Morgan Falls"],entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"229 E Main St, Lowell, MI 49331 (Headquarters)",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=229+E+Main+St,+Lowell,+MI+49331",officialWebsite:"https://www.nps.gov/noco/index.htm",bestTimeToVisit:"June through August for warmer temperatures; late September for foliage.",accessibilityInfo:"Minimal; primarily a primitive wilderness trail.",travelTips:["Carry a physical map as cell service is unreliable in the northern forest.","Be bear-aware and use proper food storage techniques.","Check for local logging activity which may impact trail accessibility."],nearbyAttractions:["Copper Falls State Park","Chequamegon-Nicolet National Forest"],designation:"National Scenic Trail"}],wyoming:[{id:"grand-teton-national-park",name:"Grand Teton National Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/00e88a9377223be9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to the jagged Teton Range, this park offers world-class alpine scenery, pristine glacial lakes, and abundant wildlife.",fullDescription:"Grand Teton National Park protects the major peaks of the Teton Range, a young mountain range formed by active faulting. The park encompasses a landscape shaped by glaciers, rivers, and the historic settlement of Jackson Hole, providing a dramatic backdrop for ecological diversity and human history.",thingsToDo:["Wildlife viewing in Moose-Wilson Road","Boating on Jenny Lake","Backcountry backpacking","Scenic photography at Mormon Row"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cascade Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"9.1 miles",description:"An iconic trail starting with a boat ride across Jenny Lake, leading into a U-shaped glacial canyon."},{name:"Taggart Lake Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.8 miles",description:"A relatively flat hike offering spectacular views of the Cathedral Group peaks reflecting in the lake."}],scenicDrives:[{name:"Teton Park Road",length:"20 miles",description:"The main artery of the park running parallel to the Teton Range, offering numerous pullouts and trailheads."}],visitorCenter:{name:"Craig Thomas Discovery and Visitor Center",info:"The primary visitor center featuring exhibits on local culture and geology.",phone:"307-739-3399",hours:"8:00 AM - 5:00 PM (varies by season)"},rangerPrograms:["Guided snowshoe walks","Evening campfire programs"],campingInfo:"Several campgrounds including Jenny Lake, Signal Mountain, and Colter Bay; reservations highly recommended via Recreation.gov.",wildlife:["Moose","Grizzly Bear","Elk","Bison"],bestViewpoints:["Snake River Overlook","Schwabacher Landing"],entranceFees:"Private Vehicle $35; Per Person $20 (per nps.gov)",hours:"Open 24 hours a day (per nps.gov; visitor center hours vary)",address:"1 Teton Park Road, M